/* MOVA · Module Exam: lecturas por etapa y nivel (la primera opción es la correcta) */
window.MOVA_ME_READ={"|Pre-A1":[{"t":"My Cat Pip","p":["This is my cat. Her name is Pip.","Pip is small and orange. She has big green eyes.","Pip likes milk. She likes fish, too.","In the morning, Pip plays with a ball.","In the afternoon, Pip sleeps in the sun.","At night, Pip sleeps on my bed.","I love my cat!"],"q":[["What is the cat’s name?",["Pip","Pam","Pat","Pop"]],["What color is Pip?",["orange","black","white","brown"]],["What color are her eyes?",["green","blue","brown","gray"]],["What does Pip do in the morning?",["She plays with a ball.","She sleeps.","She eats fish.","She sleeps on my bed."]],["Where does Pip sleep at night?",["on my bed","in the sun","in the garden","on the sofa"]]]},{"t":"A Day at the Park","p":["I go to the park with my dad.","The park is big and green.","I see a tall tree. I see three ducks.","The ducks are in the water.","My dad and I play with a red ball.","Then we sit and eat apples.","We are happy at the park."],"q":[["Who goes to the park?",["my dad and I","my mom and I","my sister and I","my grandpa and I"]],["What color is the park?",["green","blue","yellow","gray"]],["How many ducks are there?",["three","two","four","five"]],["What color is the ball?",["red","blue","green","yellow"]],["What do they eat?",["apples","bananas","cookies","sandwiches"]]]}],"|A1":[{"t":"Lila’s Busy Monday","p":["Lila is ten years old. She lives in a small house with her mom, her brother, and a dog called Max.","On Monday, Lila gets up at seven o’clock. She takes a shower and puts on her school uniform. For breakfast, she eats eggs and drinks orange juice.","Lila walks to school with her brother. Her first class is math. She doesn’t like math very much, but she likes her teacher. Her favorite class is art because she loves painting.","After school, Lila does her homework and walks Max in the park. In the evening, she reads a book in bed. She goes to sleep at nine o’clock. Monday is a busy day!"],"q":[["How old is Lila?",["ten","eight","twelve"]],["What time does she get up?",["at seven o’clock","at six o’clock","at eight o’clock"]],["What is her favorite class?",["art","math","music"]],["Who does she walk to school with?",["her brother","her mom","her friend"]],["What does Lila do in the evening?",["She reads a book.","She watches TV.","She plays soccer."]]]},{"t":"The School Garden","p":["Our school has a small garden behind the library. Every Thursday, our class goes to the garden with Mr. Ortiz, our science teacher.","In the garden, there are tomatoes, carrots, and sunflowers. The sunflowers are very tall. They are taller than me! We water the plants and take out the weeds. Weeds are plants we don’t want in the garden.","When the tomatoes are red, we pick them. We wash them and make a big salad for the whole class. It is delicious!","I like the garden because it is quiet, and I can see butterflies and bees. Plants need water, sun, and good soil to grow. They also need people who care about them."],"q":[["When does the class go to the garden?",["every Thursday","every Monday","every Friday"]],["Who is Mr. Ortiz?",["a science teacher","the principal","a gardener"]],["Which plants are very tall?",["the sunflowers","the carrots","the tomatoes"]],["What are weeds?",["plants we don’t want in the garden","small flowers","red vegetables"]],["What do plants need to grow?",["water, sun, and good soil","only water","music and light"]]]}],"|A2":[{"t":"The Lost Backpack","p":["Last Saturday, Diego took the bus to visit his grandmother. He was tired, so he listened to music and looked out of the window. When the bus arrived at his stop, he jumped up and ran to the door.","Ten minutes later, he realized something terrible: his backpack was still on the bus! Inside were his tablet, his favorite jacket, and a birthday card for his grandmother.","Diego felt worried. His grandmother made him a cup of hot chocolate and said, “Don’t panic. Let’s call the bus company.” A woman at the office answered and asked him to describe the backpack. “It’s blue, with a yellow keychain shaped like a star,” he said.","Two hours later, the phone rang. The bus driver found the backpack and took it to the station. Diego and his grandmother went to get it, and everything was inside.","Diego wrote a thank-you note to the driver. Now he always checks his seat before he gets off the bus."],"q":[["Where was Diego going?",["to visit his grandmother","to school","to the park"]],["Why did he leave the backpack on the bus?",["He was in a hurry when the bus arrived.","Someone took it.","He gave it to the driver."]],["What was NOT in the backpack?",["a book","a tablet","a jacket"]],["What shape was the keychain?",["a star","a moon","a heart"]],["What does Diego do now?",["He checks his seat before getting off.","He never takes the bus.","He leaves his backpack at home."]]]},{"t":"A Weekend in the Mountains","p":["Last weekend, my family and I went on a trip to the mountains. We left the city early on Saturday morning. The drive took three hours, and the road had a lot of curves, so my little sister felt a bit sick.","We stayed in a wooden cabin near a river. It was much colder than in the city, so we wore warm sweaters and scarves. In the afternoon, we went hiking. The trail was steep, but the view from the top was amazing. We could see the whole valley and some small villages far away.","At night, my dad made a fire, and we cooked dinner outside. Then we looked at the sky. In the city, I never see so many stars!","On Sunday, it rained all morning, so we stayed inside and played board games. We drove home in the afternoon, tired but happy. I want to go back next summer."],"q":[["How long was the drive?",["three hours","one hour","five hours"]],["Where did the family stay?",["in a wooden cabin","in a hotel","in a tent"]],["Why did they wear sweaters?",["It was cold.","It was raining.","It was a party."]],["What did they do at night?",["They cooked outside and looked at the stars.","They watched a movie.","They went swimming."]],["What did they do on Sunday morning?",["They played board games.","They went hiking.","They drove home."]]]}],"|B1":[{"t":"Why Bees Matter","p":["When most people think of bees, they think of honey, or of being stung. However, bees play a far more important role in our lives than many of us realize.","Bees are pollinators. As they move from flower to flower collecting nectar, tiny grains of pollen stick to their bodies and are carried to other flowers. This process, called pollination, allows plants to produce fruit and seeds. Scientists estimate that a large part of the food we eat depends, at least in part, on pollinators such as bees. Without them, foods like apples, almonds, and blueberries would become rare and expensive.","Unfortunately, bee populations in many parts of the world have been declining. The main causes include the loss of natural habitats, the use of certain pesticides, and climate change, which affects when flowers bloom.","The good news is that ordinary people can help. Planting local flowers, avoiding harmful chemicals in gardens, and leaving small areas of land wild can give bees the food and shelter they need. Even a few pots of flowers on a balcony can make a difference. Protecting bees is not only about saving one insect; it is about protecting the food system we all rely on."],"q":[["What is pollination?",["the process that helps plants produce fruit and seeds","the way bees make honey","a disease that affects flowers"]],["According to the text, what would happen to apples and almonds without bees?",["They would become rare and expensive.","They would grow faster.","They would taste sweeter."]],["Which cause of decline is NOT mentioned?",["too many beekeepers","loss of habitats","certain pesticides"]],["How does climate change affect bees?",["It changes when flowers bloom.","It makes bees bigger.","It makes honey sweeter."]],["How can ordinary people help?",["by planting local flowers and avoiding harmful chemicals","by buying more honey","by keeping bees indoors"]]]},{"t":"Small Steps, Big Changes","p":["At the beginning of the year, Sofía made a long list of resolutions: exercise every day, read twenty books, learn to cook, and stop using her phone before bed. By February, she had given up on all of them.","Frustrated, she started reading about how habits work. She discovered an idea that changed her approach: instead of trying to change everything at once, focus on one tiny action that is almost impossible to fail.","So Sofía chose to read just two pages every night. Two pages felt so easy that she never had an excuse to skip it. Some nights she read only those two pages; other nights she kept going for an hour. After a few weeks, reading had become part of her routine, and she no longer had to force herself.","Encouraged by this success, she added a second small habit: a ten-minute walk after lunch. Later, she started leaving her phone in the kitchen at night, which helped her sleep better and gave her more time to read.","By December, Sofía had read fourteen books. She had not reached every goal on her original list, but she felt prouder of herself than ever before. “I learned that motivation comes and goes,” she says. “Small habits carry you on the days when you don’t feel motivated.”"],"q":[["What happened to Sofía’s resolutions by February?",["She gave up on all of them.","She completed all of them.","She made the list longer."]],["What key idea did she discover?",["Focus on one tiny action that is easy to do.","Set bigger goals.","Exercise for two hours a day."]],["Why did reading two pages work?",["It was so easy that she had no excuse to skip it.","Her teacher made her do it.","The books were very short."]],["What helped her sleep better?",["leaving her phone in the kitchen","walking at night","reading for an hour"]],["What is the main message of the text?",["Small, regular habits can be more powerful than big promises.","Reading is the only important habit.","New Year’s resolutions are useless."]]]}],"kids|A1+":[{"t":"The Red Kite","p":["It is Saturday and it is very windy. Sam and his grandpa go to the hill with a big red kite.","Grandpa holds the kite and Sam runs. The kite goes up, up, up! It is high in the sky, next to the clouds.","Then a strong wind comes. The string breaks and the kite flies away. Sam is very sad.","They walk down the hill and look for the kite. They find it in a tall tree. Grandpa can’t climb the tree, but a girl with a long stick helps them.","Sam says, “Thank you!” Now Sam, Grandpa and the girl fly the kite together."],"q":[["What is the weather like?",["very windy","very hot","rainy","snowy"]],["What color is the kite?",["red","blue","yellow","green"]],["Why does the kite fly away?",["The string breaks.","Sam lets it go.","A bird takes it.","Grandpa cuts it."]],["Where do they find the kite?",["in a tall tree","on a car","in the river","on the roof"]],["Who helps them?",["a girl with a long stick","a firefighter","Sam’s mom","Sam’s teacher"]]]},{"t":"Where Is Mr. Buttons?","p":["Last night, Olivia couldn’t sleep. Her teddy bear, Mr. Buttons, wasn’t on her bed.","“Mom! Mr. Buttons is lost!” she shouted. Mom came to her room and they looked everywhere. They looked under the bed, in the closet and behind the door. He wasn’t there.","Then Olivia remembered something. In the afternoon, she played tea party in the garden with her brother, Leo.","Mom and Olivia took a flashlight and went outside. It was dark and a little cold. Next to the little table, there was Mr. Buttons! He was sitting on a chair with a cup in front of him.","Olivia hugged him and laughed. “Sorry, Mr. Buttons. The party finished a long time ago!” Five minutes later, they were both asleep."],"q":[["Who is Mr. Buttons?",["Olivia’s teddy bear","her brother","her dog","her cat"]],["Where did they look first?",["under the bed","in the garden","in the kitchen","in the living room"]],["What did Olivia play in the afternoon?",["tea party","football","hide and seek","a board game"]],["Where was Mr. Buttons?",["on a chair in the garden","in the closet","behind the door","under the bed"]],["What happened five minutes later?",["They were both asleep.","Leo came home.","It started to rain.","Mom made tea."]]]},{"t":"The Picnic and the Ants","p":["On Sunday, the weather was sunny and warm, so Carla’s family went to the park for a picnic.","Dad carried the big basket and Mom brought a blanket. Inside the basket there were sandwiches, watermelon, juice and some cookies.","They found a nice place under a big tree and sat on the blanket. Carla and her brother played with a frisbee before lunch.","When they came back, Carla saw a long black line on the blanket. “Ants!” she said. The ants were carrying little pieces of cookie to their home.","At first, Carla was angry. Then she watched them carefully. The ants worked together and carried pieces bigger than their bodies! “They are very strong,” she said. In the end, they shared one cookie with the ants."],"q":[["What was the weather like?",["sunny and warm","rainy","cold","windy"]],["Who carried the basket?",["Dad","Mom","Carla","Carla’s brother"]],["What did Carla and her brother play?",["frisbee","football","tag","basketball"]],["What were the ants carrying?",["little pieces of cookie","watermelon","bread","sandwiches"]],["What did Carla learn about ants?",["They are very strong and work together.","They are dangerous.","They like juice.","They are lazy."]]]},{"t":"My First Bike Ride","p":["For my birthday, I got a new blue bike. It was beautiful, but it didn’t have training wheels.","On Saturday morning, my dad and I went to the park. I put on my helmet and sat on the bike. My legs were shaking!","Dad held the back of the seat. “Look ahead, not down,” he said. I started to pedal. One, two, three… I fell! I hurt my knee a little, but I didn’t cry.","I tried again and again. On the fifth try, I pedaled very fast. I looked back and Dad wasn’t holding the seat. I was riding alone!","I rode around the park two times. Dad clapped and shouted, “You did it!” Now I ride my bike every weekend."],"q":[["What did Andrés get for his birthday?",["a new blue bike","a helmet","a skateboard","a red bike"]],["Why were his legs shaking?",["He was nervous.","It was cold.","He was tired.","The helmet was too small."]],["What did Dad say?",["“Look ahead, not down.”","“Go home.”","“Close your eyes.”","“Hold my hand.”"]],["On which try did he ride alone?",["the fifth","the first","the second","the third"]],["How often does he ride now?",["every weekend","every morning","once a year","every day after school"]]]},{"t":"Mia and the Caterpillar","p":["One day in spring, Mia found a little green caterpillar on a leaf in her garden. It was eating and eating.","Mia put the caterpillar in a big jar with some leaves and a stick. Every day she gave it fresh leaves. The caterpillar ate a lot and got bigger.","After two weeks, the caterpillar climbed up the stick and stopped moving. It made a hard green case around its body. “Is it sleeping?” Mia asked her mom. “It’s changing,” Mom said. “Wait and see.”","Mia waited for ten days. One morning, the case opened and something came out. It had wet wings, orange and black.","It was a butterfly! Mia opened the jar in the garden. The butterfly opened its wings and flew away into the blue sky. Mia waved goodbye."],"q":[["Where did Mia find the caterpillar?",["on a leaf in her garden","at school","in the kitchen","on a flower in the park"]],["What did Mia give it every day?",["fresh leaves","milk","flowers","water"]],["What did the caterpillar make?",["a hard green case","a nest","a hole","a web"]],["What came out of the case?",["a butterfly","a bird","a bee","a ladybug"]],["What did Mia do at the end?",["She let the butterfly go.","She kept it in the jar.","She gave it to her teacher.","She took it to school."]]]},{"t":"The School Play","p":["Last Friday, Pablo’s class did a play for the parents. The play was about animals in the jungle. Pablo was the lion!","He practiced his words every day for three weeks. His costume was yellow and orange, with a big brown mane.","On the day of the play, the room was full of people. Pablo saw his parents and his grandma in the first row. Suddenly, he felt nervous and forgot his words!","He looked at his friend Sara, the parrot. She whispered, “I am the king of the jungle!” Pablo smiled, stood tall and roared, “I AM THE KING OF THE JUNGLE!”","Everybody laughed and clapped. After the play, Grandma gave him a big hug. “You were the best lion in the world,” she said."],"q":[["Who watched the play?",["the parents","other schools","the teachers only","children from another class"]],["How long did Pablo practice?",["three weeks","one day","two months","one week"]],["Why did Pablo forget his words?",["He felt nervous.","He was sick.","He didn’t practice.","His costume was too big."]],["Who helped him?",["Sara, the parrot","his grandma","the teacher","his dad"]],["What did Grandma say?",["“You were the best lion in the world.”","“Practice more.”","“Let’s go home.”","“You forgot your words.”"]]]}],"kids|A2":[{"t":"Where Is Peanut?","p":["Our class has a pet hamster. His name is Peanut. He is small and brown, and he loves sunflower seeds.","On Monday morning, our teacher, Miss Rosa, looked at the cage. The door was open and Peanut wasn’t there! Everybody was worried.","We looked under the desks, behind the books and inside the toy box. We didn’t find him. Then Diego said, “Listen! What is that noise?”","The noise came from Ana’s backpack. She opened it slowly. Peanut was inside, eating her cookies!","Everybody laughed. Miss Rosa closed the cage very carefully, and Ana shared her last cookie with Peanut."],"q":[["What kind of animal is Peanut?",["a hamster","a rabbit","a mouse","a guinea pig"]],["What was the problem on Monday?",["The cage door was open and Peanut wasn’t there.","Peanut was sick.","Miss Rosa was late.","The classroom was very cold."]],["Who heard a noise?",["Diego","Ana","Miss Rosa","the principal"]],["Where was Peanut?",["in Ana’s backpack","under a desk","in the toy box","behind the books"]],["What was Peanut eating?",["cookies","sunflower seeds","an apple","a carrot"]]]},{"t":"The Treasure Map","p":["During the summer holidays, Rosa and her cousin Iván stayed at their grandpa’s house in the country. One rainy afternoon, they were exploring the attic when they found an old wooden box.","Inside the box there was a piece of yellow paper. It was a map! It showed the house, a big tree, a well and a red X next to some rocks.","The next morning, the sun was shining, so they followed the map. They walked twenty steps from the big tree to the old well. Then they turned left and walked to the rocks.","Iván dug with a small shovel. After a few minutes, he hit something hard. It was a metal can! Inside there were marbles, a toy car, a photo and a note.","The note said: “This is my treasure. I hope you enjoy it. — Pedro, 1972.” Pedro was their grandpa! That night, Grandpa told them stories about when he was a boy."],"q":[["Where did they find the box?",["in the attic","in the garden","under the bed","in the kitchen"]],["What was the X next to?",["some rocks","the well","the house","the big tree"]],["What did Iván use to dig?",["a small shovel","his hands","a stick","a spoon"]],["What was inside the metal can?",["marbles, a toy car, a photo and a note","gold coins","old books","toys and candy"]],["Who was Pedro?",["their grandpa","a pirate","a neighbor","their uncle"]]]},{"t":"A Night Under the Stars","p":["Sofía was very excited. It was her first time camping! Her family drove to a forest near the mountains. They put up a green tent next to a small lake.","In the evening, Dad made a campfire and they cooked sausages and marshmallows. The marshmallows were sticky and sweet. Then they turned off their flashlights and looked up.","The sky was full of stars. In the city, Sofía could only see a few stars, but here there were thousands! Mom showed her a group of stars that looked like a big spoon.","Later, inside the tent, Sofía heard strange noises: “Hoo, hoo!” She was a little scared. “What is that?” she whispered. “It’s an owl,” said Dad. “It’s saying good night.”","Sofía smiled and closed her eyes. The next morning, she woke up early and saw two deer drinking water from the lake. It was the best trip ever."],"q":[["Where did they put the tent?",["next to a small lake","in the city","on the mountain top","next to a river"]],["What did they cook?",["sausages and marshmallows","pizza","fish","chicken and rice"]],["Why could Sofía see more stars?",["She was far from the city.","She had a telescope.","It was her birthday.","The moon was very big."]],["What made the noise “Hoo, hoo”?",["an owl","a deer","her brother","the wind"]],["What did she see in the morning?",["two deer drinking water","a bear","a rainbow","a fox"]]]},{"t":"The Lion Who Couldn’t Roar","p":["Leo was a young lion who lived on the savanna with his family. All the lions had big, loud roars, but when Leo tried to roar, only a tiny “meow” came out.","The other animals laughed at him. “You sound like a kitten!” said the monkeys. Leo felt sad and walked away to the river.","At the river, he heard someone crying. A baby elephant was stuck in the mud. Her mother was far away and couldn’t hear her.","Leo had an idea. He ran to the tall grass and found the mother elephant. He couldn’t roar, but he could run very fast. He showed her the way to the river.","The mother pulled her baby out of the mud with her trunk. “Thank you, Leo,” she said. “You don’t need a big roar to have a big heart.” From that day, nobody laughed at Leo again."],"q":[["What was Leo’s problem?",["He couldn’t roar.","He couldn’t run.","He was very small.","He was afraid of water."]],["Who laughed at him?",["the monkeys","the elephants","his family","the zebras"]],["Why was the baby elephant crying?",["She was stuck in the mud.","She was hungry.","She lost her toy.","She was scared of a lion."]],["How did Leo help?",["He ran and found the mother elephant.","He roared very loud.","He pulled the baby out.","He called the other lions."]],["What is the message of the story?",["You don’t need a big roar to have a big heart.","Lions are better than elephants.","Don’t go to the river.","Big animals are always strong."]]]},{"t":"Grandma’s Secret Recipe","p":["Every December, the whole family goes to Grandma Lupita’s house to make tamales. It is a big, noisy and happy day.","The adults prepare the corn dough and the fillings: red chicken, green pork and sweet tamales with raisins. The children wash the corn husks and put them in water so they get soft.","This year, Daniela asked, “Grandma, why are your tamales the best in the world? What is your secret?” Grandma laughed. “I’ll tell you at the end of the day.”","They worked for hours. They spread the dough on the husks, added the filling and folded them carefully. Then they put more than two hundred tamales in a giant pot to cook.","In the evening, everybody sat at the long table to eat. Grandma looked at all her children and grandchildren and said, “Here is my secret: we make them together.”"],"q":[["When does the family make tamales?",["every December","every Sunday","on birthdays","every summer"]],["What do the children do?",["wash the corn husks","cook the chicken","buy the corn","make the dough"]],["Which tamales have raisins?",["the sweet ones","the green ones","the red ones","the chicken ones"]],["How many tamales did they make?",["more than two hundred","twenty","fifty","one hundred"]],["What is Grandma’s secret?",["They make them together.","a special chili","a lot of sugar","cooking them for a long time"]]]},{"t":"The Night the Lights Went Out","p":["It was a stormy night. The wind was blowing hard and the rain was hitting the windows. Suddenly, all the lights went out. The TV stopped and the house was completely dark.","“I can’t see anything!” cried little Tomás. Mom found some candles in a drawer and Dad brought a flashlight. Soon the living room had a warm, yellow light.","There was no TV, no tablet and no internet. At first, Tomás and his sister Valeria were bored. Then Dad had an idea. He put his hands in front of the flashlight and made shadow animals on the wall: a dog, a bird and a rabbit.","Everybody tried. Valeria made a crocodile and Tomás made a very strange snail. They laughed so much! Then Mom told a story about a ghost who was afraid of children.","Two hours later, the lights came back on. “Oh no!” said Tomás. “Can we turn them off again?”"],"q":[["Why did the lights go out?",["There was a storm.","Dad turned them off.","It was late.","The TV broke."]],["Where did Mom find candles?",["in a drawer","in the car","in the garden","under the bed"]],["What did Dad make on the wall?",["shadow animals","a drawing","a map","a paper airplane"]],["What did Valeria make?",["a crocodile","a snail","a rabbit","a dog"]],["How did Tomás feel when the lights came back?",["He wanted to turn them off again.","He was very happy.","He was scared.","He wanted to watch TV."]]]}],"children|A2+":[{"t":"The Sandcastle Contest","p":["Last summer, Lucía and her little brother Toño went to the beach in Mazatlán with their parents. On the second day, they saw a big sign: “Sandcastle Contest, 11 o’clock. Prize: a giant ice cream!”","They decided to enter. Lucía wanted to build a tall castle with four towers, but Toño wanted to make a sea turtle. They argued for a few minutes, and then they had an idea: a castle with a turtle in front of it.","They worked very hard. Lucía made the towers with a bucket and Toño used shells for the turtle’s shell. When they were almost finished, a big wave came and destroyed one of the towers!","Toño started to cry, but Lucía said, “Don’t worry. We can build it again, faster this time.” They finished just in time.","They didn’t win first prize, but the judges gave them a special prize for teamwork. That afternoon, they shared a giant ice cream with their parents."],"q":[["Where did the family go?",["to the beach in Mazatlán","to the mountains","to a water park","to a lake in Jalisco"]],["What did Toño want to make?",["a sea turtle","a tall castle","a boat","a dragon"]],["What happened when they were almost finished?",["A wave destroyed a tower.","It started to rain.","Toño lost the bucket.","Their bucket broke."]],["How did Lucía help her brother?",["She told him they could build it again.","She bought him an ice cream.","She called their parents.","She built the tower alone."]],["What prize did they get?",["a special prize for teamwork","first prize","no prize","second prize"]],["Why did the judges give them a prize for teamwork?",["They mixed both ideas and worked together.","They built the tallest castle.","They finished first.","Their parents helped them."]],["What does “argued” mean in “They argued for a few minutes”?",["didn’t agree and talked angrily","played together","laughed a lot","made a plan"]]]},{"t":"The Robot Competition","p":["For three months, Regina and her friends Bruno and Paola stayed after school every Wednesday to build a robot. The robot had to pick up small boxes and put them in the right place, like a robot in a warehouse.","They called it “Chispa.” Bruno was good at building, Paola was good at programming and Regina was the team captain. Sometimes they disagreed, but they always found a solution together.","On the day of the competition, the gym was full of teams from different schools. When it was their turn, Chispa moved forward, picked up the first box… and stopped. The battery was dead!","Regina remembered that they had an extra battery in the backpack. Bruno changed it in less than a minute, and Chispa finished the task with only two seconds left.","They came in third place. The judges said their robot wasn’t the fastest, but their team was the calmest under pressure. Regina felt very proud."],"q":[["What did the robot have to do?",["pick up boxes and put them in the right place","play football","clean the classroom","sort colored balls"]],["What was Paola good at?",["programming","building","drawing","music"]],["Why did Chispa stop?",["The battery was dead.","A wheel broke.","It was too slow.","It went the wrong way."]],["Who changed the battery?",["Bruno","Paola","a judge","Regina"]],["What did the judges say about the team?",["They were the calmest under pressure.","They were the fastest.","They were the youngest.","They were the most creative."]],["What helped the team finish the task?",["Regina remembered the extra battery.","The judges gave them more time.","Chispa was the fastest robot.","Another team helped them."]],["What does “under pressure” mean in the text?",["in a difficult, stressful moment","under the table","in a quiet place","with a lot of time"]]]},{"t":"The Lemonade Stand","p":["Mother’s Day was in two weeks, and Carmen and her brother Luis wanted to buy their mom a special present: a plant with purple flowers that cost two hundred pesos. They only had forty pesos in their piggy bank.","“We need to earn money,” said Carmen. “Let’s sell lemonade!” Their dad helped them buy lemons, sugar and paper cups. They made a sign with colored markers: “Fresh Lemonade — 10 pesos.”","On Saturday, they set up a small table in front of the house. The first hour was slow. Only two neighbors stopped. Luis was disappointed. “Maybe nobody likes lemonade,” he said.","Carmen had an idea. They moved the table to the corner near the park, where many people walked their dogs. They also offered free water for the dogs! Soon, there was a line of thirsty people and happy dogs.","By the end of the weekend, they had one hundred and ninety pesos. With the money from the piggy bank, it was enough. On Mother’s Day, their mom cried when she saw the plant. “The best gift,” she said, “is that you worked together.”"],"q":[["Why did they need money?",["to buy a present for their mom","to buy toys","to go to the park","to buy a new bike"]],["How much did the plant cost?",["two hundred pesos","forty pesos","ten pesos","one hundred pesos"]],["Why was Luis disappointed?",["Only two neighbors stopped.","The lemonade was bad.","It started to rain.","Their dad helped them."]],["What was Carmen’s idea?",["Move the table near the park and offer water for dogs.","Make the lemonade cheaper.","Sell cookies too.","Put the lemonade in bigger cups."]],["How much money did they earn in the weekend?",["190 pesos","200 pesos","40 pesos","100 pesos"]],["What does Mom think is the best part of the gift?",["Her children worked together.","The flowers are purple.","It was very expensive.","She can plant it outside."]],["What does “earn” mean in “We need to earn money”?",["get money by working","spend money","save money in a bank","find money on the street"]]]},{"t":"The Monarchs Come Home","p":["Every year, something amazing happens in the forests of Michoacán and the State of Mexico. Millions of orange and black butterflies arrive and cover the trees. They are monarch butterflies.","These butterflies travel from Canada and the United States, sometimes more than four thousand kilometers. That is like crossing Mexico from one side to the other two times! They fly south because the winter in the north is too cold for them.","The monarchs arrive at the beginning of November, around the Day of the Dead. For this reason, some people in Mexico have a special belief: they say the butterflies are the souls of loved ones who come to visit.","During the winter, the butterflies rest in big groups on fir trees. When spring comes, they start the trip north again. They lay eggs on milkweed plants, and their children and grandchildren continue the journey.","Today, monarchs need our help. They need forests and milkweed plants to survive. Planting milkweed in gardens is one way to protect these little travelers."],"q":[["Where do the monarchs go in Mexico?",["the forests of Michoacán and the State of Mexico","the beaches of Cancún","the desert of Sonora","the mountains of Chiapas"]],["Why do they fly south?",["The winter in the north is too cold.","They like Mexican food.","They follow the birds.","They want to see the ocean."]],["When do they arrive?",["at the beginning of November","in July","in March","in September"]],["What do some people believe about them?",["They are the souls of loved ones.","They bring rain.","They are bad luck.","They bring good weather."]],["How can people help monarchs?",["by planting milkweed","by catching them","by cutting trees","by giving them sugar water"]],["Why do the butterflies’ children and grandchildren continue the journey?",["One butterfly doesn’t live long enough to make the whole trip.","The parents get lost.","The parents stay in Mexico.","Only young butterflies can fly."]],["What does “survive” mean in “They need forests and milkweed plants to survive”?",["stay alive","fly fast","lay eggs","change color"]]]},{"t":"The Big Race","p":["Martín had trained for three months for his first swimming competition. Every Tuesday and Thursday after school, he swam lap after lap while his coach timed him.","On the day of the race, the pool was full of people. There were six swimmers in his race, and some of them were older and taller than him. His stomach felt funny.","When the whistle blew, Martín jumped into the water. He swam as fast as he could. At the halfway point, he was in fourth place. He remembered what his coach always said: “Breathe, kick, and don’t look at the others.”","He kicked harder and passed one swimmer, then another. When he touched the wall, he looked up at the board. He was second! He was only half a second behind the winner.","At first, Martín felt a little sad because he didn’t win. But then he saw his time: it was his best time ever. His coach shook his hand. “You didn’t beat everybody,” she said, “but you beat yourself. That’s what matters.”"],"q":[["How long did Martín train?",["three months","two weeks","one year","six months"]],["How did he feel before the race?",["nervous","bored","angry","excited and calm"]],["What was his position at the halfway point?",["fourth","first","sixth","second"]],["In what place did he finish?",["second","first","third","fourth"]],["Why was the coach proud?",["He swam his best time ever.","He won the race.","He was the tallest.","He beat the oldest swimmer."]],["What does “His stomach felt funny” tell us?",["He was nervous.","He was hungry.","He was sick.","He was happy."]],["What does “beat” mean in “you beat yourself”?",["do better than","hit","lose to","stop"]]]},{"t":"Layers of the Rainforest","p":["A rainforest is a forest where it rains almost every day. It is hot, wet and full of life. In fact, more than half of all the kinds of plants and animals in the world live in rainforests. Mexico has rainforests too, in the south, in states like Chiapas.","You can imagine a rainforest like a tall building with different floors. At the bottom is the forest floor. It is dark there, because the leaves above block the sun. Ants, beetles and big cats like the jaguar live here.","The next floor is the understory. Small trees and bushes grow here, and you can find frogs, snakes and colorful insects.","Above that is the canopy, a thick roof of leaves and branches. This is the busiest floor! Monkeys, toucans and parrots live here and eat fruit all day.","At the very top, a few giant trees grow higher than the canopy. Eagles build their nests there and look for food. Every floor is important, and all the animals and plants depend on each other."],"q":[["What is a rainforest like?",["hot, wet and full of life","cold and dry","empty and quiet","cold and snowy"]],["Why is the forest floor dark?",["The leaves above block the sun.","It is always night.","There are no trees.","There is too much rain."]],["Which animal lives on the forest floor?",["the jaguar","the toucan","the eagle","the parrot"]],["Which is the busiest floor?",["the canopy","the forest floor","the understory","the emergent layer"]],["Where do eagles build their nests?",["in the giant trees at the top","on the ground","in the bushes","inside caves"]],["Why does the writer compare a rainforest to a tall building?",["It has different levels, like floors.","It is made of stone.","Many people live there.","It has elevators."]],["What does “depend on” mean in “all the animals and plants depend on each other”?",["need","fight","forget","eat"]]]},{"t":"The Amazing Axolotl","p":["The axolotl is a small animal that lives only in one place in the world: the canals and lakes of Xochimilco, in Mexico City. It looks like a fish, but it is actually an amphibian, like a frog.","Axolotls have a wide head, small eyes and a mouth that looks like a smile. On each side of the head, they have three feathery gills. These gills help them breathe underwater.","Their most incredible ability is regeneration. If an axolotl loses a leg, it can grow a completely new one in a few weeks. It can even repair parts of its heart and brain! Scientists all over the world study axolotls to understand how this works.","Sadly, wild axolotls are in serious danger. The water in Xochimilco is polluted, and other fish eat their eggs. Today there are very few axolotls living in the wild.","Some families in Xochimilco are helping. They create clean areas in the canals called refuges, where axolotls can live safely.","The axolotl is so important to Mexico that it is on the fifty-peso bill."],"q":[["Where do wild axolotls live?",["Xochimilco, Mexico City","all over Mexico","in the ocean","in the rivers of Chiapas"]],["What kind of animal is an axolotl?",["an amphibian","a fish","a reptile","a mammal"]],["What can an axolotl do if it loses a leg?",["grow a new one","walk on three legs forever","change color","sleep for months"]],["Why are wild axolotls in danger?",["polluted water and other fish","too much rain","people keep them as pets","the water is too cold"]],["Where can you see an axolotl in Mexico every day?",["on the fifty-peso bill","on the flag","on stamps only","on the twenty-peso bill"]],["Why do scientists all over the world study axolotls?",["to learn how regeneration works","because they are easy to catch","to make medicine from their eggs","because they live in many countries"]],["What does “polluted” mean in “The water in Xochimilco is polluted”?",["dirty and dangerous","very deep","clean and clear","very cold"]]]},{"t":"The Cooking Contest","p":["When Camila saw the poster at the community center, she couldn’t stop smiling: “Junior Chef Contest. Ages 9–12. Cook a dish that tells a story.”","She knew immediately what she wanted to make: her grandmother’s cabrito-style chicken with red rice. The real cabrito takes too long, so her grandma invented this faster version years ago when she worked in a restaurant in Monterrey.","For three weekends, Camila practiced in her grandma’s kitchen. The first time, the rice was too dry. The second time, the chicken was too salty. Her grandma was patient. “Cooking is like learning to ride a bike,” she said. “You fall, and you try again.”","On the day of the contest, there were twelve young chefs. Camila had one hour. When she presented her dish, she told the judges the story of her grandmother’s restaurant.","She didn’t win first place. A boy won with a beautiful chocolate cake. But one of the judges asked for the recipe, and Camila won the prize for “Best Story.”","Her grandma framed the certificate and put it in the kitchen."],"q":[["What did the contest ask the children to do?",["cook a dish that tells a story","make the biggest cake","cook something new","cook a dish from another country"]],["Why did Grandma invent a faster version?",["The real cabrito takes too long.","She didn’t like cabrito.","Chicken is cheaper.","It was too spicy."]],["What was wrong the first time Camila practiced?",["The rice was too dry.","The chicken was burned.","She forgot the salt.","The chicken was raw."]],["Who won first place?",["a boy with a chocolate cake","Camila","a girl with tacos","a girl with enchiladas"]],["What did Grandma do with the certificate?",["She framed it and put it in the kitchen.","She gave it to the judge.","She lost it.","She sold it."]],["Why did Camila win the prize for “Best Story”?",["She shared the story of her grandmother’s restaurant.","Her dish was the most beautiful.","She cooked the fastest.","She made a chocolate cake."]],["What does “patient” mean in “Her grandma was patient”?",["calm when things take time","angry","very fast","worried"]]]},{"t":"Lost at the Museum","p":["Our class trip to the Museum of Mexican History was the most exciting day of the semester, especially for me. I love history, so I was reading every sign very carefully.","Too carefully. When I looked up from a display about the Mexican Revolution, my group was gone. The room was full of strangers, and I couldn’t see a single blue school uniform.","My first thought was to run and look for them, but then I remembered what my teacher always says: “If you get lost, stay calm and find a person who works there.”","I saw a woman with a museum badge near the entrance. I explained the situation, and she asked for my teacher’s name and my school. She spoke into her radio, and two minutes later, a voice answered.","My class was on the third floor, in the room about the city of Monterrey. The woman walked with me to the elevator, and when the doors opened, there was my teacher, Mr. Garza. He looked very relieved.","“Good job finding help, Pablo,” he said. “But next time, read a little faster.”"],"q":[["Why did Pablo lose his group?",["He was reading very carefully.","He went to the bathroom.","He was playing a game.","He was looking at his phone."]],["What was his first idea?",["to run and look for them","to cry","to call his mom","to hide"]],["What advice did he remember?",["Stay calm and find a person who works there.","Wait at the exit.","Shout your teacher’s name.","Call your parents."]],["Where was his class?",["on the third floor","outside the museum","in the cafeteria","in the gift shop"]],["What did Mr. Garza say at the end?",["Next time, read a little faster.","You can’t come on trips again.","Let’s go home now.","Good job, but you can’t read signs anymore."]],["Why did Mr. Garza look relieved?",["Pablo was safe.","The trip was over.","Pablo found a new display.","The museum was closing."]],["What does “strangers” mean in “The room was full of strangers”?",["people you don’t know","classmates","teachers","museum workers"]]]},{"t":"The New Student","p":["In February, a new student joined my class. His name was Hyun-woo, and his family moved to Monterrey from South Korea because his dad works for a car company. He spoke Korean and some English, but almost no Spanish.","The first week, he sat alone at lunch and didn’t say much. Some kids laughed when he made mistakes. I felt bad, but I didn’t do anything.","Then one day, our teacher asked me to be his partner for a science project. At first, it was difficult. We used English, drawings and a translation app. Sometimes we understood each other completely wrong, and we laughed a lot.","Little by little, I taught him Spanish words, and he taught me Korean ones. He showed me how to write my name in Korean letters, which are called Hangul. I showed him how to make a quesadilla.","By May, Hyun-woo was speaking Spanish with everybody, and he was the best goalkeeper in our class.","Now he is one of my best friends. I learned that sometimes a friend is waiting for you to say the first word."],"q":[["Why did Hyun-woo’s family move to Monterrey?",["His dad works for a car company.","They wanted to learn Spanish.","They were on vacation.","He wanted to study in Mexico."]],["What did the narrator do during the first week?",["Nothing, but he felt bad.","He laughed at Hyun-woo.","He sat with Hyun-woo.","He helped him every day."]],["How did they communicate at first?",["English, drawings and an app","only Spanish","only Korean","with sign language only"]],["What is Hangul?",["the Korean writing system","a Korean food","a Korean game","a Korean dance"]],["What is the main message of the story?",["Sometimes a friend is waiting for you to say the first word.","Science projects are difficult.","It is easy to learn Korean.","Learning Spanish takes years."]],["Why did the narrator feel bad during the first week?",["Hyun-woo was alone and some kids laughed at him.","He didn’t understand his homework.","He lost a soccer game.","His teacher was angry with him."]],["What does “little by little” mean in the text?",["slowly, a bit at a time","very fast","all at once","never"]]]},{"t":"Night Watch for Sea Turtles","p":["Last summer, my family stayed in a small town on the coast of Oaxaca. The town has a turtle camp, where volunteers protect sea turtle eggs. The guide, Don Ramiro, invited us to help for one night.","Every year, thousands of female olive ridley turtles come out of the ocean to lay their eggs in the sand. Each turtle digs a hole with her back flippers and lays about one hundred eggs. Then she covers them and returns to the sea.","The eggs are in danger. Dogs, birds and people can take them. So the volunteers carefully move the eggs to a protected area of the beach, surrounded by a fence.","After about forty-five days, the baby turtles hatch. That night, we watched dozens of tiny turtles climb out of the sand. Don Ramiro told us not to touch them and not to use flashlights, because the light confuses them. They follow the light of the moon on the water.","We stood in a line and watched them walk to the ocean. A wave came and they disappeared.","Only about one in a thousand will become an adult. But that night, I felt that we gave them a chance."],"q":[["Where did the narrator’s family stay?",["on the coast of Oaxaca","in Cancún","in Monterrey","in Veracruz"]],["How many eggs does a turtle lay?",["about one hundred","about ten","about one thousand","about five"]],["Why do volunteers move the eggs?",["to protect them from animals and people","to sell them","to keep them warm at home","to count them"]],["Why can’t people use flashlights?",["The light confuses the turtles.","It wakes up the birds.","It is not allowed in town.","It is too hot."]],["How many baby turtles become adults?",["about one in a thousand","about half","all of them","about one in ten"]],["Why did the narrator feel “we gave them a chance”?",["They protected the eggs and helped the turtles reach the sea.","They took the turtles home.","All the turtles became adults.","They caught many fish."]],["What does “hatch” mean in “the baby turtles hatch”?",["come out of the egg","swim away","lay eggs","go to sleep"]]]}],"children|B1":[{"t":"The Journey of a Tiny Seed","p":["Every sunflower starts as a tiny seed, smaller than your fingernail. Inside the seed, there is a baby plant and enough food to help it start growing.","When the seed is planted in soil and receives water, it begins to wake up. First, a small root grows down into the ground. The root drinks water and holds the plant in place. A few days later, a green shoot pushes up through the soil, looking for the sun.","The young plant needs three important things to grow: water, sunlight and nutrients from the soil. Its leaves use sunlight to make food for the whole plant. This amazing process is called photosynthesis.","After about two months, the sunflower can be taller than an adult! Its big yellow head follows the sun across the sky while it is young.","Finally, the flower makes hundreds of new seeds. Birds eat some of them, and the wind and animals carry others to new places. And the journey begins again."],"q":[["What is inside a seed?",["a baby plant and food","only water","a small flower","sand and rocks"]],["What grows first?",["a root","a flower","the leaves","a big leaf"]],["What do the leaves do?",["They use sunlight to make food.","They drink water from the ground.","They hold the plant in place.","They make new seeds."]],["How long does it take a sunflower to grow very tall?",["about two months","about two days","about two years","about two weeks"]],["Why does the journey “begin again”?",["The flower makes new seeds.","The sun goes down.","The root breaks.","The wind stops."]],["Why does the root grow down first?",["to drink water and hold the plant in place","to look for the sun","to make new seeds","to make food from sunlight"]],["What does “nutrients” mean in “nutrients from the soil”?",["good things a plant needs to grow","small insects","dry leaves","rain drops"]]]},{"t":"The Secret Life of Octopuses","p":["If you could choose a superpower, would you like to change color in less than a second, squeeze through a hole the size of a coin, or taste things with your arms? An octopus can do all three.","Octopuses are remarkable creatures. They have three hearts and blue blood, and most of their neurons are not in their head but in their eight arms. This means each arm can explore, touch and even make simple decisions on its own.","Scientists consider octopuses some of the most intelligent invertebrates on the planet. In aquariums, they have been observed opening jars to reach food, recognizing individual people and even escaping from their tanks at night to visit other tanks.","Their famous camouflage is not only about color. Special cells in their skin can also change texture, so an octopus can look exactly like a rock covered in seaweed. When danger is close, many octopuses release a cloud of dark ink and disappear.","Despite all these talents, most octopuses live only one or two years. Perhaps that is why they are such fast learners: they have very little time to discover the world."],"q":[["How many hearts does an octopus have?",["three","one","eight","two"]],["Where are most of an octopus’s neurons?",["in its arms","in its head","in its hearts","in its eyes"]],["What have octopuses done in aquariums?",["opened jars and escaped from their tanks","learned to speak","built small houses","played games with people"]],["What else can octopus skin change besides color?",["its texture","its temperature","its size","its weight"]],["What does the writer suggest in the last paragraph?",["Octopuses learn fast because their lives are short.","Octopuses live for many years.","Octopuses never learn new things.","Octopuses forget everything quickly."]],["Why can each arm make simple decisions on its own?",["Most of the neurons are in the arms.","Each arm has its own heart.","The arms can see.","The arms are very long."]],["What does “camouflage” mean in the text?",["changing to look like the things around it","a kind of ink","a dangerous fish","a type of rock"]]]},{"t":"Ocean Plastic: What Can Kids Do?","p":["Every year, millions of tons of plastic end up in the ocean. Much of it comes from things we use only once: bottles, bags, straws and food packaging. Because plastic can take hundreds of years to break down, it stays in the water for a very long time.","Sea animals are the first to suffer. Turtles sometimes confuse plastic bags with jellyfish, which they eat, and birds can feed small pieces of plastic to their chicks. Over time, plastic breaks into tiny pieces called microplastics, which are now found almost everywhere, even in the food we eat.","The good news is that young people around the world are taking action. In some schools, students organize beach clean-ups, while others campaign to stop using plastic straws in their cafeterias. Some teenagers have even invented machines that collect floating plastic from rivers before it reaches the sea.","You don’t need to invent a machine to help. You can carry a reusable water bottle, say no to plastic bags and recycle correctly at home. Small actions, repeated by millions of people, can make a big difference."],"q":[["Why does plastic stay in the ocean for so long?",["It can take hundreds of years to break down.","Fish eat it.","The water is too cold.","It is very heavy."]],["Why do turtles eat plastic bags?",["They confuse them with jellyfish.","They like the taste.","They use them to build nests.","They are very hungry."]],["What are microplastics?",["tiny pieces of plastic","small fish","a kind of bottle","big pieces of glass"]],["What have some teenagers invented?",["machines that collect plastic from rivers","a new kind of plastic","robot turtles","boats that make plastic"]],["What is the main message of the last paragraph?",["Small actions by many people can make a big difference.","Only inventors can help the ocean.","Recycling doesn’t work.","Plastic is not a problem."]],["Why is plastic in the ocean also a problem for people?",["Microplastics are found even in the food we eat.","Plastic makes the water hot.","Fish stop swimming.","Beaches become bigger."]],["What does “reusable” mean in “a reusable water bottle”?",["you can use it many times","you throw it away after one use","it is very small","it is made of paper"]]]},{"t":"How Do Penguins Stay Warm?","p":["Antarctica is the coldest place on our planet. In winter, the temperature can drop below minus forty degrees Celsius, and strong winds blow across the ice. Most animals could never survive there, but emperor penguins spend the whole winter on the ice. How do they do it?","First, penguins have a thick layer of fat under their skin. This fat works like a warm blanket that keeps the heat inside their bodies. On top of the fat, they have several layers of short feathers that overlap like the tiles on a roof. These feathers are so tight that the wind and the water can’t get through.","Second, penguins work as a team. When the weather is very bad, thousands of them stand close together in a big group called a huddle. The penguins in the middle stay warm, while the ones on the outside get cold. But they don’t stay in the same place: they slowly move, so every penguin gets a turn in the warm center.","Finally, emperor penguin fathers have a very special job. The mother lays one egg and goes to the ocean to find food. The father keeps the egg on top of his feet, under a warm fold of skin, for about two months, without eating! When the chick hatches, the mother returns, and the family is together again."],"q":[["What protects penguins like a blanket?",["a thick layer of fat","their eggs","the snow","long hair"]],["Why can’t wind and water get through the feathers?",["They are very tight and overlap.","They are very long.","They are wet.","They are full of oil."]],["What is a huddle?",["a big group of penguins standing close together","a penguin’s nest","a kind of fish","a type of ice"]],["How do all penguins get warm in the huddle?",["They slowly move and take turns in the center.","The biggest stay in the middle.","They sleep in the water.","They stay in the water."]],["What does the father do with the egg?",["He keeps it on his feet for about two months.","He buries it in the snow.","He gives it to another penguin.","He leaves it on the ice."]],["Why doesn’t the father eat for about two months?",["He must keep the egg warm and can’t leave it.","There is no food in winter.","He is sick.","He is waiting for the summer."]],["What does “overlap” mean in “feathers that overlap like the tiles on a roof”?",["cover part of each other","fall off","change color","grow very long"]]]},{"t":"Why Does the Moon Change Shape?","p":["Have you noticed that the Moon looks different on different nights? Sometimes it is a big, bright circle. Other times it is only a thin line, like a smile in the sky. And some nights you can’t see it at all. Does the Moon really change its shape?","The answer is no. The Moon is always a round ball of rock. It doesn’t make its own light, like the Sun or a lamp. We can see it because sunlight hits it and bounces back to our eyes, just like light reflects on a mirror.","The Sun always lights up one half of the Moon. At the same time, the Moon travels around the Earth. This trip takes about twenty-nine and a half days, almost one month. As the Moon moves, we see different amounts of its bright half. These different shapes are called phases.","When the Moon is between the Earth and the Sun, the bright side faces away from us, so we see nothing. This is the new moon. Little by little, we see a thin crescent, then a half moon, and finally the full moon, when the whole bright side faces us. Then the shapes get smaller again, and the cycle starts once more.","Long ago, people used the phases of the Moon as a calendar. In fact, the word “month” comes from the word “moon.”"],"q":[["Does the Moon really change its shape?",["No, it is always round.","Yes, every night.","Only in winter.","Yes, only in summer."]],["Why can we see the Moon?",["Sunlight bounces off it.","It makes its own light.","It is made of fire.","It is a big lamp."]],["How long does the Moon take to go around the Earth?",["about twenty-nine and a half days","one day","one year","about one week"]],["What do we see during the new moon?",["nothing","a full circle","a half moon","a crescent"]],["Where does the word “month” come from?",["from the word “moon”","from the word “sun”","from the word “Monday”","from the word “night”"]],["Why is the full moon so bright?",["The whole bright side faces the Earth.","The Moon is closer to the Earth.","The Moon makes more light.","There are no clouds."]],["What does “reflect” mean in “light reflects on a mirror”?",["bounces back","goes inside","disappears","gets hotter"]]]},{"t":"The Volcano in the Cornfield","p":["On the afternoon of February 20, 1943, a farmer called Dionisio Pulido was working in his cornfield near the village of Parícutin, in Michoacán. Suddenly, he heard a loud noise like thunder, and the ground started to shake.","Then Dionisio saw something incredible. A long crack opened in the middle of his field, and smoke and gray ash came out of it. The air smelled like rotten eggs. Frightened, he ran to the village to tell everybody what he had seen.","The next morning, there was a small hill of hot rocks where the crack had been. It was growing very fast! After one week, it was as tall as a five-story building. After one year, it was more than three hundred meters high. A new volcano had been born, and people named it Parícutin.","For nine years, the volcano threw out lava, rocks and ash. The lava slowly covered the villages of Parícutin and San Juan Parangaricutiro. Fortunately, the people had time to leave, and no one died because of the lava.","Today, you can visit the area and see something unforgettable: the tower of the old church of San Juan, still standing in the middle of a sea of black volcanic rock. Scientists from all over the world studied Parícutin, because it was the first time they could watch a volcano from the day it was born."],"q":[["What was Dionisio doing when it started?",["working in his cornfield","sleeping at home","walking to school","fishing in a river"]],["What came out of the crack?",["smoke and gray ash","water","gold","fire and water"]],["How tall was the volcano after one year?",["more than three hundred meters","as tall as a house","five meters","about fifty meters"]],["What happened to the villages?",["The lava covered them.","Nothing happened.","They became bigger.","People built new houses on them."]],["Why did scientists study Parícutin?",["They could watch a volcano from the day it was born.","It was the biggest volcano in the world.","It had gold inside.","It was the hottest volcano."]],["Why is the church tower of San Juan special today?",["It is still standing in the middle of the lava rock.","It is the newest building in Mexico.","It is made of gold.","It is on top of the volcano."]],["What does “frightened” mean in “Frightened, he ran to the village”?",["very scared","very happy","very tired","very hungry"]]]},{"t":"Dinosaur Detectives","p":["Dinosaurs lived on Earth for more than one hundred and fifty million years and disappeared about sixty-six million years ago, long before the first humans. Nobody has ever seen a living dinosaur. So how do we know what they looked like, what they ate and how they moved?","The answer is fossils. When a dinosaur died, sometimes its body was covered by mud or sand. Over millions of years, the bones slowly turned into stone. Scientists who study fossils are called paleontologists, and they work like detectives.","Paleontologists dig very carefully with small tools and brushes. When they find bones, they put them together like a giant puzzle. The shape of the teeth tells them what the dinosaur ate: sharp, pointed teeth were for eating meat, while flat teeth were for eating plants.","Bones are not the only clues. Fossil footprints show how fast a dinosaur walked and if it lived in groups. Fossil eggs and nests show how dinosaurs took care of their babies. Some fossils even show that certain dinosaurs had feathers!","Mexico has important fossils too. In Coahuila, paleontologists have found many dinosaur bones, including a duck-billed dinosaur called Velafrons. Who knows? Maybe one day you will be the detective who discovers a new species."],"q":[["When did dinosaurs disappear?",["about sixty-six million years ago","one thousand years ago","when humans arrived","ten million years ago"]],["What is a paleontologist?",["a scientist who studies fossils","a doctor for animals","a person who builds museums","a person who sells fossils"]],["What did flat teeth tell scientists?",["The dinosaur ate plants.","The dinosaur ate meat.","The dinosaur was a baby.","The dinosaur was very old."]],["What can footprints show?",["how fast dinosaurs walked and if they lived in groups","the color of dinosaurs","what dinosaurs ate","how long dinosaurs lived"]],["Where in Mexico were many dinosaur bones found?",["Coahuila","Yucatán","Mexico City","Oaxaca"]],["Why does the writer say paleontologists work like detectives?",["They use clues to find out about the past.","They catch thieves.","They work for the police.","They study crimes."]],["What does “clues” mean in “Bones are not the only clues”?",["things that help you find an answer","old toys","small animals","stones for building"]]]},{"t":"The Girl Who Counted Stars","p":["When Elena was seven, she spent every clear night on the roof of her house in a small town in Sonora, counting stars. She made drawings of the constellations in a notebook and gave names to the brightest stars.","“What do you want to be when you grow up?” her teacher asked one day. “An astronomer,” Elena answered. Some of her classmates laughed. “That’s impossible,” one boy said. “There are no astronomers in our town.”","That night, Elena told her grandmother what happened. Her grandmother was quiet for a moment. Then she went to her room and came back with an old pair of binoculars. “These were your grandfather’s,” she said. “He used them to watch birds. Now you can use them to watch the sky. Nothing is impossible if you are curious and you work hard.”","With the binoculars, Elena discovered things she had never seen: the craters of the Moon, the four bright moons of Jupiter and a fuzzy spot that her library book said was a galaxy, millions of light-years away.","Elena studied hard for many years. Today, she works at an observatory in the mountains and studies distant stars. On her desk, next to her computer, there is an old pair of binoculars."],"q":[["What did Elena do on clear nights?",["She counted stars on the roof.","She watched TV.","She read comics.","She played soccer in the park."]],["Why did her classmates laugh?",["They thought her dream was impossible.","She said something funny.","She drew a bad picture.","She didn’t know the answer."]],["Whose binoculars were they?",["her grandfather’s","her teacher’s","her father’s","her grandmother’s"]],["What did Elena see with the binoculars?",["the craters of the Moon and moons of Jupiter","birds in the trees","other houses","the planets Mars and Venus"]],["What is Elena’s job today?",["She works at an observatory.","She is a teacher.","She sells binoculars.","She builds telescopes."]],["Why does Elena keep the binoculars on her desk today?",["They remind her of her grandparents and her first dream.","She uses them for work every day.","They are very expensive.","She wants to sell them."]],["What does “curious” mean in “Nothing is impossible if you are curious”?",["wanting to learn and discover things","very tired","afraid of new things","always happy"]]]},{"t":"Life in the Deep, Dark Ocean","p":["Imagine diving down into the ocean. Near the surface, the water is bright, blue and full of fish. But as you go deeper, the light starts to fade. At about one thousand meters, sunlight disappears completely, and everything is as dark as the darkest night. Scientists call this the deep sea, and it covers most of our planet.","Life down there is extremely difficult. The water is close to freezing, food is hard to find, and the pressure is enormous. The weight of all the water above would crush a human instantly. For a long time, people believed that nothing could survive in such a place.","They were wrong. The deep sea is home to some of the strangest creatures on Earth. Many of them produce their own light, an ability called bioluminescence. The anglerfish, for example, has a glowing lure that hangs in front of its huge mouth. Small fish swim toward the light, thinking it is food, and the anglerfish swallows them in a second.","Other animals have adapted in different ways. Some have enormous eyes to catch the tiniest bit of light, while others have no eyes at all and use touch and smell instead. The giant squid, which can grow longer than a school bus, has the largest eyes of any animal.","Surprisingly, we have better maps of the Moon and Mars than of the bottom of our own oceans. Every year, scientists using robots and special submarines discover new species. The deep ocean remains one of the last great mysteries on Earth."],"q":[["At about what depth does sunlight disappear?",["one thousand meters","ten meters","one hundred meters","five thousand meters"]],["Which is NOT a problem in the deep sea?",["too much sunlight","very cold water","enormous pressure","very little food"]],["How does the anglerfish catch food?",["It uses a glowing lure to attract fish.","It chases fish very fast.","It eats plants.","It hides under the sand."]],["What is special about the giant squid?",["It has the largest eyes of any animal.","It has no eyes.","It lives near the surface.","It can fly out of the water."]],["What surprising fact does the text mention?",["We have better maps of the Moon than of the ocean floor.","The deep sea is very small.","No new species have been found.","Scientists have mapped all the oceans."]],["Why did people think nothing could live in the deep sea?",["It is dark, cold and has enormous pressure.","There is no water there.","It is too close to the surface.","Sharks eat everything there."]],["What does “adapted” mean in “Other animals have adapted in different ways”?",["changed to live in a place","moved to another ocean","stopped eating","become bigger"]]]},{"t":"The Endless Journey of a Raindrop","p":["Here is an incredible thought: the water in your glass today may be millions of years old. A dinosaur could have drunk it, it could have floated in a cloud over the Pacific Ocean, or it could have been frozen inside a glacier for centuries. This is possible because the water on Earth is constantly moving in a never-ending circle called the water cycle.","The journey usually begins in the ocean. When the sun heats the surface of the sea, some of the water turns into an invisible gas called water vapor. This process is called evaporation. The warm vapor rises high into the sky, where the air is much colder.","Up there, the vapor cools down and turns back into tiny drops of liquid water. This is called condensation. Millions of these tiny drops join together and form clouds. When the drops become too heavy to float, they fall to the ground as rain, or as snow and hail if it is cold enough. This stage is called precipitation.","Once on the ground, the water continues its trip. Some of it flows into rivers and lakes and eventually returns to the sea. Some soaks into the soil and becomes groundwater, which plants absorb through their roots. Plants then release water vapor through their leaves, and the cycle continues.","Although most of our planet is covered in water, only a very small part of it is fresh water that people can drink. That is why every drop matters, and why taking care of rivers, lakes and forests is so important for the future."],"q":[["Why could your water be millions of years old?",["Water on Earth moves in a never-ending cycle.","Water never moves.","Water is made in factories.","Water turns into ice every night."]],["What is evaporation?",["when water turns into vapor because of heat","when rain falls","when water freezes","when clouds disappear"]],["How do clouds form?",["Tiny drops of water join together.","Smoke goes up into the sky.","The wind makes them.","Rocks melt in the sun."]],["What do plants do with groundwater?",["They absorb it through their roots.","They turn it into clouds directly.","They send it to the sea.","They keep it in their flowers forever."]],["What is the main message of the last paragraph?",["Fresh water is limited, so every drop matters.","There is too much water on Earth.","People should not drink rain.","Rivers are more important than oceans."]],["Why does the writer say a dinosaur could have drunk your water?",["The same water has moved around the Earth for millions of years.","Dinosaurs lived near rivers.","Water is made by animals.","Dinosaurs still exist."]],["What does “soaks into” mean in “Some soaks into the soil”?",["goes slowly inside","flies away","turns into ice","stays on top"]]]},{"t":"How Animals Prepare for Winter","p":["When autumn arrives in many parts of the world, the days become shorter, the temperature drops, and food starts to disappear. For wild animals, winter is the most difficult season of the year. Over millions of years, however, they have developed three clever strategies to survive it: they leave, they sleep, or they adapt.","The first strategy is migration. Many birds, whales and even butterflies travel thousands of kilometers to warmer places where food is easier to find. Every autumn, for instance, millions of birds fly from Canada and the United States to Mexico and Central America, and they return in spring.","The second strategy is hibernation, a kind of very deep sleep. Animals such as some bats and ground squirrels eat a lot during the summer to build up fat. When winter comes, they find a safe place and fall into a state where their body temperature drops and their heart beats much more slowly. This helps them save energy, so they can survive for months without eating. Bears do something similar, although their sleep is lighter, and mothers even give birth during the winter.","The third strategy is adaptation. Some animals stay active and change their bodies or behavior. The arctic fox grows a thick white coat that keeps it warm and helps it hide in the snow. Squirrels, on the other hand, spend the autumn burying nuts in hundreds of hiding places. Scientists have discovered that they are surprisingly good at remembering where most of them are.","Whether they leave, sleep or adapt, all these animals share the same goal: to make it through the cold months and welcome the spring."],"q":[["What are the three strategies?",["leave, sleep or adapt","eat, run or fight","swim, fly or dig","hide, fight or eat"]],["Where do many birds from Canada fly in autumn?",["to Mexico and Central America","to the Arctic","to Europe","to Africa"]],["What happens to an animal’s body during hibernation?",["Its temperature drops and its heart beats slowly.","It grows white fur.","It becomes more active.","It moves faster and eats more."]],["How does the arctic fox adapt?",["It grows a thick white coat.","It flies south.","It sleeps all winter.","It digs a deep hole."]],["What surprised scientists about squirrels?",["They remember where most of their nuts are.","They sleep for six months.","They migrate to Mexico.","They eat all their nuts in one day."]],["Why do ground squirrels eat a lot in summer?",["to build up fat for the long winter sleep","because summer food is better","to grow a white coat","to fly south"]],["What does “bury” mean in “burying nuts in hundreds of hiding places”?",["put under the ground","eat quickly","throw away","count"]]]},{"t":"Chocolate: A Gift from Ancient Mexico","p":["Today you can find chocolate in almost every store in the world: in candy bars, cakes, ice cream and hot drinks. But few people know that the story of chocolate began thousands of years ago in Mesoamerica, the region that includes the south of Mexico and part of Central America.","Chocolate comes from the seeds of the cacao tree, which grows in hot, humid places. The seeds grow inside large fruits called pods. Ancient peoples such as the Olmecs and later the Maya discovered that if they fermented, dried and roasted the seeds, and then ground them into a paste, they could prepare a delicious drink.","However, this drink was very different from the hot chocolate we know. It had no sugar and no milk. It was bitter and often mixed with water, chili, vanilla or flowers, and people poured it from one cup to another to make foam on top. For the Maya and the Aztecs, it was a sacred drink, used in ceremonies and enjoyed by kings and warriors.","Cacao seeds were so valuable that the Aztecs used them as money. With a few seeds, a person could buy a tomato, an avocado or even a small rabbit at the market.","When the Spanish arrived in the sixteenth century, they took cacao to Europe. There, people started adding sugar and cinnamon, and over time chocolate became sweeter. Centuries later, new machines made it possible to produce solid chocolate bars. So the next time you eat a piece of chocolate, remember that you are enjoying an ancient Mexican treasure."],"q":[["Where did the story of chocolate begin?",["in Mesoamerica","in Switzerland","in Spain","in Brazil"]],["Where do the cacao seeds grow?",["inside large fruits called pods","under the ground","on flowers","in the leaves"]],["How was the ancient drink different?",["It had no sugar or milk and was bitter.","It was very sweet.","It was always cold and solid.","It was made with milk and sugar."]],["What did the Aztecs use cacao seeds for?",["as money","as decorations","to make clothes","as medicine"]],["What did Europeans add to chocolate?",["sugar and cinnamon","chili and salt","corn","milk and salt"]],["Why was cacao so valuable for the Aztecs?",["It was sacred and they used it as money.","It was very easy to grow.","It grew everywhere.","They only used it for cooking."]],["What does “sacred” mean in “it was a sacred drink”?",["very special and holy","very cheap","for children only","very cold"]]]},{"t":"Robots That Help People","p":["When we hear the word “robot,” many of us imagine a metal machine with arms and legs that talks like a person, just like in the movies. In reality, most robots look very different, and they are already helping people in amazing ways.","A robot is a machine that can sense what is around it, make simple decisions and move to complete a task. Some robots work in factories, where they build cars or pack products faster and more precisely than humans. Others clean floors in our homes, finding their way around furniture with special sensors.","In hospitals, surgical robots help doctors perform very delicate operations. The doctor controls the robot’s tiny arms from a screen, and the robot can make movements that are smaller and steadier than a human hand. This means patients often recover more quickly.","Robots can also go where it is too dangerous for people. After an earthquake, small robots shaped like snakes can crawl through broken buildings to look for survivors. Other robots explore the bottom of the ocean, or travel across the surface of Mars, sending photos and information back to scientists on Earth.","Even so, robots cannot do everything. They are excellent at repeating tasks and following instructions, but they don’t have feelings, imagination or common sense the way people do. That is why the future probably won’t be about robots replacing humans, but about humans and robots working together. Who knows? Maybe one day you will design a robot that solves a problem nobody has solved before."],"q":[["What can a robot do, according to the text?",["sense, make simple decisions and move","feel emotions","imagine new stories","think like a person"]],["How do surgical robots help doctors?",["They make smaller, steadier movements.","They talk to patients.","They replace the doctor completely.","They choose the medicine."]],["Why are snake robots useful after earthquakes?",["They can crawl through broken buildings.","They can fly.","They can build new houses.","They can swim very fast."]],["What can’t robots do well?",["have feelings and common sense","repeat tasks","follow instructions","repeat the same task"]],["What does the text say about the future?",["Humans and robots will probably work together.","Robots will replace all humans.","Robots will disappear.","Robots will take all jobs."]],["Why might the future be about humans and robots working together?",["Robots are good at tasks but don’t have feelings or common sense.","Robots are better than humans at everything.","Humans can’t follow instructions.","Robots will stop working soon."]],["What does “survivors” mean in “to look for survivors”?",["people who are still alive","broken buildings","doctors","robots"]]]},{"t":"Why Do We Need to Sleep?","p":["We spend about one third of our lives sleeping. For a long time, people thought that sleep was simply a time when the body and the brain switched off. Today, scientists know that the opposite is true: while you sleep, your body is working hard.","First, sleep helps you grow. During deep sleep, your body releases a hormone that helps your bones and muscles develop. That is one reason why children need more sleep than adults, between nine and twelve hours a night.","Sleep is also important for learning. During the day, your brain collects a huge amount of information. At night, it organizes that information and decides what to keep. If you study for a test and then sleep well, you will remember more than if you stay up late.","Your brain also cleans itself at night. A special fluid washes away waste that builds up while you are awake.","So what happens when you don’t sleep enough? You may feel tired, find it hard to concentrate and get angry more easily. You can even get sick more often.","To sleep better, experts recommend going to bed at the same time every night, avoiding screens for an hour before bed and keeping your bedroom dark and quiet."],"q":[["What did people believe about sleep in the past?",["The body and brain switched off.","Sleep helped people grow.","Sleep cleaned the brain.","Sleep was the most important time of the day."]],["How many hours of sleep do children need?",["between nine and twelve","between five and seven","about fifteen","between three and five"]],["What does the brain do with information at night?",["It organizes it and decides what to keep.","It deletes all of it.","It sends it to the muscles.","It saves it for the next week only."]],["Which is NOT mentioned as a result of too little sleep?",["growing taller","difficulty concentrating","getting angry easily","getting sick more often"]],["What do experts recommend before bed?",["avoiding screens for an hour","eating a big meal","doing intense exercise","drinking coffee"]],["Why is it a good idea to sleep well before a test?",["Your brain organizes what you studied.","You will dream about the answers.","Your muscles will grow.","You won’t need to study."]],["What does “concentrate” mean in “find it hard to concentrate”?",["think carefully about one thing","fall asleep","get angry","eat a lot"]]]},{"t":"Inventions by Kids","p":["When we think of inventors, we usually imagine scientists in laboratories. But some very important inventions were created by children.","In 1824, a fifteen-year-old French boy named Louis Braille created a system of raised dots that blind people can read with their fingers. Louis was blind himself, and he wanted to read books like everyone else. Today, his system is used all over the world, and it is called Braille in his honor.","In 1905, an eleven-year-old boy in California named Frank Epperson left a cup of soda with a stick in it outside on a cold night. The next morning, it was frozen. Years later, he sold the idea, and we now know it as the popsicle.","In 1873, Chester Greenwood, a fifteen-year-old from the state of Maine, was tired of having cold ears while ice skating. He asked his grandmother to sew fur onto a wire frame, and he invented earmuffs.","What do these stories have in common? Each inventor noticed a problem in their own life and didn’t wait for someone else to solve it.","Look around you. Is there a problem that annoys you every day? Maybe you are the person who can solve it."],"q":[["Why did Louis Braille create his system?",["He was blind and wanted to read books.","His teacher asked him to.","He wanted to win a prize.","He wanted to sell books."]],["How old was Frank Epperson when he discovered the popsicle?",["eleven","fifteen","twenty","nine"]],["How was the popsicle discovered?",["A drink froze outside on a cold night.","He made it in a laboratory.","His mother invented it.","His friend gave him the idea."]],["Why did Chester Greenwood invent earmuffs?",["His ears were cold while ice skating.","He wanted to sell them.","His grandmother needed them.","His hands were cold."]],["What do the three inventors have in common?",["They solved a problem from their own lives.","They were all from France.","They all became very rich.","They all lived in Maine."]],["What is the writer’s message to readers?",["You can solve problems in your own life too.","Only adults can invent things.","Inventions make you rich.","Stay away from laboratories."]],["What does “annoys” mean in “a problem that annoys you every day”?",["bothers you","makes you happy","helps you","scares you"]]]},{"t":"The Case of the Missing Trophy","p":["On Monday morning, the golden soccer trophy that our school won last year was missing from its glass case in the main hall. The principal was furious, and everybody was talking about it.","Renata, who wants to be a detective, decided to investigate. She noticed three things. First, the glass case wasn’t broken, so someone had opened it with a key. Second, there was a small trail of green sawdust on the floor. Third, only two people had keys to the case: the principal and Mr. Soto, the janitor.","Some students immediately accused Mr. Soto. But Renata wasn’t convinced. Why would he steal something he cleaned every day?","She followed the sawdust trail. It led to the art room, where the art teacher, Ms. Villarreal, was working on a large wooden project for the school anniversary.","Renata knocked on the door. Inside, she found the trophy on a table, next to a wooden copy of it, painted green and gold. Ms. Villarreal looked embarrassed. She had borrowed the key from the principal on Friday to make a giant sculpture of the trophy as a surprise. She forgot to tell anyone.","The principal apologized to Mr. Soto, and Renata got a certificate for “Best Detective Work.”"],"q":[["How did Renata know that someone opened the case with a key?",["The glass wasn’t broken.","She saw the key on the floor.","The janitor told her.","There was a key on the table."]],["Who had keys to the case?",["the principal and the janitor","only the principal","all the teachers","all the students"]],["Why wasn’t Renata convinced that Mr. Soto was guilty?",["It didn’t make sense for him to steal it.","He was on vacation.","She saw him at home.","He had a key."]],["Where did the sawdust trail lead?",["to the art room","to the principal’s office","outside the school","to the gym"]],["Why did Ms. Villarreal have the trophy?",["She was making a giant copy as a surprise.","She wanted to keep it.","She was cleaning it.","She wanted to clean it."]],["Why did the principal apologize to Mr. Soto?",["People had blamed him, but he did nothing wrong.","He lost his key.","He cleaned the case badly.","He found the trophy."]],["What does “accused” mean in “Some students accused Mr. Soto”?",["said he did something bad","thanked him","helped him","asked him a question"]]]},{"t":"The Ancient Ball Game","p":["Long before soccer existed, the peoples of Mesoamerica played a ball game that was much more than a sport. The Maya called it pok-ta-pok, and the Aztecs called it ullamaliztli. Archaeologists believe it started more than 3,000 years ago.","The game was played on a long court shaped like the capital letter I, with tall walls on both sides. In some courts, like the famous one in Chichén Itzá, there were stone rings high on the walls.","The ball was made of solid rubber, and it could weigh up to four kilograms. Players could not touch it with their hands or feet. Instead, they hit it with their hips, knees and elbows. To protect themselves, they wore thick leather belts and pads.","Passing the ball through a stone ring was extremely difficult, so it didn’t happen often. When it did, the game usually ended immediately.","For these civilizations, the game had religious meaning. Some historians think the movement of the ball represented the sun and the moon traveling across the sky.","Amazingly, a version of the game called ulama is still played today in some communities in the state of Sinaloa."],"q":[["What did the Maya call the game?",["pok-ta-pok","ullamaliztli","ulama","soccer"]],["What shape was the court?",["like the letter I","a circle","a square","a triangle"]],["How did players hit the ball?",["with their hips, knees and elbows","with their hands","with a wooden bat","with their heads only"]],["What usually happened when a player passed the ball through the ring?",["The game ended.","The ball broke.","The other team got a point.","The players changed teams."]],["Where is a version of the game played today?",["in Sinaloa","in Chichén Itzá","in Mexico City’s stadiums","in Monterrey"]],["Why did the game usually end when the ball passed through a ring?",["It was extremely difficult and didn’t happen often.","The ring broke.","The ball was lost.","The players were tired."]],["What does “civilizations” mean in “For these civilizations, the game had religious meaning”?",["large, organized groups of people with their own culture","small families","sports teams","temples"]]]},{"t":"Saving for Something Special","p":["Emiliano had wanted a telescope for a long time. He loved looking at the moon and reading about planets. The telescope he wanted cost 3,600 pesos. His parents said they would pay half if he could save the other half on his own.","At first, 1,800 pesos seemed impossible. He usually spent his weekly allowance of 100 pesos on snacks and stickers the same day he received it.","So he made a plan with his older cousin, who studies economics. First, they divided a jar into three sections: “Save,” “Spend” and “Share.” Every week, 70 pesos went to “Save,” 20 to “Spend” and 10 to “Share,” which he would give to an animal shelter.","Second, Emiliano found ways to earn extra money. He washed his neighbor’s car on Sundays and sold lemonade with his sister at a school festival.","Some weeks were hard. Once, all his friends bought a new video game, and he almost used his savings. But he looked at a picture of Saturn on his wall and decided to wait.","After five months, he had 1,800 pesos. The first night with his telescope, he saw Saturn’s rings. They looked even better because he had worked for them."],"q":[["How much money did Emiliano need to save?",["1,800 pesos","3,600 pesos","100 pesos","2,000 pesos"]],["What was his problem at first?",["He spent his allowance immediately.","He didn’t get an allowance.","His parents said no.","He didn’t like telescopes."]],["What were the three sections of the jar?",["Save, Spend and Share","Toys, Food and School","Week, Month and Year","Money, Bank and Gifts"]],["How did he earn extra money?",["washing a car and selling lemonade","selling his video games","working at a shop","asking his friends for money"]],["Why didn’t he buy the video game?",["He looked at a picture of Saturn and decided to wait.","It was too expensive.","His parents didn’t allow it.","He didn’t like video games."]],["Why did the rings of Saturn look “even better”?",["He worked hard to buy the telescope.","The telescope was new.","It was a very clear night.","His cousin helped him."]],["What does “allowance” mean in “his weekly allowance of 100 pesos”?",["money that parents give children regularly","a kind of job","a prize at school","a bank account"]]]}],"children|Pre-A1":[{"t":"My School Bag","p":["Hello! I am Leo. This is my school bag. It is blue.","In my bag, I have two books and three pencils. I have a red pen and a ruler.","I have a green notebook. I have an apple and a bottle of water.","I like my school bag. It is big and it is my favorite!"],"q":[["What color is Leo’s bag?",["blue","red","green","black"]],["How many books does he have?",["two","three","one","four"]],["What color is his pen?",["red","blue","black","green"]],["What food is in the bag?",["an apple","a sandwich","a banana","an orange"]],["How is the bag?",["big","small","old","new"]],["How does Leo feel about his bag?",["He likes it a lot.","He wants a new bag.","He thinks it is too big.","He doesn’t like the color."]]]},{"t":"My Bedroom","p":["Hello! I am Emma. I am nine. This is my bedroom.","My bedroom is small, but I love it. The walls are blue.","There is a bed next to the window. On my bed, there is a big teddy bear.","There is a desk, too. I do my homework at my desk.","I have a lot of books. They are on a shelf.","My cat sleeps on my bed every night!"],"q":[["How old is Emma?",["nine","six","twelve","seven"]],["What color are the walls?",["blue","pink","green","yellow"]],["Where is the bed?",["next to the window","next to the door","under the desk","next to the desk"]],["Where are the books?",["on a shelf","on the bed","in a box","under the bed"]],["Who sleeps on Emma’s bed?",["her cat","her dog","her sister","her brother"]],["Why does Emma love her bedroom?",["It has her favorite things.","It is very big.","It has no windows.","Her cat doesn’t go in."]]]},{"t":"My Best Friend","p":["My name is Tomás. My best friend is Leo.","Leo is ten. He has short black hair and brown eyes.","We are in the same class at school.","Leo is very good at math. I am good at drawing.","After school, we play soccer in the park.","On Saturday, we watch movies and eat popcorn.","Leo is kind and funny. He is a great friend!"],"q":[["Who is Tomás’s best friend?",["Leo","Max","Emma","Tomás"]],["What color is Leo’s hair?",["black","brown","red","blond"]],["What is Leo good at?",["math","drawing","music","soccer"]],["Where do they play soccer?",["in the park","at home","at the beach","at school"]],["What do they eat on Saturday?",["popcorn","pizza","ice cream","tacos"]],["Why is Leo a great friend?",["He is kind and funny.","He is very tall.","He has a lot of toys.","He always wins at soccer."]]]},{"t":"What’s the Weather Like?","p":["Today it is sunny in Monterrey. It is very hot!","I wear a T-shirt and a cap.","In London, it is rainy. People have umbrellas.","In Chicago, it is windy and cold. People wear jackets.","In Canada, it is snowy. Children make a snowman!","What’s the weather like in your city today?"],"q":[["What is the weather like in Monterrey?",["sunny","rainy","snowy","cloudy"]],["What does the child wear?",["a T-shirt and a cap","a coat and a hat","a jacket and boots","a sweater and shorts"]],["What do people have in London?",["umbrellas","snowmen","kites","sunglasses"]],["How is the weather in Chicago?",["windy and cold","hot and sunny","rainy and warm","sunny and cold"]],["What do children make in Canada?",["a snowman","a sandcastle","a cake","a kite"]],["Which city is the hottest?",["Monterrey","London","Chicago","Canada"]]]},{"t":"A Trip to the Zoo","p":["Today my class is at the zoo. It is fun!","Look at the giraffe. It is very tall. It has a long neck.","The elephant is big and gray. It has big ears.","The monkeys are funny. They jump and climb.","The lion is sleeping. Shh!","My favorite animal is the penguin. It is black and white, and it can swim."],"q":[["Where is the class?",["at the zoo","at the park","at the farm","at the school"]],["Which animal has a long neck?",["the giraffe","the lion","the monkey","the elephant"]],["What color is the elephant?",["gray","brown","black","white"]],["What is the lion doing?",["sleeping","eating","running","swimming"]],["What is the writer’s favorite animal?",["the penguin","the elephant","the monkey","the giraffe"]],["Why does the writer say “Shh!”?",["The lion is sleeping.","The monkeys are noisy.","The penguin is swimming.","The class is in the library."]]]},{"t":"My Busy Week","p":["I am Daniel. My week is very busy!","On Monday and Wednesday, I have English class.","On Tuesday, I play basketball.","On Thursday, I go to swimming lessons.","On Friday, I visit my grandma. She makes enchiladas.","On Saturday and Sunday, I rest and play with my brother.","What is your favorite day?"],"q":[["When does Daniel have English class?",["Monday and Wednesday","Tuesday and Thursday","Saturday","Friday and Saturday"]],["What does he do on Tuesday?",["He plays basketball.","He swims.","He visits Grandma.","He goes to English class."]],["When does he go swimming?",["on Thursday","on Friday","on Sunday","on Tuesday"]],["What does Grandma make?",["enchiladas","cookies","soup","tamales"]],["Who does he play with on the weekend?",["his brother","his cousin","his teacher","his sister"]],["Why does Daniel say his week is busy?",["He has something to do almost every day.","He has a lot of homework.","He works on weekends.","He is sick all week."]]]}],"children|A1+":[{"t":"Football After School","p":["Mateo is ten years old. He goes to school in the morning, and in the afternoon he plays football with his team, the Blue Tigers.","The team practices on Tuesdays and Thursdays. The coach is very friendly. She always says, “Pass the ball and work together!”","Today is Saturday and the Blue Tigers have a big match. Mateo is a little nervous. His family is at the stadium: his mom, his dad and his little sister, Camila.","In the second half, Mateo passes the ball to his friend Iker, and Iker scores a goal! The Blue Tigers win two to one.","After the match, the team eats pizza together. Mateo is tired but very happy."],"q":[["When does the team practice?",["on Tuesdays and Thursdays","every day","on Saturdays","on Mondays and Fridays"]],["What does the coach always say?",["Pass the ball and work together!","Run faster!","Don’t be late!","Score more goals!"]],["Who is Camila?",["Mateo’s little sister","the coach","Mateo’s friend","Mateo’s cousin"]],["Who scores the goal?",["Iker","Mateo","the coach","Camila"]],["What do they do after the match?",["They eat pizza together.","They go to school.","They practice again.","They watch a movie."]],["Why is Mateo a little nervous on Saturday?",["It is a big match.","He is sick.","His family is not there.","He doesn’t like football."]],["What does “score” mean in “Iker scores a goal”?",["get a point by putting the ball in the goal","stop the ball","run fast","lose the match"]]]},{"t":"The Surprise Party","p":["Next Friday is Miss Laura’s birthday. She is our teacher, and everybody loves her. So our class has a plan: a surprise party!","It is not easy to keep a secret. On Monday, we make a big card and everyone signs it. We hide it in Marco’s backpack.","On Wednesday, Miss Laura asks, “Why are you all smiling?” Fernanda almost tells her, but Marco says, “We’re just happy, Miss!”","On Friday morning, the principal asks Miss Laura to go to the office. We have only ten minutes. We put balloons on the walls and a chocolate cake on her desk.","When she comes back, we shout, “SURPRISE!” Miss Laura is so surprised that she drops her coffee. Then she laughs and hugs everyone.","It is the best day of the school year."],"q":[["Who is the party for?",["Miss Laura","Marco","the principal","the art teacher"]],["Where do they hide the card?",["in Marco’s backpack","in the teacher’s desk","in the office","in the classroom closet"]],["Who almost tells the secret?",["Fernanda","Marco","the principal","Miss Laura"]],["How much time do they have to prepare the room?",["ten minutes","one hour","all day","five minutes"]],["What does Miss Laura drop?",["her coffee","the cake","her bag","her phone"]],["Why is it hard to keep the secret?",["Everybody is excited and almost tells her.","Miss Laura reads their messages.","The principal tells her.","The card is very big."]],["What does “hide” mean in “We hide it in Marco’s backpack”?",["put something where nobody can see it","throw something away","open something","buy something"]]]},{"t":"Afraid of the Deep End","p":["Regina takes swimming lessons every Tuesday and Thursday. She can swim very well in the shallow end of the pool, where her feet touch the bottom.","But the deep end scares her. The water is dark blue, and it is three meters deep.","One day, her coach, Pedro, says, “Today we’re going to swim in the deep end.” Regina’s heart beats fast.","“Remember,” says Pedro, “the water is the same. You already know how to swim. Breathe slowly and kick your legs.”","Regina closes her eyes for a second. Then she jumps in. She goes under the water, kicks her legs and comes up again. She is floating!","Now the deep end is her favorite part of the pool."],"q":[["When are Regina’s lessons?",["Tuesday and Thursday","Monday and Friday","every weekend","every Wednesday"]],["Why does the deep end scare her?",["It is very deep.","It is very cold.","There are fish.","The water is dirty."]],["Who is Pedro?",["her swimming coach","her brother","a lifeguard","her teacher"]],["What advice does Pedro give?",["Breathe slowly and kick your legs.","Don’t look at the water.","Swim very fast.","Close your eyes and wait."]],["How does Regina feel about the deep end now?",["It is her favorite part.","She still hates it.","She never goes there.","She is still a little afraid."]],["Why does Pedro say “the water is the same”?",["to show her she can swim there too","because the pool is small","because the water is warm","to tell her to stop swimming"]],["What does “float” mean in “She is floating!”?",["stay on top of the water","go to the bottom","swim very fast","jump into the pool"]]]},{"t":"A Weekend at the Ranch","p":["Every year, Santiago spends a long weekend at his grandpa’s ranch near Linares. There is no internet there, and at first, Santiago doesn’t like that.","But the ranch is full of things to do. In the morning, he helps Grandpa feed the chickens and milk the cow. The milk is warm!","In the afternoon, they ride horses by the river. Santiago’s horse is called Canela. She is calm and gentle.","At night, the sky is very dark, and Santiago can see hundreds of stars. Grandpa tells him the names of some of them.","On Sunday, it is time to go home. Santiago is a little sad.","“Can I come back for the summer?” he asks. Grandpa smiles. “Of course!”"],"q":[["Where is Grandpa’s ranch?",["near Linares","in Monterrey","near the beach","near Saltillo"]],["Why doesn’t Santiago like the ranch at first?",["There is no internet.","There are no animals.","It is too cold.","There is no TV."]],["What is the name of his horse?",["Canela","Luna","Paloma","Estrella"]],["What can he see at night?",["hundreds of stars","city lights","a big moon only","airplanes"]],["What does Santiago ask at the end?",["if he can come back in summer","if he can go home early","if he can take the horse home","if he can bring his friends"]],["How does Santiago feel about the ranch at the end?",["He loves it and wants to go back.","He is happy to leave.","He is still angry about the internet.","He is scared of the horses."]],["What does “gentle” mean in “She is calm and gentle”?",["kind and not rough","very fast","wild","angry"]]]},{"t":"The Science Fair Volcano","p":["Andrés and Valentina are partners for the school science fair. Their project is a volcano.","They make the volcano with clay and paint it brown and green. Inside, there is a small bottle. When you put baking soda and vinegar in the bottle, red foam comes out, like lava.","On the day of the fair, the judges come to their table. Valentina explains the science: the baking soda and the vinegar make a gas, and the gas pushes the foam up.","Then Andrés adds the vinegar. But he is nervous, and he adds too much! The red foam goes everywhere, even on the judge’s shoes.","Everyone laughs, including the judge. “That is a very real volcano,” she says.","They don’t win first place, but they win the prize for “Most Exciting Project.”"],"q":[["What is their project?",["a volcano","a robot","a plant","a solar system"]],["What is the volcano made of?",["clay","paper","wood","metal"]],["What makes the gas?",["baking soda and vinegar","water and salt","paint and soap","sugar and milk"]],["What is Andrés’s mistake?",["He adds too much vinegar.","He forgets the bottle.","He drops the volcano.","He paints it the wrong color."]],["What prize do they win?",["Most Exciting Project","First place","Best Painting","Best Science Project"]],["Why does the judge say “That is a very real volcano”?",["The foam goes everywhere, like real lava.","The volcano is very big.","The volcano is made of rock.","She is angry about her shoes."]],["What does “partners” mean in “Andrés and Valentina are partners”?",["people who work together","brothers and sisters","teachers","judges"]]]},{"t":"Our Day of the Dead Altar","p":["Every year at the beginning of November, my family makes an altar for the Day of the Dead. This year, it is for my great-grandma Rosa.","First, we put a table in the living room and cover it with a white cloth. Then we put her photo at the top.","We decorate the altar with orange cempasúchil flowers, candles and colorful papel picado. My little cousins draw sugar skulls.","We also put her favorite things on the altar: hot chocolate, pan de muerto and a small radio, because she loved music.","In the evening, my grandma tells us stories about Great-Grandma Rosa. She was funny, and she danced at every party.","The Day of the Dead is not a sad day. It is a day to remember the people we love."],"q":[["When does the family make the altar?",["at the beginning of November","in December","on her birthday","in October"]],["Who is the altar for?",["Great-Grandma Rosa","Grandpa","a famous singer","Great-Grandpa"]],["What color are the cempasúchil flowers?",["orange","white","purple","red"]],["Why is there a radio on the altar?",["She loved music.","It is a decoration.","Grandma wants to listen.","It is for the cousins."]],["How does the writer describe the Day of the Dead?",["a day to remember people we love","a very sad day","a day for scary costumes","a day for big parties only"]],["Why do they put hot chocolate and pan de muerto on the altar?",["They were Great-Grandma Rosa’s favorite things.","They are for the cousins.","They are the decorations every family uses.","The family is hungry."]],["What does “remember” mean in “a day to remember the people we love”?",["think about someone from the past","forget someone","meet new people","say goodbye forever"]]]}],"children|B2":[{"t":"How Video Games Are Made","p":["When you play your favorite video game, it is easy to forget that hundreds of people may have spent years creating it. Making a game is a complex process that combines art, storytelling, music and engineering.","Everything begins with an idea. Game designers decide what the player will do, what the rules are and what makes the game challenging but fun. Writers then develop the story and the characters, while concept artists sketch how the world will look.","Next, programmers turn those ideas into code, the instructions that tell the computer exactly what to do. Meanwhile, 3D artists build characters and landscapes, animators bring them to life, and sound designers record everything from footsteps to explosions. Composers write music that changes depending on what is happening on the screen.","Before a game is released, it goes through a long testing phase. Testers play the same levels again and again, looking for bugs: errors that make the game freeze, crash or behave strangely. Fixing these problems can take months.","If you enjoy drawing, writing, music, mathematics or solving puzzles, there may be a place for you in the game industry. The most successful teams are those in which people with very different talents learn to collaborate."],"q":[["What does the first paragraph say about making a game?",["It can take many people and many years.","One person usually makes it in a week.","It only needs programmers.","It is easy and fast."]],["What do game designers decide?",["the rules and what the player will do","the music","the price of the game","the story and characters"]],["What do composers do?",["They write music that changes with the action.","They record footsteps.","They test the levels.","They draw the landscapes."]],["What is a bug?",["an error that makes the game behave strangely","a character in the game","a type of music","a big level at the end"]],["According to the writer, what makes a team successful?",["people with different talents who collaborate","only expert programmers","working alone","having the most money"]],["Why does testing take a long time?",["Testers must find and fix many errors.","Testers play only once.","Games are very short.","The music must be recorded again."]],["What does “collaborate” mean in “people with very different talents learn to collaborate”?",["work together","compete","work alone","argue"]]]},{"t":"Life on the International Space Station","p":["About 400 kilometers above our heads, the International Space Station (ISS) travels around the Earth at roughly 28,000 kilometers per hour. That is so fast that it completes an orbit every ninety minutes, which means astronauts on board see around sixteen sunrises and sixteen sunsets every day.","Living in microgravity changes almost everything. Astronauts don’t sleep in beds; instead, they zip themselves into sleeping bags attached to the wall so they don’t float around and bump into equipment. Food comes in sealed packages, and drinks are consumed through straws. Even something as simple as crumbs can be dangerous, because they could float into an astronaut’s eye or into delicate machines.","One of the biggest challenges is staying healthy. Without gravity, muscles and bones get weaker because they don’t have to support the body’s weight. To prevent this, astronauts must exercise for about two hours every day, using special treadmills and machines with straps.","Water is extremely valuable on the station. A recycling system collects moisture from the air, sweat and even urine, and turns it into clean drinking water. As astronauts like to joke, “Today’s coffee is yesterday’s coffee.”","Despite the difficulties, many astronauts describe their time on the ISS as life-changing. Seeing the Earth from above, without borders, often makes them feel a strong responsibility to protect our planet."],"q":[["How often does the ISS complete an orbit?",["every ninety minutes","every twenty-four hours","every sixteen minutes","every week"]],["Why do astronauts sleep in bags attached to the wall?",["so they don’t float and hit equipment","because it is very cold","because there are no beds","because it is noisy"]],["Why can crumbs be dangerous?",["They can float into eyes or machines.","They attract insects.","They make the food go bad.","They are very hot."]],["Why must astronauts exercise two hours a day?",["to stop their muscles and bones from weakening","because they are bored","to produce electricity","to have fun with friends"]],["What does “Today’s coffee is yesterday’s coffee” refer to?",["water recycling","old coffee packets","the speed of the station","the coffee machine"]],["Why do astronauts feel responsible for protecting the planet?",["They see the Earth from above, without borders.","They work for the government.","Their coffee is recycled.","They see sixteen sunsets a day."]],["What does “sealed” mean in “Food comes in sealed packages”?",["closed so nothing can get in or out","open","very big","frozen"]]]},{"t":"The Secret Language of Elephants","p":["Elephants are famous for their size and their memory, but scientists are discovering that their communication is far more complex than anyone imagined.","Much of it happens at frequencies too low for humans to hear. These deep rumbles can travel several kilometers, allowing family groups that are far apart to stay in contact. Even more surprisingly, elephants can detect some of these vibrations through the ground. Special sensors in their feet and trunks pick up the signals, so in a sense, they “listen” with their feet.","In 2024, a group of researchers studying wild African elephants in Kenya published an extraordinary finding. After analyzing hundreds of recorded calls, they concluded that elephants seem to use individual names for one another. When researchers played a call addressed to a particular elephant, that elephant responded more strongly than when it heard a call meant for someone else. Very few animals are known to do this.","Elephants also communicate through touch, using their trunks to comfort a stressed calf or greet a friend, and through body language, such as spreading their ears when they feel threatened.","Understanding how elephants communicate is not just fascinating; it may also help protect them. If conservationists can recognize alarm calls, for example, they may be able to prevent conflicts between elephants and farmers."],"q":[["Why can’t humans hear many elephant sounds?",["They are too low.","They are too quiet.","They happen at night.","They are too fast."]],["How can elephants detect vibrations?",["through sensors in their feet and trunks","only with their ears","through their tusks","through their tails"]],["What did researchers in Kenya discover?",["Elephants seem to use names for each other.","Elephants can sing.","Elephants forget their families.","Elephants can read."]],["How did the researchers test their idea?",["They played calls and watched how elephants responded.","They taught elephants new words.","They counted elephants in the park.","They followed elephants with drones."]],["How could this research help elephants?",["It may help prevent conflicts with farmers.","It will help sell more tickets to zoos.","It will make elephants grow bigger.","It will teach elephants to talk."]],["Why is the finding about names so surprising?",["Very few animals are known to do it.","Elephants never make sounds.","Names are only for zoo animals.","Elephants forget their families."]],["What does “detect” mean in “elephants can detect some of these vibrations”?",["notice or feel","make","stop","forget"]]]},{"t":"The City in the Jungle","p":["Deep in the jungle of Campeche, in southern Mexico, stand the ruins of Calakmul, a city that was once among the most powerful in the Maya world. Between roughly 500 and 700 AD, it was home to tens of thousands of people and controlled a network of smaller cities across the region.","The Maya of Calakmul called their kingdom the Kingdom of the Snake. For more than a century, it fought a long rivalry with another great city, Tikal, in what is now Guatemala. Inscriptions carved in stone describe alliances, battles and the kings who led them, giving historians a detailed picture of Maya politics.","Calakmul’s largest pyramid, known as Structure II, rises about forty-five meters above the forest floor. Visitors who climb it are rewarded with an extraordinary view: an ocean of green that stretches to the horizon in every direction, interrupted only by the tops of other ancient buildings.","Because the city is so remote, it receives far fewer tourists than places like Chichén Itzá. That isolation has helped protect the surrounding forest, which is now part of a biosphere reserve. Jaguars, howler monkeys and hundreds of bird species live there.","In 2014, UNESCO recognized Calakmul as a World Heritage Site for both its cultural and natural value, one of very few places in the world to receive that double honor."],"q":[["Where is Calakmul?",["in the jungle of Campeche","in Guatemala","near Mexico City","in Yucatán"]],["What was Calakmul’s main rival?",["Tikal","Chichén Itzá","Palenque","Teotihuacan"]],["What do the inscriptions describe?",["alliances, battles and kings","recipes and medicine","the weather","sports and games"]],["Why does Calakmul receive fewer tourists?",["It is very remote.","It is closed to visitors.","It is very small.","It is dangerous."]],["Why is Calakmul’s UNESCO recognition special?",["It was recognized for both cultural and natural value.","It was the first site in Mexico.","It is the oldest city in the world.","It has the biggest pyramid in the world."]],["How has the small number of tourists helped Calakmul?",["The surrounding forest is better protected.","The pyramids are taller.","More people live there.","It has more hotels."]],["What does “remote” mean in “Because the city is so remote”?",["far away from towns and roads","very old","very busy","very small"]]]},{"t":"Can You Believe Everything Online?","p":["Every day, we see hundreds of headlines, videos and images online. Some are accurate, some are mistaken, and some are deliberately false. Learning to tell the difference is one of the most useful skills you can develop.","False information spreads quickly for a simple reason: it is often designed to provoke strong emotions. A shocking headline makes us feel surprised, angry or scared, and we share it before we stop to think. Researchers have found that false news can travel faster across social media than true stories.","Fortunately, there are a few simple questions that can help you investigate. First, who created this? Is it a known news organization, an expert, or an anonymous account? Second, what is the evidence? Reliable information usually explains where facts come from. Third, what do other sources say? If an important event really happened, several trustworthy sources will report it.","Images deserve special attention. A photo can be real but taken in a different place or year, and modern technology can create completely artificial pictures and videos. A reverse image search can sometimes reveal where a photo originally came from.","Being skeptical doesn’t mean believing nothing. It means taking a moment before you accept or share something. That moment can stop a rumor from spreading."],"q":[["Why does false information spread quickly?",["It provokes strong emotions.","It is always funny.","It is written by experts.","It is very short."]],["What is the first question you should ask?",["Who created this?","How many likes does it have?","Is it long or short?","Is it in color?"]],["What do reliable sources usually do?",["explain where their facts come from","use shocking headlines","hide the author’s name","post many photos"]],["What can a reverse image search reveal?",["where a photo originally came from","who liked the photo","how to edit the photo","how many people saw it"]],["According to the text, what does being skeptical mean?",["taking a moment before accepting or sharing","believing nothing at all","never using the internet","sharing everything quickly"]],["Why should you check what other sources say?",["Important true events are reported by several trustworthy sources.","Other sources are always wrong.","It is more fun.","It gives you more likes."]],["What does “skeptical” mean in “Being skeptical doesn’t mean believing nothing”?",["careful about believing things too quickly","very angry","happy and excited","sure about everything"]]]},{"t":"From Olympia to the Modern Games","p":["The first recorded Olympic Games took place in 776 BC in Olympia, a religious site in ancient Greece dedicated to the god Zeus. For the first few competitions, there was only one event: a sprint of about 192 meters called the stadion. The winner was a cook named Koroibos.","Over time, the Games expanded to include wrestling, boxing, chariot racing and the pentathlon. Athletes competed naked, and only free Greek men were allowed to participate. Married women were not even permitted to watch. Winners did not receive gold medals; they were crowned with a wreath of olive leaves, but they returned home as heroes.","Remarkably, the Games were held every four years for more than a thousand years, until they were abolished around 393 AD.","The modern Olympics were revived in Athens in 1896, thanks largely to a French educator, Pierre de Coubertin, who believed sport could promote peace and international understanding. Women first competed in 1900, and today the Games include thousands of athletes from over two hundred nations.","Mexico hosted the Summer Olympics in 1968, becoming the first Latin American country to do so. Those Games are remembered for several world records, partly due to Mexico City’s high altitude, and for Enriqueta Basilio, the first woman ever to light the Olympic cauldron."],"q":[["What was the only event in the first Olympic Games?",["a sprint called the stadion","wrestling","chariot racing","boxing"]],["What did ancient winners receive?",["a wreath of olive leaves","a gold medal","a bag of money","a crown of gold"]],["Who helped revive the modern Olympics?",["Pierre de Coubertin","Koroibos","Zeus","Enriqueta Basilio"]],["When did women first compete in the modern Games?",["1900","1896","1968","1924"]],["What was special about Enriqueta Basilio?",["She was the first woman to light the Olympic cauldron.","She won Mexico’s first gold medal.","She organized the 1968 Games.","She was the youngest athlete."]],["Why did Pierre de Coubertin want to bring back the Olympics?",["He believed sport could promote peace.","He wanted to win a medal.","He liked ancient Greece.","He wanted to build stadiums."]],["What does “abolished” mean in “until they were abolished around 393 AD”?",["officially stopped","made bigger","moved to another city","started again"]]]}],"teens|Pre-A1":[{"t":"This Is Me","p":["Hi! My name is Daniela. I am fourteen years old.","I am from Monterrey, in Mexico. I live with my mom, my dad and my brother.","I like music and basketball. I don’t like math.","My favorite color is purple. My favorite food is tacos.","I have a cat. Her name is Luna. She is black and white."],"q":[["How old is Daniela?",["fourteen","twelve","sixteen","fifteen"]],["Where is she from?",["Monterrey","Cancún","Guadalajara","Mexico City"]],["What sport does she like?",["basketball","football","tennis","volleyball"]],["What is her favorite food?",["tacos","pizza","salad","burgers"]],["What is her cat’s name?",["Luna","Daniela","Sol","Pepa"]],["Which sentence is true about Daniela?",["She doesn’t like math.","She lives alone.","She has a dog.","She doesn’t like music."]]]},{"t":"My Weekend","p":["Hi! I’m Andrea. I’m fourteen and I live in Monterrey.","On Saturday morning, I sleep late. I get up at ten o’clock!","In the afternoon, I meet my friends at the mall. We eat ice cream and talk a lot.","On Saturday night, I watch a series with my sister.","On Sunday, I have lunch with my grandparents. Then I do my homework.","I love weekends!"],"q":[["How old is Andrea?",["fourteen","twelve","sixteen","fifteen"]],["What time does she get up on Saturday?",["at ten o’clock","at seven o’clock","at noon","at eight o’clock"]],["Where does she meet her friends?",["at the mall","at school","at the park","at the movies"]],["Who does she watch a series with?",["her sister","her friends","her grandparents","her mom"]],["What does she do after lunch on Sunday?",["her homework","sports","shopping","She visits her friends."]],["Why does Andrea love weekends?",["She can relax and see friends and family.","She has no homework.","She goes on trips.","She works at the mall."]]]},{"t":"My School Timetable","p":["My name is Iker. I’m in ninth grade. This is my Monday.","School starts at seven thirty. My first class is math. It is difficult, but my teacher is good.","At nine o’clock, I have English. It is my favorite class!","Then I have history and science.","At eleven, it’s break time. I eat a sandwich and play soccer.","After the break, I have art and P.E. School finishes at two o’clock."],"q":[["What grade is Iker in?",["ninth grade","sixth grade","eleventh grade","seventh grade"]],["What is his first class?",["math","English","art","history"]],["What is his favorite class?",["English","history","science","math"]],["What does he do at break time?",["eats a sandwich and plays soccer","reads a book","goes home","talks to his teacher"]],["What time does school finish?",["at two o’clock","at eleven o’clock","at four o’clock","at one o’clock"]],["What does Iker think about math?",["It is difficult, but the teacher is good.","It is his favorite class.","It is very easy.","He doesn’t have math on Monday."]]]},{"t":"Food I Like (and Don’t Like)","p":["I’m Mateo. I love food, but I don’t like everything!","I love pizza with pepperoni. I love tacos de trompo, too.","I like fruit. My favorite fruit is mango.","I don’t like broccoli. It’s green and boring.","I really don’t like fish. My mom loves it, but not me!","For breakfast, I have cereal and orange juice.","And you? What food do you like?"],"q":[["What pizza does Mateo love?",["pepperoni","cheese","Hawaiian","vegetarian"]],["What is his favorite fruit?",["mango","apple","banana","orange"]],["Why doesn’t he like broccoli?",["It’s green and boring.","It’s spicy.","It’s expensive.","It’s too sweet."]],["Who loves fish?",["his mom","Mateo","his friend","his sister"]],["What does he have for breakfast?",["cereal and orange juice","tacos","pizza","eggs and bread"]],["Which food does Mateo NOT like?",["fish","pizza","mango","tacos de trompo"]]]},{"t":"My City: Monterrey","p":["I’m Leo, and this is my city. It’s Monterrey, in the north of Mexico.","There are big mountains around the city. My favorite mountain is the Cerro de la Silla.","There is a big park. It’s called Parque Fundidora. I ride my bike there.","There is a river walk, too. It’s called Paseo Santa Lucía. You can take a boat!","In summer, it’s very hot.","I like my city a lot. Come and visit!"],"q":[["Where is Monterrey?",["in the north of Mexico","in the south of Mexico","in the center of Mexico","near the beach"]],["What is Leo’s favorite mountain?",["the Cerro de la Silla","the Cerro de las Mitras","the Huasteca","the Pico de Orizaba"]],["What does he do in Parque Fundidora?",["He rides his bike.","He swims.","He takes a boat.","He plays soccer."]],["What can you do at Paseo Santa Lucía?",["take a boat","climb a mountain","see animals","go skiing"]],["What is the weather like in summer?",["very hot","very cold","rainy","snowy"]],["Why does Leo say “Come and visit!”?",["He likes his city and wants people to see it.","He wants to sell his bike.","He wants to leave Monterrey.","He needs help with homework."]]]},{"t":"My Music","p":["Hi! I’m Daniela. Music is my life!","I listen to music every day. I listen in the car, on the bus and in my room.","I like pop and rock. My favorite singer is from Colombia.","I have headphones. They are pink and white.","I play the guitar, too. I have lessons on Wednesday.","My brother plays the drums. It’s very loud! My dad says, “Stop, please!”"],"q":[["When does Daniela listen to music?",["every day","only on weekends","only at school","only at night"]],["What music does she like?",["pop and rock","jazz","classical","reggaeton only"]],["What color are her headphones?",["pink and white","black","blue","green"]],["When are her guitar lessons?",["on Wednesday","on Saturday","on Monday","on Friday"]],["What does her brother play?",["the drums","the guitar","the piano","the violin"]],["Why does Daniela’s dad say “Stop, please!”?",["The drums are very loud.","Daniela sings badly.","It is very late.","The guitar is broken."]]]}],"teens|A1+":[{"t":"My Favorite App","p":["My name is Santiago and I’m fifteen. I use my phone every day, but my favorite app isn’t a game or a social network. It’s an app to learn the guitar.","The app listens to me when I play. When I play a note correctly, it turns green. When I make a mistake, it turns red and the app shows me the right position for my fingers.","I practice for twenty minutes every evening, after my homework. On weekends, I practice more. The app has songs for every level, from very easy to very difficult.","I started six months ago. Now I can play eight songs, and my favorite is a song by my dad’s favorite band. He is very surprised!","Next year, I want to play in the school concert with my friends."],"q":[["What is Santiago’s favorite app for?",["learning the guitar","playing games","watching videos","learning English"]],["What happens when he makes a mistake?",["The note turns red.","The app closes.","The note turns green.","The phone turns off."]],["When does he practice on school days?",["in the evening, after his homework","in the morning","at school","before school"]],["How many songs can he play now?",["eight","six","twenty","twelve"]],["What does he want to do next year?",["play in the school concert","buy a new phone","make an app","teach guitar classes"]],["Why is Santiago’s dad surprised?",["Santiago can play a song by his favorite band.","Santiago stopped practicing.","Santiago made his own app.","Santiago broke the guitar."]],["What does “practice” mean in “I practice for twenty minutes”?",["do something many times to get better","listen to music","play a game","buy something"]]]},{"t":"My First Concert","p":["For my fifteenth birthday, my parents gave me the best present ever: two tickets to see my favorite band at Estadio Banorte. I invited my best friend, Luis.","On the day of the concert, we arrived three hours early because we wanted to be close to the stage. There were already thousands of people outside. Some had painted faces, and many were wearing the band’s T-shirts.","When the lights went out, everybody screamed. Then the music started, and the whole stadium started singing. I knew every word of every song.","In the middle of the concert, the singer said in Spanish, “¡Gracias, Monterrey!” and everyone went crazy. Luis and I recorded some videos, but most of the time we just enjoyed the moment.","The concert finished at midnight. My ears were ringing and my voice was gone, but I was so happy.","I still keep my ticket in a frame in my room."],"q":[["Why did the narrator go to the concert?",["It was his birthday present.","He won a contest.","Luis invited him.","His teacher gave him tickets."]],["Why did they arrive three hours early?",["to be close to the stage","to buy T-shirts","to meet the band","to eat dinner"]],["What did the singer say in Spanish?",["¡Gracias, Monterrey!","¡Buenas noches!","¡Hola, México!","¡Adiós, amigos!"]],["How did the narrator feel after the concert?",["very happy","bored","angry","tired and sad"]],["Where does the narrator keep the ticket?",["in a frame in his room","in his wallet","on his phone","on his wall with tape"]],["What does “My ears were ringing and my voice was gone” tell us?",["The concert was very loud and he sang a lot.","He was sick before the concert.","He didn’t like the music.","He slept during the concert."]],["What does “stage” mean in “close to the stage”?",["the place where the band plays","the entrance","the parking lot","the ticket office"]]]},{"t":"Online Friends","p":["I have three good friends that I have never met in person. Their names are Aiko from Japan, Lucas from Brazil and Emma from Ireland. We met two years ago in an online art community, where people share their drawings.","We usually chat in English, because it’s the only language we all speak. At first, my English was not very good, but now I write much better. Aiko says her English improved a lot, too.","Every month, we have a drawing challenge. Someone chooses a topic, like “ocean” or “a city in the future,” and we all draw it. Then we give each other feedback.","My parents were worried at the beginning, so I showed them our conversations and explained the rules I follow: I never share my address, my school or personal photos.","One day, I hope we can meet in person. Until then, I’m happy to have friends on three continents."],"q":[["How did the narrator meet these online friends?",["in an online art community","at school","on a trip","at a summer camp"]],["Why do they chat in English?",["It’s the only language they all speak.","Their teacher told them to.","They all live in the U.S.","They are learning English at school."]],["What happens every month?",["They have a drawing challenge.","They meet in person.","They send letters.","They play a video game together."]],["What does the narrator never share online?",["her address, school or personal photos","her drawings","her opinions","her favorite colors"]],["What does the narrator hope for the future?",["to meet her friends in person","to stop drawing","to move to Japan","to work as a translator"]],["Why did the narrator show the conversations to the parents?",["They were worried about online friends.","They wanted to learn English.","They wanted to draw too.","They asked for the passwords."]],["What does “feedback” mean in “we give each other feedback”?",["comments to help someone improve","presents","money","new friends"]]]},{"t":"Volunteer Day at the Animal Shelter","p":["Every year, my school organizes a volunteer day. Students can choose to help at a food bank, clean a park or work at an animal shelter. I chose the shelter because my friends chose it, not because I was very interested.","When we arrived, the shelter manager, Doña Elvira, explained the jobs. There were more than eighty dogs and thirty cats, and most of them were waiting for a family.","First, we cleaned the dogs’ areas. It wasn’t pleasant, but it was necessary. Then we gave them fresh water and food.","After lunch, we took the dogs for walks. My dog was an old, brown dog called Bruno. He was slow and a little sad, but when I sat with him, he put his head on my leg.","At the end of the day, I didn’t want to leave. Now I volunteer at the shelter every second Saturday.","And good news: last week, a family adopted Bruno."],"q":[["Why did the narrator choose the shelter at first?",["His friends chose it.","He loves animals.","His teacher told him to.","It was close to his house."]],["How many dogs were at the shelter?",["more than eighty","thirty","ten","fifty"]],["What was the first job?",["cleaning the dogs’ areas","walking the dogs","feeding the cats","giving baths to the cats"]],["What did Bruno do when the narrator sat with him?",["He put his head on Ricardo’s leg.","He ran away.","He barked.","He jumped and played."]],["What happened to Bruno last week?",["A family adopted him.","He got sick.","Ricardo took him home.","He went to another shelter."]],["How did the narrator change after volunteer day?",["The narrator started to care about the animals and keeps volunteering.","The narrator decided to never go back.","The narrator adopted a cat.","The narrator got a job at the food bank."]],["What does “pleasant” mean in “It wasn’t pleasant”?",["nice or enjoyable","difficult","dangerous","fast"]]]},{"t":"The Art Class","p":["For years, I always said the same thing: “I can’t draw.” When I had to choose an elective class this semester, art was the only class with space, so I took it. I was not happy.","On the first day, our teacher, Mr. Herrera, asked us to draw our hand. My drawing looked like a potato with five sausages. Everyone laughed, including me.","But Mr. Herrera said something I didn’t expect. “Drawing is not a talent. It’s a skill, like riding a bike. Nobody is good at the beginning.”","Every class, we practiced simple things: lines, shadows, shapes. He taught us to look carefully before drawing. Little by little, my drawings started to look better.","At the end of the semester, I drew my hand again. I put the two drawings next to each other. I couldn’t believe it was the same person.","Now I carry a small sketchbook in my backpack, and I draw almost every day."],"q":[["Why did the narrator take art?",["It was the only class with space.","She loves drawing.","Her friends were in it.","Her parents made her."]],["What did her first drawing look like?",["a potato with five sausages","a perfect hand","a flower","a tree"]],["What did Mr. Herrera say about drawing?",["It’s a skill, not a talent.","Some people can never draw.","It’s easy for everyone.","Only talented people can draw."]],["What did they practice in class?",["lines, shadows and shapes","painting portraits","sculpture","computer design"]],["What does the narrator do now?",["She draws almost every day.","She never draws.","She teaches art.","She stopped taking art."]],["What surprised the narrator at the end of the semester?",["The new drawing was much better than the first one.","The teacher gave a prize.","Nobody laughed anymore.","The class was cancelled."]],["What does “skill” mean in “It’s a skill, like riding a bike”?",["something you learn by practicing","something you are born with","a kind of art class","a type of pencil"]]]},{"t":"A Different Morning","p":["Last year, my mornings were terrible. My alarm rang at 6:30, but I always pressed snooze three or four times. Then I jumped out of bed, put on my uniform, didn’t eat breakfast and ran to the bus. I arrived at school tired and hungry, and I couldn’t concentrate in my first class.","The problem started the night before. I was on my phone in bed until one or two in the morning, watching videos.","So my older brother gave me a challenge: for two weeks, my phone had to stay in the kitchen after ten o’clock. I thought it was impossible.","The first nights, I couldn’t sleep, so I read a book. After four days, I started to fall asleep earlier. After a week, I woke up before my alarm!","Now I have time to eat breakfast and walk to the bus calmly. My grades are better, and I feel less stressed.","My phone still sleeps in the kitchen."],"q":[["What did the narrator do when the alarm rang?",["He pressed snooze several times.","He got up immediately.","He turned it off and slept all day.","He asked his brother to wake him up."]],["What was the real cause of the problem?",["He used his phone late at night.","His alarm was broken.","His bus left too early.","He had too much homework."]],["What was his brother’s challenge?",["Leave the phone in the kitchen after ten.","Get up at five o’clock.","Go to school by bike.","Don’t use the phone at school."]],["What did the narrator do the first nights?",["He read a book.","He watched TV.","He played video games.","He went for a run."]],["What changed for the narrator?",["He has time for breakfast and feels less stressed.","He sleeps in class now.","He bought a new phone.","He goes to school by taxi now."]],["Why couldn’t the narrator concentrate in the first class?",["He was tired and hungry.","The class was boring.","The teacher spoke too fast.","He forgot his books."]],["What does “stressed” mean in “I feel less stressed”?",["worried and tense","sleepy","bored","hungry"]]]}],"teens|A2+":[{"t":"A Trip to Chichén Itzá","p":["In March, my class went on a school trip to Yucatán. We flew from Monterrey to Mérida and then traveled by bus for about two hours to Chichén Itzá, the ancient Maya city.","When we arrived, it was incredibly hot, so our teacher made sure everyone had a hat and plenty of water. Our guide, Don Felipe, explained that Chichén Itzá was one of the most important cities of the Maya world more than a thousand years ago.","The most famous building is El Castillo, a pyramid with 365 steps if you add all four sides together, one for each day of the year. Don Felipe told us that on the spring and autumn equinoxes, the shadows on the stairs look like a snake coming down the pyramid.","The part I liked most was the ball court. Don Felipe asked us to clap in the middle of it, and the sound echoed several times. Nobody could believe it!","In the afternoon, we swam in a cenote, a natural pool of fresh water in the rocks. I was a bit scared at first because the water was so deep, but it was the best moment of the trip."],"q":[["How did the class get from Mérida to Chichén Itzá?",["by bus","by plane","by train","by boat"]],["Why does El Castillo have 365 steps?",["one for each day of the year","one for each Maya king","because it is very tall","one for each student"]],["What can people see on the equinoxes?",["shadows that look like a snake","the sun rising inside the pyramid","a festival of lights","snow on the pyramid"]],["What happened when the students clapped in the ball court?",["The sound echoed several times.","The guide got angry.","Nothing happened.","The lights turned on."]],["How did the narrator feel at first in the cenote?",["a bit scared","bored","very tired","very happy"]],["Why did the teacher make sure everyone had a hat and water?",["It was incredibly hot.","It was raining.","They were going to swim.","The guide asked for it."]],["What does “echoed” mean in “the sound echoed several times”?",["came back and was heard again","stopped immediately","became music","was very quiet"]]]},{"t":"Is Esports a Real Sport?","p":["Esports, or electronic sports, are competitive video game tournaments. Today, they are a huge industry. The biggest events fill stadiums, millions of fans watch online, and top players can win prizes worth millions of dollars.","But many people ask the same question: is esports a real sport?","People who say yes point to the training. Professional players often practice eight to ten hours a day. They study strategy, analyze their opponents and work with coaches, nutritionists and even psychologists. Their reaction times are incredibly fast, and they must make hundreds of decisions per minute under pressure.","People who say no argue that sports require physical effort. A soccer player runs about ten kilometers in a game, while a gamer sits in a chair. They also worry that spending so many hours in front of a screen is not healthy.","Some organizations have made their decision. Esports were included as a medal event at the 2022 Asian Games, and many universities now offer esports scholarships.","Maybe the answer depends on how we define “sport.” What do you think?"],"q":[["What are esports?",["competitive video game tournaments","sports played outside","online fitness classes","online math competitions"]],["How long do professional players often practice?",["eight to ten hours a day","one hour a day","only on weekends","thirty minutes a day"]],["What is one argument against esports being a sport?",["Sports require physical effort.","Esports are too expensive.","Esports are too new.","Players earn too much money."]],["Where were esports a medal event?",["at the 2022 Asian Games","at the 2020 Olympics","at the World Cup","at the Pan American Games"]],["What does the writer suggest at the end?",["The answer depends on how we define sport.","Esports are definitely not a sport.","Esports are better than soccer.","Esports should be banned."]],["Why does the writer mention coaches, nutritionists and psychologists?",["to show that esports players train like athletes","to show that esports are expensive","to show that gamers are often sick","to show that esports are easy"]],["What does “opponents” mean in “analyze their opponents”?",["the people you play against","your teammates","your fans","your coaches"]]]},{"t":"The Exchange Student","p":["When my parents told me that a German exchange student was going to live with us for five months, I was nervous. What if we didn’t get along? What if she didn’t like Mexican food?","Clara arrived in August. She was sixteen, like me, very tall and a little shy. Her Spanish was basic, so at first we spoke a mix of English and Spanish, with a lot of gestures.","Some things surprised her. She couldn’t believe how late we eat dinner, sometimes at nine or ten at night. In Germany, her family eats at six. She was also surprised that people greet each other with a kiss on the cheek, even people they just met.","Some things surprised me, too. Clara was always on time, exactly on time. If we said “at four,” she was ready at 3:59. And she taught me that in Germany, students often go to school by bike, even when it’s cold.","By December, her Spanish was excellent, and she loved tacos al pastor more than I do. When she left, we all cried at the airport.","This summer, I’m going to visit her in Hamburg."],"q":[["Why was the narrator nervous at first?",["She worried they wouldn’t get along.","She didn’t speak German.","Clara was older.","Her house was too small."]],["How did they communicate at first?",["a mix of English and Spanish with gestures","only German","only Spanish","with a translation app only"]],["What surprised Clara about dinner?",["Mexicans eat dinner very late.","The food was very spicy.","Everyone eats alone.","Dinner was very early."]],["What surprised the narrator about Clara?",["She was always exactly on time.","She didn’t like tacos.","She spoke perfect Spanish.","She was always late."]],["What is the narrator going to do this summer?",["visit Clara in Hamburg","host another student","study German in Mexico","move to Germany forever"]],["How did the relationship between the narrator and Clara change?",["They became very close, and everyone cried when she left.","They never got along.","Clara wanted to leave early.","They only spoke in German."]],["What does “get along” mean in “What if we didn’t get along?”",["have a friendly relationship","travel together","study together","argue"]]]},{"t":"How I Learned Japanese Online","p":["Two years ago, I became obsessed with Japanese anime. At first, I watched everything with Spanish subtitles. Then I started to wonder: what if I could understand it without them?","I didn’t have money for classes, so I decided to learn on my own. I started with a free app for twenty minutes every day. The first challenge was the writing. Japanese uses three different writing systems: hiragana, katakana and kanji. It took me two months just to learn the first two.","I also found other free resources: a YouTube channel with grammar lessons, podcasts for beginners and an online community where Japanese people practice Spanish and Mexicans practice Japanese. I had my first video call with a girl from Osaka. I was so nervous that I forgot how to say “hello.”","The most important thing I learned is that consistency is more important than talent. Twenty minutes every day is better than three hours once a week.","Last month, I watched a full episode without subtitles and understood most of it. It was one of the best moments of my life.","My next goal is to pass a beginner Japanese exam."],"q":[["Why did the writer start learning Japanese?",["He loved anime.","He moved to Japan.","His school offered classes.","His friends spoke Japanese."]],["Why didn’t the writer take classes?",["He didn’t have money.","There were no classes.","He didn’t have time.","He preferred to study alone."]],["What was the first challenge?",["the writing systems","the pronunciation","finding an app","learning grammar"]],["What happened in the writer’s first video call?",["He forgot how to say hello.","He spoke perfectly.","The call didn’t work.","His internet stopped."]],["What does the writer say is more important than talent?",["consistency","money","a good teacher","expensive books"]],["Why was watching an episode without subtitles such an important moment?",["It showed that two years of daily practice worked.","It was the last episode of the series.","The writer didn’t like subtitles.","The writer won a prize for it."]],["What does “consistency” mean in “consistency is more important than talent”?",["doing something regularly over time","speaking very fast","having a lot of money","being very intelligent"]]]},{"t":"Climbing at El Potrero Chico","p":["About forty-five minutes from Monterrey, near the town of Hidalgo, there are enormous limestone walls that rise hundreds of meters into the sky. This place is called El Potrero Chico, and it is one of the most famous rock climbing destinations in the world.","Every winter, from November to March, climbers arrive from the United States, Canada, Europe and beyond. They come for the excellent rock, the dry weather and the long routes. Some routes are more than five hundred meters tall and take a full day to climb.","My cousin Mariana is a climbing instructor there, and last December she took me for my first climb. Before we started, she checked my harness, helmet and ropes very carefully. “Safety first, always,” she said.","My route was only twenty meters, but halfway up I looked down and my legs started shaking. Mariana shouted from below: “Don’t look down. Look for the next hold.” I took a deep breath and kept going.","When I reached the top, the view of the mountains was incredible.","Climbing taught me that big challenges become possible when you focus on one small step at a time."],"q":[["How far is El Potrero Chico from Monterrey?",["about forty-five minutes","about five hours","about ten minutes","about two hours"]],["When do most climbers visit?",["from November to March","in summer","all year equally","only in July"]],["Who is Mariana?",["the narrator’s cousin and a climbing instructor","a famous climber from Canada","the narrator’s teacher at school","a guide from the town"]],["What happened halfway up the route?",["The narrator’s legs started shaking.","The rope broke.","It started to rain.","The helmet fell off."]],["What lesson did climbing teach the narrator?",["Focus on one small step at a time.","Never try difficult things.","Always climb alone.","Climbing is only for experts."]],["Why did Mariana check the equipment so carefully?",["Safety is the most important thing in climbing.","She wanted to sell new equipment.","The narrator asked her to.","The equipment was broken."]],["What does “hold” mean in “Look for the next hold”?",["a place to put your hand or foot","a short rest","a rope","a helmet"]]]},{"t":"The Real Cost of a Cheap T-Shirt","p":["Today, it is possible to buy a T-shirt for less than one hundred pesos. Stores change their collections every few weeks, so there is always something new. This system is called fast fashion.","Fast fashion is convenient, but it has hidden costs. Producing clothes uses enormous amounts of water. According to some estimates, making a single cotton T-shirt can require about 2,700 liters of water, enough for one person to drink for more than two years.","There is also the problem of waste. Because clothes are so cheap, people often wear them only a few times before throwing them away. Huge amounts of textiles end up in landfills, and many synthetic fabrics take hundreds of years to break down.","Finally, many of the workers who make these clothes earn very low salaries and work long hours in difficult conditions.","So what can teenagers do? You don’t have to stop buying clothes. But you can buy less and choose better quality, swap clothes with friends, shop at second-hand stores or learn to repair a small hole instead of throwing something away.","The most sustainable T-shirt is the one you already have."],"q":[["What is fast fashion?",["cheap clothes with collections that change often","clothes for running","expensive designer clothes","clothes made very quickly by hand"]],["How much water can one cotton T-shirt require?",["about 2,700 liters","about 27 liters","about 270,000 liters","about 2,700 milliliters"]],["Why do people throw away cheap clothes quickly?",["They are cheap, so people wear them only a few times.","They are too big.","They are dangerous.","They become unfashionable in one day."]],["Which is NOT suggested in the text?",["buying a new T-shirt every week","swapping clothes with friends","shopping at second-hand stores","learning to repair small holes"]],["What does the last sentence mean?",["Keeping and using what you have is best.","You should buy a new T-shirt.","T-shirts are not sustainable.","Old T-shirts are bad for the planet."]],["Why does the writer say fast fashion has “hidden costs”?",["Its effects on water, waste and workers are not in the price.","The clothes are secretly expensive.","Stores hide the price tags.","People pay with credit cards."]],["What does “swap” mean in “swap clothes with friends”?",["exchange","sell","wash","buy"]]]}],"teens|B2":[{"t":"Connected but Lonely?","p":["Today’s teenagers are the most connected generation in history. With a phone in their pocket, they can message dozens of friends, follow celebrities and share every moment of their day. And yet, surveys in many countries suggest that loneliness among young people has been increasing.","Researchers point out that the problem is not technology itself, but how it is used. Passive scrolling, simply watching other people’s lives without interacting, is strongly linked to feelings of envy and isolation. Seeing a constant stream of edited photos can make ordinary life feel disappointing by comparison.","Active use, on the other hand, tends to have the opposite effect. Messaging a friend to arrange a meeting, collaborating on a project or joining an online community around a shared interest can actually strengthen relationships.","Another factor is what social media replaces. Hours spent online are often hours taken away from sleep, exercise and face-to-face conversations, all of which play a crucial role in our mood.","None of this means that teenagers should abandon their phones. A more realistic goal is to use them more intentionally: to notice how certain apps make us feel, to set limits when necessary, and to remember that the people around us deserve our full attention too."],"q":[["What paradox does the first paragraph describe?",["Teenagers are very connected, but loneliness is increasing.","Teenagers don’t use phones anymore.","Celebrities feel lonely.","Teenagers have more friends than ever."]],["According to researchers, what is linked to envy and isolation?",["passive scrolling","messaging friends","joining online communities","using a study app"]],["What is an example of active use?",["messaging a friend to arrange a meeting","watching edited photos","scrolling for hours","scrolling through celebrity photos"]],["Why does the writer mention sleep and exercise?",["Time online can replace activities that affect our mood.","They are popular topics on social media.","Teenagers don’t like them.","Teenagers sleep and exercise too much."]],["What does the writer recommend?",["using phones more intentionally","deleting all social media","using phones only at night","stopping face-to-face conversations"]],["What is the writer’s attitude toward technology?",["Balanced: the problem is how we use it, not the technology itself.","Completely negative: phones should be banned.","Completely positive: phones solve loneliness.","Uninterested: the writer doesn’t care about it."]],["What does “intentionally” mean in “use them more intentionally”?",["with a clear purpose","by accident","very often","secretly"]]]},{"t":"AI in the Classroom: Shortcut or Tool?","p":["In just a few years, artificial intelligence tools have moved from science fiction into students’ pockets. With a simple request, a chatbot can summarize a novel, solve an equation or produce a five-paragraph essay in seconds. Unsurprisingly, schools around the world are struggling to decide how to respond.","Some educators see AI mainly as a threat. If a machine can complete an assignment, they argue, students may never develop the skills that the assignment was designed to build: organizing ideas, constructing an argument or persevering through a difficult problem. Learning, after all, often requires effort, and shortcuts can rob students of that productive struggle.","Others believe that banning AI is both unrealistic and counterproductive. These tools are likely to be part of almost every profession in the future, so students need to learn to use them responsibly. Used well, AI can act as a patient tutor that explains a concept in different ways, generates practice questions or gives immediate feedback on a draft.","Many teachers are therefore redesigning their assessments. They are asking students to explain their reasoning orally, write in class, reflect on their process or critically evaluate an AI-generated answer, which is often confidently wrong.","Perhaps the most important question is not whether students use AI, but how. Asking it to do your thinking for you is very different from asking it to help you think better."],"q":[["What concern do some educators have about AI?",["Students may not develop important skills.","AI is too expensive.","AI is too slow.","AI uses too much electricity."]],["Why do some believe banning AI is unrealistic?",["AI will be part of almost every profession.","Students don’t like AI.","AI is always correct.","Teachers already use it every day."]],["How can AI act as a good tool?",["as a patient tutor that gives feedback","by writing all assignments","by replacing teachers","by grading exams automatically"]],["How are many teachers redesigning assessments?",["asking students to explain reasoning orally","giving more multiple-choice tests","banning all computers","allowing AI only at home"]],["What is the writer’s main point?",["How students use AI matters more than whether they use it.","AI should be banned in all schools.","AI is always confidently right.","AI answers are always reliable."]],["Why does the writer mention that AI answers are “often confidently wrong”?",["to show why students must evaluate AI critically","to prove that AI is useless","to encourage students to trust AI","to criticize teachers"]],["What does “productive struggle” mean in the text?",["useful effort that helps you learn","a fight between students","work that produces money","a problem with no solution"]]]},{"t":"Living with Climate Anxiety","p":["In a large international survey published in 2021, researchers asked ten thousand young people aged sixteen to twenty-five in ten countries how they felt about climate change. Nearly sixty percent said they were very or extremely worried, and many reported feeling sad, anxious, angry or powerless. Psychologists now often use the term “eco-anxiety” to describe this experience.","It’s important to understand that eco-anxiety is not a mental illness. In many ways, it is a rational response to a real threat. Feeling concerned about the future shows that you care about the world and the people in it.","However, when anxiety becomes overwhelming, it can lead to paralysis, the feeling that the problem is so enormous that nothing we do matters. This is where psychologists recommend a shift in focus.","One of the most effective antidotes to eco-anxiety is action, especially collective action. Joining a group that plants trees, reduces waste at school or raises awareness turns abstract fear into concrete purpose. Connecting with others who share your concerns also reduces the sense of isolation.","Balance matters, too. Staying informed is valuable, but constantly consuming alarming news can intensify anxiety. Setting limits on news consumption and spending time in nature can help.","As one climate scientist put it, hope is not a feeling you wait for; it is something you create by doing."],"q":[["What did nearly sixty percent of young people in the survey say?",["They were very or extremely worried.","They didn’t care.","They felt optimistic.","They were slightly bored by the topic."]],["How does the text describe eco-anxiety?",["a rational response to a real threat","a serious mental illness","an exaggeration","a temporary trend on social media"]],["What can happen when anxiety becomes overwhelming?",["paralysis","more motivation","better sleep","faster decisions"]],["What is one of the most effective antidotes?",["collective action","ignoring the news completely","moving to another country","reading more alarming news"]],["What does the final sentence suggest about hope?",["It is created through action.","It arrives on its own.","It is not realistic.","It is only for scientists."]],["What is the writer’s purpose in this text?",["to validate young people’s worry and suggest healthy ways to cope","to prove that climate change is exaggerated","to criticize young people for being anxious","to sell environmental products"]],["What does “antidote” mean in “the most effective antidotes to eco-anxiety”?",["something that counteracts a harmful effect","a type of illness","a scientific survey","a feeling of fear"]]]},{"t":"The Summer Monterrey Ran Dry","p":["In the summer of 2022, millions of people in Monterrey turned on their taps and nothing came out. After years of below-average rainfall, the reservoirs that supply the metropolitan area had dropped to critically low levels. One of them, La Boca dam, was almost completely dry, its cracked bottom becoming a symbol of the crisis.","The authorities introduced strict rationing. In many neighborhoods, water was available for only a few hours a day, sometimes in the early morning. Families filled buckets, bottles and large tanks called tinacos whenever they could. Long lines formed around water trucks, and the price of bottled water rose. Schools adjusted schedules, and some businesses had to reduce operations.","Experts pointed out that the drought was only part of the story. Monterrey’s population and industry had grown rapidly, increasing demand, while infrastructure had not kept pace. Leaks in the distribution network also meant that a significant percentage of water was lost before it reached homes.","In the autumn, heavy rains finally refilled the reservoirs and ended the most acute phase of the crisis. The government accelerated new projects, including an aqueduct to bring water from another river basin.","Yet for many residents, the experience left a lasting lesson: water security cannot be taken for granted. Collecting rainwater, fixing leaks and reducing consumption are habits that matter long after the taps start running again."],"q":[["What caused the reservoirs to drop?",["years of below-average rainfall","a broken dam","an earthquake","a sudden drop in temperature"]],["What became a symbol of the crisis?",["the cracked bottom of La Boca dam","long lines at schools","bottled water","the price of tinacos"]],["Besides drought, what contributed to the problem?",["growing demand and leaks in the network","too much rain","lower population","fewer factories in the city"]],["What ended the most acute phase of the crisis?",["heavy rains in the autumn","a new aqueduct","water trucks","a ban on watering gardens"]],["What lesson does the text highlight?",["Water security cannot be taken for granted.","Droughts never happen twice.","Bottled water solves the problem.","Rain always solves water problems."]],["Why does the writer say the drought was “only part of the story”?",["Human factors like demand and leaks also caused the crisis.","There was no drought at all.","The crisis happened in another city.","The government invented the crisis."]],["What does “taken for granted” mean in “cannot be taken for granted”?",["assumed to always be available","shared with others","bought cheaply","measured carefully"]]]},{"t":"The Science of First Impressions","p":["How long does it take to form an opinion about someone you have just met? According to research by psychologists at Princeton University, people make judgments about a stranger’s trustworthiness and competence after seeing their face for just one tenth of a second. Longer exposure didn’t change their opinions much; it mostly made them more confident.","These rapid judgments probably evolved for a reason. For our ancestors, quickly deciding whether a stranger was a friend or a threat could be a matter of survival. Today, however, these instant impressions influence decisions in job interviews, classrooms and even elections, often without our awareness.","The trouble is that first impressions are frequently wrong. They are shaped by stereotypes, by how similar someone seems to us and by superficial details such as clothing or facial expression in a single moment. A nervous person may seem cold; a confident person may seem competent even when they aren’t.","Another problem is that first impressions tend to persist. Once we have formed an opinion, we unconsciously look for information that confirms it and ignore information that contradicts it. Psychologists call this confirmation bias.","So what can we do? Simply being aware of the bias helps. Giving people a second, third and fourth chance, and actively looking for evidence that challenges our initial assumptions, makes our judgments fairer. After all, the person you misjudged on the first day of school might become your closest friend."],"q":[["How quickly do people form judgments about faces?",["in one tenth of a second","in about ten seconds","after several minutes","after one full day"]],["What did longer exposure mainly do?",["It made people more confident.","It changed opinions completely.","It made people less sure.","It made people forget the face."]],["Why did rapid judgments probably evolve?",["to quickly identify friends or threats","to choose good food","to win elections","to recognize family members by voice"]],["What is confirmation bias?",["looking for information that supports our opinion","changing our mind easily","trusting strangers","trusting only experts"]],["What does the text recommend?",["giving people more chances and challenging our assumptions","trusting first impressions completely","avoiding new people","making decisions as fast as possible"]],["Why does the writer end with the example of a misjudged classmate?",["to show the personal cost of trusting first impressions","to give advice about the first day of school","to prove first impressions are always right","to tell a story about the writer’s best friend"]],["What does “persist” mean in “first impressions tend to persist”?",["continue to exist","disappear quickly","become positive","change often"]]]},{"t":"Should You Take a Gap Year?","p":["In countries like the United Kingdom, Australia and Denmark, it is common for students to take a “gap year” between finishing high school and starting university. During this time, they may work, travel, volunteer, learn a language or simply reflect on what they want to study. In Mexico, the idea is less common, but it is gaining attention.","Supporters argue that a gap year can provide clarity. Many students choose a university degree at seventeen or eighteen without really knowing what interests them, and a significant number later change programs or drop out. Time spent working or volunteering can help young people discover their strengths and choose more carefully. Some studies have found that students who took a structured gap year arrived at university more motivated and performed as well as or better than their peers.","Critics, however, point to real risks. Without a clear plan, a gap year can easily become a year of scrolling on the sofa. Some students lose academic momentum and find it difficult to return to studying. There are also financial considerations: travel programs can be expensive, and not every family can support a year without studies.","The key, most experts agree, is structure. A successful gap year has goals, whether that means saving money, gaining work experience, improving a language or completing a specific project.","Ultimately, there is no universal right answer. The more useful question may be: what do you want to have learned about yourself by the time you start the next chapter?"],"q":[["In which countries is a gap year common?",["the UK, Australia and Denmark","Mexico and Brazil","China and Japan","the United States and Canada"]],["What benefit do supporters mention?",["It can help students choose more carefully.","It guarantees a good job.","It makes university shorter.","It helps students finish university faster."]],["What is one risk critics mention?",["Students may lose academic momentum.","Students learn too much.","Universities don’t accept them.","Universities give fewer scholarships."]],["What do most experts say is the key to a successful gap year?",["structure and clear goals","travelling as far as possible","spending a lot of money","traveling without any plan"]],["What is the writer’s final position?",["There is no universal right answer.","Everyone should take a gap year.","Gap years are always a mistake.","A gap year is right for everyone."]],["What tone does the writer use toward gap years?",["balanced, presenting benefits and risks","strongly enthusiastic","completely negative","humorous and sarcastic"]],["What does “momentum” mean in “lose academic momentum”?",["the energy to keep going","good grades","a university program","free time"]]]}],"teens|C1":[{"t":"The Paradox of Choice","p":["Conventional wisdom holds that freedom is maximized by choice: the more options we have, the more likely we are to find exactly what we want. Supermarkets offering forty varieties of yogurt and streaming platforms with thousands of titles appear to be the logical endpoint of this belief.","Yet a growing body of research suggests that, beyond a certain point, additional options do not liberate us so much as paralyze us. In one frequently cited experiment, shoppers who were offered twenty-four types of jam were far less likely to make a purchase than those presented with only six.","The study, conducted by the psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper and published in 2000, was carried out in an upscale grocery store in California. On some days, a tasting table displayed twenty-four varieties of jam; on others, only six. The larger display attracted more curious visitors, which seemed to confirm the intuition that people are drawn to abundance. Yet when it came to actually buying, the pattern reversed dramatically: only about three percent of those who stopped at the extensive display made a purchase, compared with roughly thirty percent of those who saw the limited one. Variety, it appeared, was appealing to look at but exhausting to act upon.","The psychologist Barry Schwartz popularized the term “the paradox of choice” to describe this phenomenon. He distinguishes between “maximizers,” who feel compelled to examine every alternative in search of the best possible option, and “satisficers,” who settle on the first option that meets their criteria. Paradoxically, maximizers often make objectively better decisions yet report feeling less satisfied with them, haunted by the possibility that something better was available.","Why should more options produce less satisfaction? Several mechanisms seem to be at work. The first is the sheer cognitive effort involved in comparing alternatives: every additional option multiplies the number of trade-offs that must be weighed. The second is what economists call opportunity cost. When we choose one option, we implicitly give up the attractive features of all the others, and the more alternatives there are, the more acutely we may feel those losses. The third is a shift in responsibility. When choices are scarce, a disappointing outcome can be blamed on circumstances; when they are plentiful, a poor result feels like a personal failure. As expectations rise with the number of options, even a good outcome may feel like a missed opportunity.","The implications extend well beyond shopping. Students facing an overwhelming range of university courses, or professionals weighing countless career paths, may experience the same mixture of anxiety and regret.","Later research has complicated the picture somewhat. Some attempts to replicate the original findings produced weaker effects, and researchers now argue that choice overload depends on context: it is most likely when options are difficult to compare, when people do not have clear preferences, or when they feel pressure to make the right decision quickly. In other situations, especially when people already know what they want, a wide selection can be genuinely helpful. The paradox, in other words, is not a universal law but a tendency that emerges under particular conditions.","None of this implies that choice is undesirable; rather, it suggests that well-being may depend less on the number of options available than on our ability to decide when enough is genuinely enough.","For individuals, the practical lessons are modest but useful: decide in advance what really matters, limit the number of options you are willing to consider, and accept a choice that is good enough rather than torturing yourself in pursuit of the perfect one. Some people find it helpful to treat certain decisions as irreversible, which, research suggests, can make them more satisfied with what they chose."],"q":[["What belief is described in the first paragraph?",["More choice leads to more freedom and satisfaction.","Supermarkets have too few products.","Streaming platforms are expensive.","Too many choices are illegal."]],["What did the jam experiment show?",["Fewer options led to more purchases.","People prefer more types of jam.","Most shoppers ignored the tasting table.","Shoppers bought more with twenty-four types."]],["How are “satisficers” different from “maximizers”?",["They accept the first option that meets their needs.","They always search for the best possible option.","They never make decisions.","They never compare products."]],["Why are maximizers often less satisfied?",["They keep wondering if a better option existed.","They always make bad decisions.","They have fewer options.","They take too long to buy anything."]],["What is the author’s conclusion?",["Well-being depends on knowing when enough is enough.","Choice should be eliminated.","More options always lead to happiness.","People should always maximize."]],["Why does the writer mention university courses and career paths?",["to show the paradox applies beyond shopping","to recommend specific careers","to criticize universities","to explain how to choose jam"]],["What does “paralyze” mean in “additional options… paralyze us”?",["make us unable to act","make us happier","help us decide","make us spend more"]],["According to later research, when is choice overload most likely?",["when options are hard to compare and preferences are unclear","whenever there are more than six options","only in grocery stores","when people already know what they want"]]]},{"t":"The Attention Economy","p":["Most of the apps on your phone cost nothing to download. Social networks, video platforms and many games are free at the point of use. Yet several of the companies behind them are among the most valuable in the world. How is that possible? The answer lies in what economists and technologists call the attention economy.","In this model, the product being sold is not the app itself but the user’s attention, which is packaged and sold to advertisers. The longer you stay on a platform, the more advertisements you see and the more data you generate about your preferences, which in turn makes the advertising more precisely targeted and more profitable. Attention, in other words, has become a commodity.","The idea is older than the internet. In 1971, the economist and psychologist Herbert Simon observed that a wealth of information creates a poverty of attention. When information is scarce, the challenge is to find it; when it is abundant, the challenge becomes deciding where to look. Television and newspapers had long financed themselves through advertising, but digital platforms transformed the model in two crucial ways: they can measure attention with extraordinary precision, and they can adjust their content for each individual user in real time.","This creates a powerful incentive for designers to make their products as engaging as possible. Features such as infinite scroll, autoplay, streaks and unpredictable notifications are not accidental. Some draw on the same psychological principle as slot machines: variable rewards. Because we never know whether the next swipe will bring something exciting, we keep swiping.","Recommendation algorithms play a central role in this process. Every pause, like, share and replay becomes a signal that helps the system predict what will keep you engaged. Over time, the feed learns your preferences, sometimes better than you could describe them yourself. This can be genuinely useful when it helps you discover music or ideas you enjoy. But because these systems are typically optimized for engagement rather than for accuracy or wellbeing, they may also favor content that provokes strong emotions, such as outrage, fear or envy, simply because such content tends to hold attention.","Critics argue that this business model sets up a fundamental conflict of interest. What is good for a company’s revenue, maximum time spent, is not necessarily what is good for users’ wellbeing, sleep or ability to concentrate. Several former tech employees have publicly expressed regret about the techniques they helped develop.","Defenders respond that users are not passive victims; they choose what to consume, and these platforms provide genuine value, from connection to creativity to information.","Some governments and organizations have begun to respond. Proposals include restricting certain design features for younger users, giving people more control over recommendation algorithms and requiring platforms to be more transparent about how their systems work. Some designers have also called for “humane technology”: products built to respect users’ time rather than maximize it. Whether such efforts will succeed against powerful commercial incentives remains an open question.","Perhaps the most empowering response lies between these positions: understanding how these systems work. Once you recognize a design feature intended to capture your attention, you are in a far better position to decide whether to give it."],"q":[["In the attention economy, what is actually being sold?",["users’ attention","the apps","phones","users’ passwords"]],["Why does more time on a platform increase profit?",["Users see more ads and generate more data.","Users pay per minute.","Apps become more expensive.","Companies charge higher prices for apps."]],["What principle do some features share with slot machines?",["variable rewards","fixed prices","clear rules","clear instructions"]],["What conflict of interest do critics identify?",["Company revenue vs. user wellbeing","Advertisers vs. governments","Designers vs. engineers","Users vs. advertisers"]],["What does the writer suggest is the most empowering response?",["understanding how these systems work","deleting all apps","trusting the companies","ignoring all notifications"]],["Why does the writer mention former tech employees who regret their work?",["to support the critics’ argument with insider views","to show that tech jobs are badly paid","to prove that apps are illegal","to praise their creativity"]],["What does “commodity” mean in “Attention… has become a commodity”?",["something that can be bought and sold","a free gift","a kind of app","a personal feeling"]],["Why might engagement-based algorithms favor content that provokes outrage or fear?",["Such content tends to hold attention.","It is more accurate.","Users ask for it directly.","It is cheaper to produce."]]]},{"t":"Should Zoos Still Exist?","p":["For centuries, zoos were essentially displays of power and curiosity, places where exotic animals were exhibited for public entertainment, often in small, barren cages. Today, many leading zoos describe their mission in very different terms: conservation, research and education. Yet the question of whether keeping wild animals in captivity can be justified remains fiercely debated.","The transformation has been gradual and uneven. In the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, many zoos competed to display the largest number of exotic species, and animals were often captured from the wild, transported in terrible conditions and kept in enclosures designed for the convenience of visitors rather than the needs of the animals. Over the course of the twentieth century, growing scientific understanding of animal behavior, along with public pressure, pushed many institutions to create larger, more naturalistic enclosures and to rely on breeding rather than capture.","Supporters point to concrete successes. Captive breeding programs have played a crucial role in saving species that had virtually disappeared from the wild. The Arabian oryx, for instance, was declared extinct in the wild in the early 1970s; animals bred in zoos were later reintroduced to the Middle East. Similarly, when the population of California condors fell to just twenty-two birds in the 1980s, the remaining individuals were captured, and a breeding program gradually brought the species back from the brink.","Zoos also fund field research and claim to inspire a sense of connection with nature, particularly among children who will never see a tiger or an elephant in its natural habitat.","Supporters also emphasize the role of zoos as scientific institutions. Researchers working with animals in human care have learned a great deal about reproduction, nutrition, disease and behavior, knowledge that can be applied to wild populations. Veterinary techniques developed in zoos have, for example, been used to treat endangered animals in the field. Many accredited zoos also contribute part of their income to conservation projects in the animals’ native habitats, protecting forests, wetlands and grasslands that benefit thousands of species that will never be displayed.","Critics counter that these successes involve a small fraction of the animals actually held in zoos, many of which belong to species that are not endangered at all. They argue that certain animals, particularly large, intelligent, wide-ranging species such as elephants and orcas, suffer psychologically in captivity, sometimes exhibiting repetitive behaviors associated with stress. Moreover, they question whether watching a confined animal genuinely teaches visitors much about its life in the wild.","There are also wider ethical objections. Some philosophers argue that, regardless of the benefits, it is wrong in principle to confine an animal for human purposes, much as we would consider it wrong to confine a person who had committed no crime. Others point out that zoo quality varies enormously. While a small number of institutions meet high standards of care, many smaller roadside zoos and private collections around the world operate with little regulation, and it is in these places that animal suffering is often most severe.","Between these positions, a middle ground is emerging: sanctuaries that prioritize animal welfare over display, stricter regulation, and a focus on protecting habitats rather than individual animals. The question may no longer be whether zoos should exist, but what kind of institutions they need to become."],"q":[["How do leading zoos describe their modern mission?",["conservation, research and education","entertainment only","selling animals","research on animal intelligence only"]],["What happened to the Arabian oryx?",["Zoo-bred animals were reintroduced to the wild.","It became extinct forever.","It was never endangered.","It was moved to Europe permanently."]],["How many California condors remained in the 1980s?",["twenty-two","two hundred","two thousand","two"]],["What is one argument critics make?",["Successes involve only a small fraction of zoo animals.","Zoos are too cheap.","Zoos don’t have enough visitors.","Zoos teach too much science."]],["What does the writer suggest at the end?",["The key question is what kind of institutions zoos should become.","All zoos should close immediately.","Zoos need more animals.","Zoos should replace natural habitats."]],["How does the writer organize the text?",["history, arguments for, arguments against, then a middle ground","only arguments in favor of zoos","a personal story about a zoo visit","a list of the best zoos in the world"]],["What does “brink” mean in “back from the brink”?",["the edge of disaster","a safe place","a zoo enclosure","the beginning of life"]],["Where, according to critics, is animal suffering often most severe?",["in poorly regulated roadside zoos and private collections","in large accredited zoos","in wildlife sanctuaries","in national parks"]]]},{"t":"The Myth of Multitasking","p":["Many people consider themselves skilled multitaskers. They answer messages during lectures, keep several tabs open while writing an essay and listen to podcasts while doing homework. It feels efficient. However, decades of research in cognitive psychology suggest that, for tasks that require conscious attention, true multitasking is largely an illusion.","The phenomenon is not limited to students. Office workers often juggle emails, messages and meetings, sometimes checking notifications dozens of times an hour. Drivers talk on the phone while navigating traffic. In each case, the person typically believes that the second activity has little effect on the first. The research suggests otherwise.","What we perceive as doing two things at once is usually rapid task switching. The brain shifts its focus back and forth between activities, and each switch carries a cost. Researchers have repeatedly found that switching between tasks takes extra time and increases errors, even when each switch lasts only a fraction of a second. Some estimates suggest that frequent switching can significantly reduce overall productivity.","Part of the explanation lies in the structure of attention itself. Working memory, the mental space we use to hold and manipulate information, has a limited capacity. When we switch from one task to another, the brain must disengage from the rules and goals of the first task and load those of the second. Psychologists sometimes describe a lingering effect called “attention residue”: even after we have moved on to a new task, part of our mind remains occupied with the previous one, particularly if it was left unfinished. The more often we switch, the more residue accumulates.","There is also evidence that habitual multitasking may affect how we process information. In a frequently cited 2009 study at Stanford University, researchers compared people who regularly consumed several forms of media simultaneously with those who rarely did. Surprisingly, the heavy multitaskers performed worse on tests that measured their ability to filter out irrelevant information and switch between tasks, precisely the skills they were assumed to have practiced.","This does not mean that we can never combine activities. Tasks that are highly automatic, such as walking, can be paired with conversation without much difficulty. The problem arises when two tasks compete for the same mental resources, such as reading and listening to speech.","Driving offers a sobering illustration. Studies using driving simulators have found that people talking on the phone, even using a hands-free device, react more slowly to hazards and miss more of what happens around them. The problem is not where their hands are but where their attention is. Many countries have introduced laws restricting phone use while driving for precisely this reason.","The practical implications are clear. Working in focused blocks, silencing notifications and completing one task before beginning another may feel slower, but the evidence suggests it is often faster, and the quality of the work tends to be higher.","In an age of constant distraction, the ability to concentrate on one thing at a time may be one of the most valuable skills a person can develop."],"q":[["What does research suggest about true multitasking?",["It is largely an illusion for attention-demanding tasks.","It is the most efficient way to work.","Only teenagers can do it.","It works only for simple tasks."]],["What usually happens instead of real multitasking?",["rapid task switching","deep focus","automatic thinking","sleeping"]],["What did the Stanford study find about heavy multitaskers?",["They were worse at filtering irrelevant information.","They were better at everything.","They were faster readers.","They were more creative."]],["When is combining tasks less of a problem?",["when one task is highly automatic","when both tasks involve reading","when you are very tired","when both tasks require listening"]],["What practical advice does the text give?",["Work in focused blocks and silence notifications.","Open more tabs.","Always listen to podcasts while studying.","Do several tasks at once to save time."]],["Why were the Stanford results described as “surprising”?",["Heavy multitaskers were worse at skills they were assumed to practice.","Nobody had studied multitasking before.","Only students took part.","The study was very short."]],["What does “illusion” mean in “true multitasking is largely an illusion”?",["something that seems real but is not","a useful skill","a scientific law","a kind of game"]],["What is “attention residue”?",["part of the mind staying on a previous task after switching","a type of distraction caused by noise","the ability to do two tasks perfectly","memory loss caused by stress"]]]},{"t":"When a Language Disappears","p":["Mexico is one of the most linguistically diverse countries in the world. In addition to Spanish, the government recognizes sixty-eight Indigenous languages, which together include hundreds of distinct variants. Náhuatl, spoken by well over a million people, is the most widely used; others, such as Ayapaneco in Tabasco, have only a handful of fluent speakers left.","This diversity is the product of thousands of years of history. Long before the arrival of the Spanish, the territory that is now Mexico was home to a remarkable variety of peoples and languages, belonging to several unrelated language families. Some, like the Mayan languages, have written records that go back nearly two thousand years. Others were transmitted orally from generation to generation, carrying with them myths, medical knowledge and detailed understanding of local ecosystems.","Linguists estimate that around half of the world’s roughly seven thousand languages could disappear by the end of this century. When a language dies, the loss extends far beyond vocabulary and grammar. Languages encode ways of perceiving the world: systems for classifying plants and animals, concepts of time and kinship, oral histories, songs and jokes that may not exist anywhere else. Much traditional ecological knowledge, for instance, is preserved in the names and descriptions communities have given to their local environment.","Consider a simple example. Many Indigenous languages contain precise words for varieties of corn, medicinal plants or types of soil that have no equivalent in Spanish or English. These words are not merely labels; they reflect careful observation accumulated over centuries. When the last speakers of a language die, this knowledge may disappear with them, often before anyone has had the chance to record it. Linguists sometimes compare the situation to the burning of a library whose books were never copied.","Languages rarely disappear because speakers simply stop valuing them. More often, the process is driven by economic pressure, migration and, historically, discrimination. For generations, many Indigenous children in Mexico were punished at school for speaking their mother tongue, and parents sometimes chose not to pass their language on, hoping to spare their children the same prejudice.","In recent decades, however, attitudes have begun to shift. Bilingual education programs, Indigenous-language radio stations, and young artists who rap, write poetry and create online content in Zapotec, Mixe or Náhuatl are demonstrating that these languages are not relics of the past but living vehicles of contemporary expression.","Technology has created new opportunities. Online dictionaries, mobile apps, podcasts and social media accounts now allow speakers to share their languages with audiences far beyond their communities. Young people who once felt that their heritage language had no place in modern life are discovering that it can be used to post, joke, sing and argue just like any other. Mexico’s legal framework has also changed: a law passed in 2003 recognized Indigenous languages as national languages, with the same validity as Spanish, at least in principle.","Revitalization is difficult, but not impossible; the revival of Hebrew and the resurgence of Welsh show what sustained community effort can achieve. Ultimately, the survival of a language depends on whether new generations see it not as a burden, but as a source of identity and pride."],"q":[["How many Indigenous languages does the Mexican government recognize?",["sixty-eight","seven thousand","twelve","one hundred and twenty"]],["What is lost when a language dies, according to the text?",["ways of perceiving the world, histories and knowledge","only vocabulary","only grammar","only songs"]],["Why did some parents not pass on their language?",["to spare their children prejudice","because it was too difficult","because schools required it","because the language had no writing"]],["What is helping to change attitudes?",["bilingual education, radio and young artists","banning Spanish","closing rural schools","new laws against Spanish"]],["What do the examples of Hebrew and Welsh show?",["Revitalization is possible with sustained effort.","All languages will disappear.","Only European languages survive.","Languages can’t change."]],["What does the writer imply about language loss in Mexico?",["It was largely caused by social pressure, not by lack of value.","Speakers chose to forget their languages for no reason.","It is a natural process that cannot be stopped.","It only affects very old people."]],["What does “relics” mean in “not relics of the past”?",["things that survive from an earlier time","modern inventions","popular songs","school subjects"]],["Why do linguists compare language loss to burning a library?",["Knowledge stored in the language may disappear before it is recorded.","Old books are written in Indigenous languages.","Libraries do not contain Indigenous books.","Languages are usually written on paper."]]]},{"t":"Science Without a Laboratory","p":["We often picture science as something that happens exclusively in laboratories, conducted by experts in white coats. Increasingly, however, ordinary people around the world are contributing to genuine research, often from their phones or computers. This phenomenon is known as citizen science.","The idea is not entirely new. For more than a century, volunteers in North America have participated in the Christmas Bird Count, an annual survey in which ordinary people record the birds they see in their area. Amateur astronomers have long contributed to the discovery of comets and variable stars. What has changed is the scale. The internet and smartphones have made it possible for millions of people to participate, often from home, and for scientists to collect and analyze their contributions efficiently.","One of the best-known examples began in 2007, when a team of astronomers launched Galaxy Zoo. They had images of around a million galaxies, far too many for professionals to classify on their own. They invited the public to help by identifying the shapes of galaxies online. The response was overwhelming: within the first year, volunteers had made tens of millions of classifications. Along the way, a Dutch schoolteacher spotted a mysterious green object that astronomers had never seen before. It was later named Hanny’s Voorwerp, after her.","In 2011, players of an online puzzle game called Foldit, most of them without scientific training, worked out the structure of a protein related to a virus that causes an AIDS-like disease in monkeys. Scientists had struggled with the problem for over a decade; the gamers solved it in about three weeks.","Citizen science is equally valuable for studying the natural world. Platforms where people upload photos of birds, insects and plants have produced vast databases that help researchers track migration patterns, the spread of invasive species and the effects of climate change across entire continents, something no single research team could achieve.","In Mexico, such platforms are also widely used. Thousands of people contribute observations of birds, butterflies, reptiles and plants, and these records have helped researchers document the distribution of species across the country, including some that had rarely been recorded. Students participating in school projects have occasionally made notable sightings simply by photographing what they found in a park or garden.","Beyond the data, these projects have another benefit: they make science more democratic. Participants gain a deeper understanding of how knowledge is produced, and many discover a passion that shapes their education and careers.","Citizen science is not without its challenges. Volunteers may misidentify species, and some regions or groups are better represented than others, which can distort the data. To address these problems, many projects combine contributions from many people, so that errors can be detected, and invite experts to review uncertain observations. Researchers have found that, when projects are well designed, data collected by volunteers can be remarkably reliable. Some projects even train their volunteers through short tutorials and quizzes before they begin, improving the quality of their contributions.","So the next time you photograph an unusual insect in your garden, consider that you might be contributing to science."],"q":[["Why did astronomers launch Galaxy Zoo?",["They had too many galaxy images to classify alone.","They needed funding.","They wanted to sell telescopes.","They wanted to test a new website."]],["Who discovered Hanny’s Voorwerp?",["a Dutch schoolteacher","a team of astronomers","a computer program","a famous astronomer"]],["What did Foldit players achieve?",["They solved a protein structure in about three weeks.","They discovered a new planet.","They created a vaccine.","They designed a new game console."]],["How do photo platforms help researchers?",["They help track migration, invasive species and climate effects.","They replace all scientists.","They sell nature photos.","They make scientists unnecessary."]],["What additional benefit of citizen science is mentioned?",["It makes science more democratic.","It is always more accurate.","It is cheaper for universities.","It makes science faster than laboratories."]],["What is the writer’s purpose in describing Galaxy Zoo and Foldit?",["to show that non-experts can make real scientific contributions","to criticize professional scientists","to advertise video games","to explain how telescopes work"]],["What does “democratic” mean in “they make science more democratic”?",["open to everyone, not only experts","controlled by the government","more expensive","more complicated"]],["How do many projects deal with mistakes made by volunteers?",["They combine many contributions and ask experts to review uncertain ones.","They only accept data from professionals.","They ignore all volunteer data.","They pay volunteers to be more careful."]]]}],"teens|C2":[{"t":"In Praise of Boredom","p":["Of all the minor afflictions of modern life, boredom is perhaps the one we are most determined to eradicate. The moment a queue slows or a conversation falters, a hand reaches instinctively for a screen, as though a few seconds of unoccupied attention were intolerable.","The impulse is understandable. Boredom is, after all, unpleasant by definition: a restless awareness that time is passing without anything to occupy it. For most of human history, however, such intervals were unavoidable. People waited for harvests, for letters, for the long evenings to end. What distinguishes the present is not that we experience boredom less often, but that we have acquired, for the first time, an almost inexhaustible means of interrupting it the instant it appears.","It was not always regarded with such hostility. Philosophers from Pascal to Kierkegaard treated boredom as a revealing, if uncomfortable, condition: a moment in which the mind, deprived of external stimulation, is thrown back upon itself. Pascal went so far as to claim that most of humanity’s problems stem from our inability to sit quietly in a room alone.","The word itself is surprisingly recent. In English, “boredom” became common only in the nineteenth century, around the same period in which industrial work and urban life began to divide time into strictly regulated units. Some historians have suggested that boredom, as we understand it, is partly a product of modernity: a by-product of repetitive labor on the one hand and of rising expectations of stimulation on the other. Whether or not this account is entirely accurate, it reminds us that our attitudes toward empty time are shaped by culture as much as by biology.","Contemporary psychology lends a degree of empirical weight to these intuitions. Studies suggest that periods of mild boredom can foster divergent thinking, prompting the mind to wander, make unexpected connections and, on occasion, arrive at genuinely original ideas. Boredom, in this reading, is less a void than a signal: an indication that our current activity lacks meaning and that something else deserves our attention.","In one frequently cited series of experiments, participants who first completed a deliberately tedious task, such as copying numbers from a telephone directory, subsequently generated more creative ideas than those who had not been bored. Researchers have proposed that the mind, deprived of engaging input, begins to search inward and outward for stimulation, loosening its habitual associations in the process. Many writers, scientists and artists have described their most fertile ideas as arriving not during concentrated work but during idle moments: a walk, a shower, a long journey with nothing to read.","The danger of perpetual distraction, then, is not merely that it wastes time, but that it silences that signal before it can be heard. By filling every interval with frictionless entertainment, we may be depriving ourselves of the very discomfort that drives reflection and, ultimately, change.","There is also a quieter cost. Moments of unstructured attention have traditionally been when people reflected on their own lives: when they replayed a conversation, reconsidered a decision or noticed, perhaps reluctantly, a dissatisfaction they had been avoiding. If every pause is immediately filled, those reflections may never take place. The result is not necessarily unhappiness, but a kind of drift, in which life is continuously occupied yet rarely examined. In a culture that measures the value of time by its productivity or entertainment, an hour spent staring out of a window can seem indefensible. Yet such hours may be precisely where we discover what we actually want.","None of this requires a dramatic renunciation of technology. Experiments in which people are asked to leave their phones in another room, to take a walk without headphones or simply to wait without reaching for a screen are often described, at first, as mildly uncomfortable and, later, as surprisingly refreshing. The discomfort, it seems, is the point: it is the brief friction through which the mind regains access to itself.","To defend boredom is not to romanticize tedium. It is, rather, to suggest that a mind never permitted to idle may gradually lose the capacity to decide, for itself, what is worth doing."],"q":[["How does the writer describe our modern attitude toward boredom?",["We try to eliminate it immediately.","We enjoy it as much as possible.","We have never experienced it.","We study it carefully."]],["What did Pascal claim?",["Many problems come from our inability to sit quietly alone.","Boredom should be avoided at all costs.","People need more entertainment.","Boredom is the worst human problem."]],["According to the third paragraph, what can mild boredom encourage?",["creative and original thinking","anxiety and fear","better memory for facts","faster reactions"]],["What is the main danger of perpetual distraction?",["It silences a signal that leads to reflection and change.","It makes people physically tired.","It is too expensive.","It makes conversations shorter."]],["Which statement best reflects the conclusion?",["Allowing the mind to idle helps us decide what is worth doing.","Boredom is always pleasant.","Entertainment should be banned.","Tedium should be celebrated."]],["What is the writer’s tone toward constant distraction?",["critical and reflective","enthusiastic","neutral and technical","humorous"]],["What does “eradicate” mean in “determined to eradicate”?",["eliminate completely","understand","enjoy","measure"]],["What do the experiments with tedious tasks suggest?",["Being bored first can lead to more creative ideas.","Boring tasks make people less intelligent.","Copying numbers improves memory.","Creative people never get bored."]]]},{"t":"The Uses of Failure","p":["Contemporary culture has a curious relationship with failure. On the one hand, it is endlessly romanticized: entrepreneurs boast of their early bankruptcies, and motivational slogans exhort us to “fail fast.” On the other, the actual experience of failing, a rejected application, a lost competition, a disappointing grade, remains something most of us go to considerable lengths to conceal. We admire failure retrospectively, once it has been redeemed by eventual success, but we rarely tolerate it in the present tense.","This ambivalence obscures what failure genuinely offers. Most obviously, it provides information. A plan that collapses reveals assumptions that were mistaken; a test we fail exposes the precise boundaries of our understanding. Success, by contrast, is often epistemically lazy: when things go well, we seldom examine why, and so we may attribute to skill what was partly due to luck.","Consider how scientific knowledge advances. A hypothesis that survives every test tells us relatively little; one that fails tells us exactly where our model of the world breaks down. The philosopher Karl Popper argued that the defining feature of a scientific theory is not that it can be proven, but that it can, in principle, be shown to be false. Experiments that fail are therefore not embarrassments to be hidden but essential contributions to understanding. The same logic applies, in a more modest way, to individual learning. Educational researchers have found that students who attempt difficult problems before being taught how to solve them, and who initially fail, often understand the material more deeply afterward than students who are simply shown the correct method. The struggle, it seems, prepares the mind to recognize what matters in the solution.","Failure can also cultivate qualities that uninterrupted success cannot. Psychologists who study resilience have observed that people who encounter manageable setbacks often develop greater emotional flexibility than those who have faced either no adversity or overwhelming adversity. Struggle, in moderate doses, seems to build the capacity to struggle.","Much depends, however, on how failure is interpreted. Researchers studying motivation have distinguished between people who regard ability as fixed and those who regard it as something that can be developed through effort and strategy. For the former, a failure is a verdict: evidence of a permanent limitation. For the latter, it is information: evidence that a different approach is needed. The same event can thus demoralize one person and motivate another. Although some of the more ambitious claims made for teaching this second outlook have proven difficult to replicate, the underlying insight remains plausible: it is not failure itself but the story we tell about it that shapes what happens next.","Yet we should resist the temptation to sentimentalize. Not every failure is instructive; some are simply painful, and the lessons extracted from them are sometimes rationalizations constructed after the fact. Moreover, the freedom to fail is unevenly distributed. A wealthy student can afford to experiment; for someone whose opportunities are scarce, a single failure may carry irreversible consequences. A culture that genuinely valued failure would therefore need to build safety nets, not merely slogans.","Schools and institutions face a related dilemma. Systems that penalize every mistake heavily encourage caution rather than experimentation; students learn to choose safe topics, avoid difficult courses and conceal confusion. Some educators have therefore introduced opportunities to revise work, ungraded drafts and assessments that reward improvement, attempting to create spaces in which failure is survivable. Such measures do not eliminate the pain of falling short, but they change its consequences.","Samuel Beckett’s much-quoted line, “Fail again. Fail better,” is often read as a cheerful mantra of perseverance. In its original context, it is considerably bleaker and more honest: an acknowledgment that failure is not a detour on the road to success, but a permanent condition of any serious attempt. The question, perhaps, is not how to avoid failure, but how to fail in ways that teach us something."],"q":[["What contradiction does the writer identify in contemporary culture?",["Failure is romanticized but concealed in real life.","Success is always criticized.","Failure is openly celebrated in the present.","Failure is ignored in every situation."]],["Why does the writer call success “epistemically lazy”?",["We rarely examine why things went well.","Successful people stop working.","Success makes us forget facts.","Successful people stop learning facts."]],["What do resilience researchers observe?",["Manageable setbacks can build emotional flexibility.","Any adversity is harmful.","Overwhelming adversity is best.","Avoiding all difficulties is healthiest."]],["What does the writer say about the freedom to fail?",["It is unevenly distributed.","Everyone has it equally.","It only matters for adults.","It depends only on age."]],["How does the writer interpret Beckett’s line?",["as an honest acknowledgment that failure is permanent in any serious attempt","as a cheerful motivational slogan","as advice to give up","as a reason to stop trying"]],["Why does the writer warn against “sentimentalizing” failure?",["Not every failure teaches something, and some carry serious consequences.","Failure is always useless.","People talk about failure too little.","Success is more important than learning."]],["What does “ambivalence” mean in “This ambivalence obscures what failure genuinely offers”?",["having mixed, contradictory feelings","total rejection","strong admiration","complete indifference"]],["According to the text, what most shapes what happens after a failure?",["the story we tell ourselves about it","how much money we have","whether other people see it","how quickly we forget it"]]]},{"t":"Who Owns an Idea?","p":["Few notions are as cherished in Western culture as that of the original genius, the solitary artist who conjures something entirely new from nothing. The history of art, however, tells a rather different story. Shakespeare borrowed the plot of Romeo and Juliet almost wholesale from an English poem published decades earlier; jazz musicians routinely built new melodies over the chord progressions of popular songs; and entire genres, from hip-hop to collage, are founded on the creative reuse of existing material. Creativity, it seems, is less an act of creation from nothing than one of recombination.","Even works celebrated as revolutionary reveal, on closer inspection, a dense web of influences. Picasso’s early experiments with cubism drew heavily on African masks and on the late paintings of Cézanne. The Beatles began by imitating American rock and roll and rhythm and blues before developing a sound of their own. Mexican muralists such as Diego Rivera combined techniques from Italian Renaissance frescoes with imagery from pre-Columbian art and the political concerns of their own time. In each case, originality lay not in the absence of influence but in the distinctive way influences were combined and transformed.","If this is so, the question of who “owns” an idea becomes remarkably complicated. Copyright law attempts a compromise: it protects specific expressions of an idea, a particular text, recording or image, but not the idea itself. You may write a story about star-crossed lovers; you may not reproduce someone else’s novel about them. The boundary sounds clear in principle and proves notoriously blurry in practice, as numerous court cases over melodies and visual styles have demonstrated.","Copyright itself is a relatively recent invention. The first modern copyright law, the Statute of Anne, was passed in Britain in 1710, partly to encourage learning by giving authors a temporary right to profit from their work. Crucially, that protection was designed to expire, after which works would enter the public domain, free for anyone to use. Today, protection typically lasts for the author’s lifetime plus several decades, a duration that critics argue has shifted the balance too far from the public toward rights holders, while defenders insist it is necessary to reward creators and their families. The tension between encouraging creation and preserving access to culture has never been fully resolved.","The rise of generative artificial intelligence has made these questions newly urgent. Systems trained on millions of images and texts can produce work “in the style of” living artists within seconds. Defenders argue that this is merely an accelerated version of what human artists have always done: absorbing influences and transforming them. Critics respond that scale changes the nature of the act. A human apprentice learns from a few masters over many years and inevitably adds something of their own; a system that ingests an artist’s entire body of work without consent or compensation, and can replicate it indefinitely, may undermine the very livelihoods that sustain creative culture.","Courts and governments in several countries are now considering how existing laws apply to these systems. Some artists have sued technology companies, arguing that training on their work without permission constitutes infringement; companies have generally responded that the training process is a form of analysis rather than copying. Meanwhile, legal authorities in some jurisdictions have indicated that works generated entirely by machines, without meaningful human creative input, may not qualify for copyright protection at all, raising the question of whether such works belong to anyone.","Perhaps the most productive response is to distinguish between two values that are often conflated: the freedom to be influenced, which is essential to all art, and the right of creators to recognition and fair reward, which is essential to their survival. A healthy creative ecosystem probably requires both."],"q":[["What myth does the writer challenge?",["the solitary original genius","the value of copyright","the importance of art","the value of collaboration"]],["What does copyright protect?",["specific expressions, not ideas","ideas themselves","artistic styles","titles and names only"]],["How do defenders view AI-generated art?",["as an accelerated version of absorbing influences","as completely original","as illegal","as a threat to all art"]],["What do critics say changes with AI?",["the scale, which may undermine artists’ livelihoods","nothing important","the colors used in art","the price of paintings"]],["What two values does the writer distinguish?",["freedom to be influenced and creators’ right to recognition","speed and quality","tradition and technology","originality and imitation"]],["Why does the writer mention Shakespeare and jazz musicians?",["to show that great art has always reused existing material","to criticize them for copying","to compare them with AI companies","to explain copyright law"]],["What does “conflated” mean in “two values that are often conflated”?",["mixed together as if they were the same","clearly separated","forgotten","protected by law"]],["What was one original purpose of copyright law?",["to encourage learning with a temporary right to profit","to protect artists forever","to stop people from reading books","to give governments control of art"]]]},{"t":"When a Measure Becomes a Target","p":["We live in an era of relentless measurement. Students are evaluated through grades and standardized test scores, hospitals through waiting times, police forces through crime statistics, and teenagers, increasingly, through likes and followers. The appeal is understandable: numbers appear objective, comparable and transparent, offering a seemingly fair alternative to subjective judgment.","Yet a principle formulated by the British economist Charles Goodhart warns of a fundamental problem. In its popular paraphrase, Goodhart’s Law states: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. Once people are rewarded or punished according to a particular number, they begin to optimize for the number itself rather than the quality it was intended to reflect.","A closely related idea, often called Campbell’s Law after the social scientist Donald Campbell, holds that the more a quantitative indicator is used for social decision-making, the more it will be subject to corruption pressures and the more it will distort the processes it is intended to monitor. Both principles point to the same insight: measurement is never a neutral act. The moment people know they are being measured, the measurement begins to change their behavior.","The examples are numerous and occasionally absurd. Schools judged solely by exam results may narrow their curriculum to what is tested, sacrificing art, music and genuine curiosity. A hospital measured by how quickly patients are seen might classify a brief initial check as being “seen.” Researchers evaluated by their number of publications may divide a single study into several thinner papers. In each case, the indicator improves while the underlying reality may stagnate or even deteriorate.","History offers a famous, possibly embellished, example. During the period of British rule in India, officials concerned about the number of venomous snakes in Delhi are said to have offered a reward for every dead cobra. At first, the policy seemed successful. Soon, however, enterprising residents began breeding cobras in order to collect the reward. When the authorities discovered the scheme and cancelled the program, the breeders released their now worthless snakes, leaving the city with more cobras than before. Whether or not every detail is accurate, the story has given its name to a recognizable pattern: the “cobra effect,” in which an incentive produces the opposite of its intended result.","Social media offers perhaps the most intimate illustration. A like count was presumably designed as a rough signal of appreciation. For many users, it has become an objective in itself, shaping what they post, how they present themselves and, in some cases, how they assess their own worth.","Part of the difficulty is that the most important qualities are often the hardest to measure. It is relatively easy to count how many problems a student answers correctly, but much harder to quantify curiosity, persistence, kindness or the ability to ask a good question. When institutions focus on what can be measured, they may gradually undervalue what cannot. Over time, people may even begin to doubt that unmeasurable qualities matter at all.","Teenagers may feel this pressure acutely. Grades, test scores, rankings, step counts, screen-time reports and follower numbers can create the impression that a life can be summarized in a dashboard. Psychologists have warned that when young people evaluate themselves primarily through such numbers, their sense of worth can become fragile, rising and falling with each new figure. A low score on a single exam, or a post that receives fewer reactions than expected, may then feel like a judgment on the whole person rather than on one isolated performance.","None of this implies that we should abandon measurement. Data can reveal injustices that intuition overlooks and hold institutions accountable. The danger lies in mistaking the metric for the thing it represents. Learning is more than a grade; health is more than a waiting time; friendship is not a follower count. A thoughtful society, like a thoughtful individual, uses numbers as instruments of understanding, not as substitutes for judgment."],"q":[["Why are numbers appealing as a form of evaluation?",["They appear objective and comparable.","They are always accurate.","They are easy to ignore.","They are never questioned."]],["What does Goodhart’s Law state?",["When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.","More data always improves decisions.","Targets should never change.","Targets make measures more accurate."]],["What might a school judged only by exam results do?",["narrow its curriculum to what is tested","add more art classes","stop giving exams","teach more creative subjects"]],["How does the writer describe the like count?",["as a signal that became an objective in itself","as a perfectly accurate measure of value","as irrelevant to users","as useless for social media"]],["What is the writer’s final position?",["Use numbers as instruments of understanding, not substitutes for judgment.","Abandon all measurement.","Trust metrics completely.","Replace judgment with numbers."]],["What do the hospital and research examples have in common?",["The number improves while the real quality doesn’t.","Both institutions became more honest.","Both show that metrics always work.","Both are about social media."]],["What does “stagnate” mean in “the underlying reality may stagnate”?",["stop improving","improve quickly","disappear","become visible"]],["What is the “cobra effect”?",["an incentive that produces the opposite of its intended result","a method for counting animals","a law that protects snakes","a reward that always works"]]]},{"t":"The Unreliable Archive","p":["Most of us intuitively regard memory as a kind of archive: experiences are recorded, stored and later retrieved, perhaps faded but essentially intact. Decades of research in psychology and neuroscience have thoroughly dismantled this metaphor. Memory, it turns out, is not reproductive but reconstructive. Each time we recall an event, we rebuild it from fragments, filling the gaps with expectations, later knowledge and the emotions of the present moment.","Neuroscience offers a partial explanation. A memory is not stored in a single location, like a file in a drawer, but distributed across networks of neurons in different regions of the brain. Recalling an event involves reactivating those networks, and research on a process called reconsolidation suggests that, each time a memory is retrieved, it may become temporarily unstable before being stored again. During that window, it can be strengthened, weakened or subtly altered by new information. In a sense, we do not remember the original event so much as the last time we remembered it.","The implications are unsettling. In a series of influential experiments beginning in the 1970s, the psychologist Elizabeth Loftus demonstrated how easily memories can be altered. In one classic study, participants who watched a film of a car accident estimated significantly higher speeds when asked how fast the cars were going when they “smashed” into each other than when the verb used was “hit.” Some even later recalled seeing broken glass that had never appeared in the film. Subsequent research showed that it was possible to implant entirely fictitious childhood memories, such as having been lost in a shopping mall, in a substantial minority of participants.","Even our most vivid memories are not immune. After dramatic public events, researchers have asked people to describe where they were when they heard the news, then questioned them again years later. Their accounts often changed considerably, yet their confidence remained high. Vividness, it appears, is no guarantee of accuracy.","Social influences add another layer. When people discuss an event together, they often converge on a shared version, absorbing details from one another without realizing it. Researchers call this memory conformity. Photographs, family stories and repeated retellings can also shape what we believe we remember, so that some of our earliest childhood “memories” may in fact be reconstructions based on things we were later told or shown.","These findings have serious consequences in courtrooms, where eyewitness testimony has historically been treated as compelling evidence. But they also raise more personal, almost philosophical questions. If our memories are continually revised, in what sense is our sense of self, which depends so heavily on them, stable?","The legal consequences have been significant. In the United States, organizations that review wrongful convictions have found that mistaken eyewitness identification played a role in a large proportion of cases later overturned by DNA evidence. Many of these witnesses were not lying; they sincerely believed what they remembered. In response, researchers have recommended changes to police procedures, such as recording a witness’s confidence immediately, avoiding suggestive questions and ensuring that the officer conducting an identification does not know which person is the suspect.","It would be a mistake, however, to conclude that memory is simply defective. Its flexibility may be a feature rather than a flaw. A system that updated memories with new information, extracted general patterns and discarded irrelevant details would be well suited to helping us anticipate the future, which is arguably the main purpose of remembering the past. Some researchers have even suggested that the same capacity for recombination that produces false memories also enables imagination, planning and creativity.","Perhaps the answer is that identity was never a fixed record but an ongoing narrative. We are, in part, the stories we keep telling ourselves about who we have been, which suggests, intriguingly, that we have more authorship over those stories than we tend to assume."],"q":[["What metaphor has research dismantled?",["memory as an archive","memory as a story","memory as a muscle","memory as a camera"]],["What did Loftus’s car accident study show?",["The verb used in a question influenced speed estimates.","People remember accidents perfectly.","Films are unreliable.","Witnesses remembered the film perfectly."]],["What was implanted in some participants?",["a false memory of being lost in a mall","a fear of cars","a real childhood memory","a memory of winning a prize"]],["What did research on memories of dramatic events find?",["Accounts changed, but confidence remained high.","Accounts never changed.","People forgot everything.","Confidence dropped over time."]],["What does the writer suggest about identity?",["It is an ongoing narrative we partly author.","It is completely fixed.","It has no connection to memory.","It is controlled by others."]],["Why does the writer call memory “the unreliable archive”?",["Memories feel like stored records but are rebuilt each time.","Memories are stored in libraries.","Most people have bad memory.","Archives are always wrong."]],["What does “unsettling” mean in “The implications are unsettling”?",["disturbing or worrying","boring","encouraging","simple"]],["Why might the flexibility of memory be useful?",["It helps us anticipate the future, imagine and plan.","It makes us remember every detail perfectly.","It protects us from all false memories.","It helps witnesses in court."]]]},{"t":"From Disease to Comfort: The Story of Nostalgia","p":["In 1688, a Swiss medical student named Johannes Hofer coined a new term to describe a mysterious affliction he had observed among Swiss mercenaries serving far from home. Combining the Greek words nostos (homecoming) and algos (pain), he called it nostalgia. Its symptoms, he reported, included melancholy, insomnia, loss of appetite and, in severe cases, death. For roughly two centuries thereafter, nostalgia was treated as a serious medical condition, occasionally attributed to causes as improbable as damage to the eardrums from the constant clanging of cowbells in the Alps.","Hofer’s diagnosis reflected the experience of his era. Soldiers, students and servants frequently lived far from their families for years, with no means of communication beyond occasional letters. Physicians across Europe began to report similar cases, and during later conflicts, military doctors documented soldiers who seemed to suffer from intense longing for home. Some armies reportedly discouraged the singing of certain traditional songs, fearing that they would trigger the condition among homesick troops.","By the twentieth century, the diagnosis had migrated from medicine to psychiatry, where nostalgia was regarded as a symptom of depression or an unhealthy refusal to accept the present. Only in recent decades has research painted a markedly different picture.","Studies led by social psychologists, notably Constantine Sedikides and his colleagues, suggest that nostalgia is a predominantly positive, if bittersweet, emotion, and one that appears to serve important functions. People tend to experience it more frequently when they feel lonely, anxious or uncertain about the meaning of their lives, and recalling cherished memories often counteracts those very states, increasing feelings of social connectedness, continuity and self-esteem. In some experiments, participants who engaged in nostalgic reflection even reported feeling physically warmer.","The methods used in this research are often simple. In a typical experiment, some participants are asked to write about a nostalgic event from their past, while others write about an ordinary recent event. The researchers then measure variables such as mood, self-esteem or feelings of social connection. Across many studies in different countries, participants in the nostalgic condition have tended to report a greater sense of meaning and stronger connections to others. Interestingly, people in many cultures describe similar triggers for nostalgia: music, smells, photographs, conversations with old friends and particular seasons or holidays.","Nostalgia, in this view, functions less as a retreat from the present than as a resource for facing it: a way of reminding ourselves that our lives have contained love, belonging and meaning, and might again.","Music appears to be an especially powerful trigger. Songs heard during adolescence and early adulthood seem to hold a special place in memory, perhaps because these years are associated with forming identity and experiencing many emotions for the first time. Hearing such a song decades later can bring back not only facts but feelings, transporting a listener, at least briefly, to an earlier version of themselves. Some care facilities for older people have used familiar music to help residents with memory loss reconnect with their past and with the people around them.","Researchers have also distinguished between different ways of relating to the past. Personal nostalgia, which focuses on one’s own experiences, appears largely beneficial. A different tendency, sometimes described as a longing for an idealized collective past that the person may never have lived, can have more complex effects, especially when it encourages the belief that everything was better before and that change itself is a threat. This distinction helps explain how the same emotion can comfort an individual and, at the same time, be used to divide a society.","Its power, however, can also be exploited. Advertisers, entertainment companies and political movements frequently evoke an idealized past to sell products or ideas, smoothing away the injustices and hardships that the original era contained. The challenge, then, is to draw comfort from memory without mistaking a carefully curated past for a lost paradise to which we ought to return."],"q":[["Who first used the term “nostalgia”?",["Johannes Hofer","Constantine Sedikides","a group of Swiss soldiers","a Greek philosopher"]],["How was nostalgia originally regarded?",["as a serious medical condition","as a pleasant emotion","as a type of music","as a sign of intelligence"]],["According to recent research, when do people tend to feel nostalgic?",["when they feel lonely, anxious or uncertain","only when they are very happy","only in old age","when they are busy at work"]],["What effect did some participants report after nostalgic reflection?",["feeling physically warmer","feeling more anxious","losing their appetite","feeling sleepy"]],["What risk does the writer mention in the final paragraph?",["Nostalgia can be exploited by presenting an idealized past.","Nostalgia always causes depression.","Nostalgia makes people forget the past.","It makes people too happy."]],["How has the understanding of nostalgia changed over time?",["from a disease to a mostly positive, useful emotion","from a pleasant emotion to a disease","It has never changed.","from a type of music to a medical term"]],["What does “bittersweet” mean in “a predominantly positive, if bittersweet, emotion”?",["happy and sad at the same time","completely sad","very intense","tasteless"]],["Why do songs from adolescence seem to have special power?",["Those years are linked to forming identity and new emotions.","Teenagers listen to more music than adults.","Old songs are louder.","People only remember music from childhood."]]]}],"kids|Pre-A1":[{"t":"My Family","p":["Hi! My name is Nico. I am six.","This is my mom. She is tall. This is my dad. He is funny.","I have a sister. Her name is Ana. She is two.","I have a grandma, too. She makes cookies.","We have a dog. His name is Max.","I love my family!"],"q":[["How old is Nico?",["six","two","ten","eight"]],["What is Dad like?",["funny","tall","sad","short"]],["Who is Ana?",["his sister","his mom","his grandma","his cousin"]],["What does Grandma make?",["cookies","pizza","a cake","bread"]],["What is the dog’s name?",["Max","Nico","Pip","Rex"]]]},{"t":"On the Farm","p":["This is Uncle Joe’s farm. It is big and green.","Look! A cow. The cow is black and white. It says “moo.”","Look! A duck. The duck is in the water. It says “quack.”","Look! Three pigs. They are pink and fat.","Look! A horse. It is brown. I can ride the horse!","I like the farm."],"q":[["Whose farm is it?",["Uncle Joe’s","Grandma’s","Nico’s","Aunt Rosa’s"]],["What color is the cow?",["black and white","brown","pink","gray"]],["Where is the duck?",["in the water","on the horse","in the house","under a tree"]],["How many pigs are there?",["three","one","five","two"]],["What animal can the child ride?",["the horse","the cow","the pig","the duck"]]]},{"t":"My Lunch Box","p":["This is my lunch box. It is blue.","What is in my lunch box?","A sandwich. I like sandwiches!","An apple. It is red.","Two carrots. They are orange.","And water in a bottle.","I eat my lunch at school with my friend Leo. Yum!"],"q":[["What color is the lunch box?",["blue","red","orange","green"]],["What color is the apple?",["red","green","yellow","pink"]],["How many carrots are there?",["two","one","three","four"]],["What is the drink?",["water","milk","juice","tea"]],["Who eats with the child?",["Leo","Mom","Max","Ana"]]]},{"t":"My Toy Box","p":["I have a big toy box. Let’s open it!","Here is my teddy bear. He is soft and brown.","Here is my car. It is small and fast.","Here are my blocks. They are red, blue and yellow.","Here is my doll. She has a pink dress.","I play with my toys every day. Then I put them in the box."],"q":[["Is the toy box big or small?",["big","small","pink","empty"]],["What is the teddy bear like?",["soft and brown","small and fast","red and blue","small and pink"]],["What is small and fast?",["the car","the doll","the box","the teddy bear"]],["What does the doll have?",["a pink dress","a red hat","blue shoes","a blue dress"]],["Where do the toys go after playing?",["in the box","on the bed","in the garden","under the bed"]]]}],"kids|A1":[{"t":"Sam’s Birthday Party","p":["Today is Saturday and it is Sam’s birthday. He is seven years old!","There are balloons in the garden. They are green and yellow, Sam’s favorite colors. His mom makes a big chocolate cake.","At four o’clock, his friends arrive. Lucy has a present with a red bow. Tom has a card with a dinosaur on it.","The children play games and dance to music. Then it is time for the cake. Everybody sings “Happy Birthday.” Sam closes his eyes, makes a wish and blows out the candles.","What is the wish? It’s a secret!"],"q":[["How old is Sam today?",["seven","six","eight","five"]],["What are Sam’s favorite colors?",["green and yellow","red and blue","black and white","pink and purple"]],["What kind of cake does Mom make?",["chocolate","strawberry","lemon","vanilla"]],["What is on Tom’s card?",["a dinosaur","a cat","a car","a robot"]],["What is Sam’s wish?",["It’s a secret.","a new bike","a puppy","a trip to the zoo"]]]},{"t":"A Day at the Zoo","p":["Emma’s class goes to the zoo today. The children are very excited.","First, they see the elephants. Elephants are very big and gray. They have long noses called trunks.","Next, they see the monkeys. The monkeys jump from tree to tree. They are noisy and funny.","Then they visit the giraffes. Giraffes have very long necks. They eat leaves from the top of the trees.","Last, they see a tiger. It is orange with black stripes. It is sleeping on a rock.","“What is your favorite animal?” asks the teacher. “The giraffe!” says Emma. “I want a long neck, too!”"],"q":[["Who goes to the zoo with Emma?",["her class","her grandma","her dog","her family"]],["What do elephants have?",["long noses called trunks","black stripes","long necks","very short tails"]],["What do the monkeys do?",["They jump from tree to tree.","They sleep on a rock.","They eat leaves.","They swim in a pool."]],["What is the tiger doing?",["sleeping on a rock","eating leaves","jumping","drinking water"]],["What is Emma’s favorite animal?",["the giraffe","the tiger","the monkey","the elephant"]]]},{"t":"Pancakes with Grandpa","p":["Every Sunday, Mateo makes pancakes with his grandpa. It is their special breakfast.","First, they put flour, milk and two eggs in a big bowl. Mateo mixes everything with a spoon. Grandpa adds a little sugar.","Next, Grandpa heats the pan. He is careful because the pan is very hot. Mateo watches.","Grandpa makes the pancakes round and flat. When there are bubbles on top, he turns them over. Mateo counts: “One, two, three… ten pancakes!”","They put honey and bananas on the pancakes. Grandma comes to the kitchen. “Mmm, it smells delicious!” she says."],"q":[["When do they make pancakes?",["every Sunday","every Monday","on birthdays","every Saturday"]],["How many eggs do they use?",["two","one","ten","three"]],["Why is Grandpa careful?",["The pan is very hot.","The eggs are small.","Mateo is sleeping.","Grandma is watching."]],["How many pancakes does Mateo count?",["ten","three","seven","five"]],["What do they put on the pancakes?",["honey and bananas","chocolate and nuts","cheese","jam and strawberries"]]]},{"t":"Lucy Can Swim","p":["Lucy is eight. She can run fast and she can ride a bike, but she can’t swim.","This summer, Lucy goes to swimming lessons. Her teacher’s name is Coach Rita. She is kind and patient.","On Monday, Lucy is scared of the water. She holds the side of the pool. On Wednesday, she can put her face in the water and blow bubbles.","On Friday, she can kick her legs and float on her back. Coach Rita says, “Great job, Lucy!”","At the end of the summer, Lucy can swim from one side of the pool to the other. Her family claps and cheers. Lucy is very proud."],"q":[["What can’t Lucy do at first?",["swim","run fast","ride a bike","jump rope"]],["Who is Coach Rita?",["her swimming teacher","her sister","her friend","her aunt"]],["How does Lucy feel on Monday?",["scared","proud","tired","happy"]],["What can Lucy do on Wednesday?",["put her face in the water","float on her back","swim across the pool","dive into the pool"]],["How does Lucy feel at the end?",["very proud","sad","scared","bored"]]]},{"t":"Our New Puppy","p":["The Torres family has a new puppy. She is small and white with one black ear.","The children want to give her a name. Diego says, “Snowball!” Paula says, “Cookie!” Dad says, “Let’s wait and see.”","The puppy runs everywhere. She plays with socks and shoes. She sleeps in a little basket next to Paula’s bed.","Every morning, Diego gives her food and fresh water. In the afternoon, Paula takes her for a short walk.","One day, the puppy finds a bag of cookies under the table and eats one! Everybody laughs. “That’s her name,” says Dad. “Cookie!”"],"q":[["What color is the puppy?",["white with one black ear","all black","brown","brown with one white ear"]],["What name does Diego want?",["Snowball","Cookie","Max","Lucky"]],["Where does the puppy sleep?",["in a basket next to Paula’s bed","in the garden","under the table","in a box next to Diego’s bed"]],["Who takes the puppy for a walk?",["Paula","Diego","Mom","Dad"]],["Why is her name Cookie?",["She eats a cookie.","She is brown like a cookie.","Diego likes cookies.","Paula likes cookies."]]]},{"t":"Halloween Night","p":["Today is October 31. It is Halloween!","Leo is a black cat. He has ears and a long tail. Mia is a witch. She has a big hat and a broom.","Mom has a pumpkin. It has two eyes and a big smile. It is orange and it shines at night.","Leo and Mia go to the houses on their street. They knock on the doors and say, “Trick or treat!”","The neighbors give them candy. Leo has ten candies. Mia has twelve!","At home, they share the candy with Mom and Dad. Happy Halloween!"],"q":[["What day is Halloween?",["October 31","December 25","May 10","November 2"]],["What is Leo’s costume?",["a black cat","a witch","a ghost","a pumpkin"]],["What does Mia have?",["a big hat and a broom","ears and a tail","a pumpkin","a cat costume"]],["What do they say at the doors?",["“Trick or treat!”","“Good morning!”","“Happy birthday!”","“Good night!”"]],["How many candies does Mia have?",["twelve","ten","two","eleven"]]]}],"kids|A2+":[{"t":"The Case of the Missing Cookies","p":["On Saturday afternoon, Mom baked chocolate chip cookies and left them on a plate in the kitchen to cool down. When she came back, the plate was empty!","“Who took the cookies?” she asked. Ema, who was eight and loved detective stories, took her magnifying glass. “Don’t worry, Mom. I’ll solve this case.”","First, she looked for clues. On the floor, there were some crumbs going toward the back door. Next to the door, she found small muddy footprints. They were too small for a person.","Ema followed the footprints into the garden. Behind the bushes, she found her dog, Bruno. He was lying on the grass with chocolate on his nose and a very guilty face.","“Case closed!” said Ema. But Mom looked worried. “Chocolate is dangerous for dogs,” she explained. They called the vet, who said Bruno only ate a little and would be fine. That night, Mom baked new cookies and put them on a very high shelf."],"q":[["Where did Mom leave the cookies?",["on a plate in the kitchen","in the garden","in a box","on a high shelf"]],["What did Ema use?",["a magnifying glass","a camera","a flashlight","a notebook"]],["Why weren’t the footprints from a person?",["They were too small.","They were too big.","They were blue.","They were very old."]],["Who took the cookies?",["Bruno, the dog","Ema’s brother","a neighbor","a cat"]],["Why was Mom worried?",["Chocolate is dangerous for dogs.","There were no more cookies.","Bruno was lost.","The kitchen was dirty."]]]}],"children|A1":[{"t":"Taco Friday","p":["In my house, Friday is a special day. It is Taco Friday!","After school, my mom and I go to the market. We buy tortillas, tomatoes, onions, avocados and meat.","At home, everybody helps. My dad cooks the meat. My little brother washes the tomatoes. I make the guacamole. I am very good at it!","My grandpa makes the salsa. His salsa is very spicy, so I only eat a little.","At seven o’clock, we sit at the table. We put music on and we talk about our week.","I like Taco Friday because we are all together and the food is delicious."],"q":[["Where do the writer and Mom go after school?",["to the market","to the park","to a restaurant","to the supermarket"]],["What does the writer make?",["the guacamole","the salsa","the meat","the tortillas"]],["Who makes the salsa?",["her grandpa","her dad","her brother","her mom"]],["Why does the writer eat only a little salsa?",["It is very spicy.","It is cold.","She doesn’t like tomatoes.","It is too salty."]],["Why does the writer like Taco Friday?",["The family is together and the food is great.","She doesn’t go to school.","She watches TV all night.","There is no school on Friday."]],["What does “spicy” mean in the text?",["hot, with a lot of chili","very sweet","cold","very soft"]]]},{"t":"Diego’s New Bike","p":["It is Diego’s birthday. He is ten today. His parents give him a big present. It is a new red bike!","Diego is very happy, but there is a problem. He can’t ride a bike.","“Don’t worry,” says his older sister, Paula. “I can help you.”","Every afternoon, they go to the park. Paula holds the bike and Diego pedals. He falls three times, but he doesn’t stop.","On Saturday, Paula lets go of the bike. Diego doesn’t know. He rides alone to the end of the park!","“I can do it!” he shouts. Now Diego rides his bike every day."],"q":[["How old is Diego today?",["ten","nine","eleven","twelve"]],["What color is the bike?",["red","blue","black","green"]],["What is Diego’s problem?",["He can’t ride a bike.","The bike is broken.","He doesn’t like red.","He doesn’t have a helmet."]],["Who helps Diego?",["his sister Paula","his dad","his friend","his grandpa"]],["What happens on Saturday?",["He rides alone.","He falls again.","He sells the bike.","Paula falls."]],["How does Diego feel when he rides alone?",["proud and excited","angry","scared and sad","tired and bored"]]]},{"t":"Library Day","p":["At my school, Thursday is library day. My class goes to the library at ten o’clock.","The library is big and quiet. There are thousands of books, comfortable cushions and big windows.","Mrs. Ramos is the librarian. She is very nice. She always helps us find good books.","I love adventure stories. My friend Ana likes books about animals. Carlos only reads comics!","We can take two books home for one week. This week, I have a book about pirates and a book about space.","At night, I read in bed for twenty minutes. Reading is my favorite hobby."],"q":[["When is library day?",["Thursday","Monday","Friday","Tuesday"]],["Who is Mrs. Ramos?",["the librarian","the principal","the writer’s mom","a math teacher"]],["What does Carlos read?",["comics","animal books","adventure stories","books about space"]],["How many books can students take home?",["two","one","five","three"]],["How long does the writer read at night?",["twenty minutes","one hour","five minutes","half an hour"]],["Why do you think the library is a good place to read?",["It is big and quiet, with comfortable cushions.","It is very noisy.","There are no books for kids.","Students can play video games there."]]]},{"t":"Shelly the Turtle","p":["I have a pet turtle. Her name is Shelly. She is five years old.","Shelly is green and brown. She has a hard shell on her back. When she is scared, she puts her head inside her shell.","Shelly lives in a big tank with water and rocks. She likes swimming, and she also likes sitting on a rock under a warm lamp.","I feed her every morning before school. She eats special turtle food, lettuce and sometimes small pieces of fruit.","Every Saturday, I clean her tank with my dad.","Turtles can live for more than thirty years. Shelly and I can be friends for a long time!"],"q":[["How old is Shelly?",["five","thirty","ten","two"]],["What does Shelly do when she is scared?",["She puts her head in her shell.","She swims fast.","She jumps.","She bites."]],["Where does Shelly sit to get warm?",["on a rock under a lamp","in the writer’s bed","in the garden","in the water all day"]],["When does the writer feed Shelly?",["every morning","every night","on Saturdays","after school"]],["How long can turtles live?",["more than thirty years","about five years","one year","more than one hundred years"]],["Why does the writer say “Shelly and I can be friends for a long time”?",["Turtles can live for more than thirty years.","Shelly is very friendly.","Shelly is a young turtle.","Turtles are easy to clean."]]]}],"children|A2":[{"t":"Our First Camping Trip","p":["Last summer, my family went camping for the first time. We drove to a forest in the mountains near Santiago, Nuevo León.","When we arrived, my dad tried to put up the tent. It took him almost an hour because he didn’t read the instructions! My mom and I collected wood for the campfire while my little brother looked for interesting rocks.","In the evening, we cooked sausages and made s’mores with marshmallows and chocolate. Then we sat around the fire and told stories.","At midnight, I woke up because I heard a strange noise outside the tent. Something was moving near our food. My heart was beating very fast. I woke up my dad, and he turned on his flashlight.","It was a raccoon! It was trying to open our bag of bread. When it saw the light, it ran away into the trees.","The next morning, we laughed about it. Now we always keep our food in the car."],"q":[["Where did the family go camping?",["in the mountains near Santiago","at the beach","in a park in Monterrey","at a lake in Coahuila"]],["Why did it take Dad a long time to put up the tent?",["He didn’t read the instructions.","It was raining.","The tent was broken.","The wind was very strong."]],["What did they make with marshmallows?",["s’mores","a cake","hot chocolate","pancakes"]],["What woke the narrator up?",["a strange noise","the rain","her brother","the wind"]],["What do they do now with their food?",["keep it in the car","eat it all at night","hang it in a tree","leave it outside the tent"]],["How did the family feel about the raccoon the next morning?",["They thought it was funny.","They were still very scared.","They were angry with Dad.","They wanted to go home."]],["What does “strange” mean in “a strange noise”?",["unusual and surprising","very quiet","beautiful","normal"]]]},{"t":"A Letter from Canada","p":["Dear Isabela,","Thank you for your letter and the photos of Monterrey! The mountains are amazing. Here in Winnipeg, the land is very flat, so I can see the sky everywhere.","Right now it is winter, and it is minus twenty-five degrees. Can you believe it? My nose freezes when I walk to school! We wear snow pants, big boots, gloves and two hats.","But winter is also fun. On weekends, my brother and I go ice skating on the river. Last Saturday, we built a snow fort in our backyard and had a snowball fight with our neighbors.","You wrote that you never saw snow. That’s so interesting! I never ate a real taco. Maybe one day you can visit me in winter, and I can visit you in the summer.","Please tell me more about your school. Do you have a uniform? What do you eat for lunch?","Your friend,","Hannah"],"q":[["Where does Hannah live?",["Winnipeg, Canada","Monterrey","New York","Toronto"]],["What does Hannah say about the land where she lives?",["It is very flat.","It has many mountains.","It is near the ocean.","It is very hot."]],["What do Hannah and her brother do on weekends?",["go ice skating","go swimming","go to the movies","go to the beach"]],["What has Isabela never seen?",["snow","mountains","a river","a taco"]],["What does Hannah want to know?",["about Isabela’s school","about Isabela’s pets","about Mexican music","about Isabela’s family"]],["Why does Hannah’s nose freeze?",["It is very, very cold in Winnipeg.","She walks very fast.","She doesn’t wear a hat.","She is sick."]],["What does “flat” mean in “the land is very flat”?",["with no hills or mountains","very green","full of snow","very small"]]]},{"t":"The School Play","p":["Every year, our school performs a play for the parents. This year, it was “Alice in Wonderland.” I really wanted to be the Mad Hatter, but my teacher gave the part to my friend Rodrigo. I was a tree. A tree with no words!","I was disappointed, but I decided to be the best tree in the history of the school. I practiced standing very still and moving my branches slowly, like in the wind.","On the night of the play, the auditorium was full. Ten minutes before the show, Rodrigo’s mom called the teacher. Rodrigo had a fever and couldn’t come!","My teacher looked at me. “Martín, you came to every rehearsal. Do you know the Mad Hatter’s lines?” Of course I knew them. I listened to Rodrigo every day.","I put on the big hat and walked on the stage. My legs were shaking, but I didn’t forget a single word. At the end, everybody clapped.","Being a tree taught me something: always be ready."],"q":[["What play did the school perform?",["Alice in Wonderland","Peter Pan","The Lion King","Snow White"]],["What part did Martín get first?",["a tree","the Mad Hatter","Alice","a rock"]],["Why couldn’t Rodrigo come?",["He had a fever.","He forgot.","He was on a trip.","His family moved."]],["How did Martín know the Mad Hatter’s lines?",["He listened to Rodrigo at every rehearsal.","He read the book at home.","The teacher gave him the lines that day.","His mom told him."]],["What lesson did Martín learn?",["Always be ready.","Never be a tree.","Plays are boring.","Mad Hatters are funny."]],["Why did Martín decide to be “the best tree”?",["He wanted to do his best with any part.","He liked trees more than people.","His teacher asked him.","He didn’t want to talk."]],["What does “rehearsal” mean in “you came to every rehearsal”?",["a practice before the real show","a party after the show","a test at school","a costume"]]]},{"t":"The Fun Run","p":["Last October, my school organized a five-kilometer fun run in Parque Fundidora. The money from the tickets was for a children’s hospital, so my mom and I signed up together.","We trained for six weeks. Three times a week, we ran around our neighborhood after dinner. The first time, I could only run for five minutes before I needed to walk. By the end, I could run for thirty minutes without stopping.","On the day of the race, there were more than six hundred people. Some of them were wearing funny costumes. I saw a dinosaur, two superheroes and a man dressed like a giant taco!","The first three kilometers were easy. But in the fourth kilometer, my legs felt heavy and I wanted to stop. My mom held my hand and said, “Just one more kilometer. We can do it.”","We crossed the finish line together in thirty-four minutes. We didn’t win, but we got medals, and our school raised eighty thousand pesos for the hospital."],"q":[["Where was the fun run?",["Parque Fundidora","at the school","in Santiago","Parque Chipinque"]],["Who was the money for?",["a children’s hospital","the school","the winners","the animals at the zoo"]],["How long did they train?",["six weeks","two weeks","six months","six days"]],["When was the race most difficult?",["in the fourth kilometer","at the start","in the first kilometer","at the finish line"]],["How much money did the school raise?",["eighty thousand pesos","eight thousand pesos","six hundred pesos","eighty pesos"]],["How did the narrator change during the training?",["At first five minutes, at the end thirty minutes without stopping.","At first fast, at the end very slow.","The narrator stopped running.","The narrator ran only on weekends."]],["What does “raised” mean in “our school raised eighty thousand pesos”?",["collected money for a cause","lost money","spent money","counted money"]]]},{"t":"A Week Without Screens","p":["One evening, my dad looked around the living room. My mom was on her phone, my sister was watching videos, and I was playing video games. Nobody was talking.","“Let’s try something,” Dad said. “One week with no screens after school. For everyone, including me.”","The first two days were terrible. I was bored, and I kept looking at the place where my tablet usually was. My sister complained all the time.","But on Wednesday, something changed. We found an old board game in the closet and played it after dinner. Mom laughed so much that she cried.","On Thursday, I finished a book about a dragon. On Friday, my sister and I baked cookies with Grandma. On Saturday, we went to the park and flew a kite.","When the week ended, we didn’t go back to screens all the time. Now we have a rule: no phones at dinner, and Saturday afternoon is family time."],"q":[["Why did Dad suggest the idea?",["Nobody in the family was talking.","The TV was broken.","The kids had bad grades.","The internet was very slow."]],["How were the first two days?",["terrible","easy","exciting","fun"]],["What did they find on Wednesday?",["an old board game","a new phone","a dragon book","a lost tablet"]],["What did the narrator do on Friday?",["baked cookies with Grandma","flew a kite","played soccer","went to the movies"]],["What is the family rule now?",["no phones at dinner","no TV ever","games only on Monday","phones only on Sunday"]],["How did the family change after the week?",["They spend more time together now.","They use screens all the time again.","They sold their phones.","They don’t eat dinner together."]],["What does “complained” mean in “My sister complained all the time”?",["said she was unhappy","laughed","helped","slept"]]]},{"t":"The Haunted Classroom","p":["The day before Halloween, the students in Room 4 were decorating their classroom with paper bats and orange lights.","Suddenly, they heard a noise. Scratch, scratch, scratch. It came from the closet at the back of the room. Everyone stopped working.","“It’s a ghost!” whispered Sofía. “Ghosts don’t scratch,” said Daniel, but he looked a little nervous too.","Their teacher, Ms. Garza, walked slowly to the closet. The students held their breath. She opened the door very carefully and…","A small gray kitten jumped out! It was hiding in a box of costumes. It was hungry and a little scared.","The class gave the kitten some milk and named it Boo. Ms. Garza called the school director, and the next day a family from the school adopted Boo. It was the best Halloween surprise ever."],"q":[["What were the students doing?",["decorating their classroom","taking an exam","eating lunch","painting the walls"]],["Where did the noise come from?",["the closet","the window","the teacher’s desk","under the floor"]],["What did Sofía think it was?",["a ghost","a kitten","a mouse","a rat"]],["What was really in the closet?",["a small gray kitten","a ghost","a bat","a mouse"]],["What happened to Boo?",["a family adopted it","it stayed in the closet","it ran away","the teacher took it home"]],["Why were the students nervous?",["They heard a strange noise in the closet.","They had a big exam.","The teacher was angry.","The lights went out."]],["What does “whispered” mean in “whispered Sofía”?",["said very quietly","shouted","sang","cried"]]]}],"teens|A1":[{"t":"The New Café","p":["There is a new café near our school. It is called “Bean There.” My friends and I go there every Friday after school.","The café is small, but it is very cool. There are plants everywhere and lights on the ceiling. The music is good, and the chairs are comfortable.","I usually order a mango smoothie and a slice of carrot cake. My friend Valeria always has a hot chocolate, even in summer! Pablo orders a coffee, but he puts five spoons of sugar in it.","The owner is a young woman named Sara. She is very friendly, and she remembers our names.","On Fridays, the café is full of students. Some people study, some play board games and some just talk.","It is our favorite place in the neighborhood."],"q":[["What is the name of the café?",["Bean There","Café Monterrey","The Plant","Bean Here"]],["What is everywhere in the café?",["plants","books","pictures","toys"]],["What does Valeria always have?",["a hot chocolate","a mango smoothie","a coffee","an orange juice"]],["What is strange about Pablo’s coffee?",["He puts five spoons of sugar in it.","He drinks it cold.","He never finishes it.","He adds salt to it."]],["What does Sara do?",["She is the owner.","She is a student.","She is a teacher.","She is the cook."]],["Why do the students like the café so much?",["It is cool and friendly, and they can study, play or talk.","It is very cheap.","It is next to their houses.","It has video games."]]]},{"t":"My Sister’s Job","p":["My older sister, Fernanda, is twenty-four. She has a very interesting job. She is an air traffic controller at Monterrey airport.","Air traffic controllers help airplanes take off and land safely. Fernanda works in a tall tower with big windows. She watches the planes on a screen and talks to the pilots on a radio.","Her job is difficult and very important. She needs to concentrate all the time, and she can’t make mistakes. She often speaks English with pilots, because English is the international language of aviation.","Fernanda works different hours every week. Sometimes she starts at six in the morning, and sometimes she works at night.","I want to be a pilot one day. Maybe one day Fernanda can help me land!"],"q":[["How old is Fernanda?",["twenty-four","thirty","eighteen","twenty"]],["Where does she work?",["in a tower at the airport","on an airplane","in an office downtown","at a hotel"]],["How does she talk to pilots?",["on a radio","by email","on her phone","with signs"]],["Why does she speak English at work?",["It is the international language of aviation.","Her boss is American.","She likes practicing.","It is required at her school."]],["What does the narrator want to be?",["a pilot","an air traffic controller","a doctor","an engineer"]],["Why can’t Fernanda make mistakes?",["Airplanes and people could be in danger.","Her boss is very strict.","She is new at her job.","The radio is old."]]]},{"t":"The Big Game","p":["Today is the final game of the school basketball season. Our team, the Falcons, is playing against the Tigers. The Tigers are very good. They usually win every game.","Before the game, our coach talks to us. “Don’t worry about winning,” she says. “Play as a team and have fun.”","The game starts, and the Tigers score first. At half time, we are losing by ten points. Everybody is quiet.","In the second half, we play better. We pass the ball more, and we run faster. My best friend, Iván, scores three times!","With five seconds left, the score is 48 to 48. I have the ball. I throw it… and it goes in!","We win, 50 to 48! Everybody jumps and shouts. It is the best day of the year."],"q":[["What is the name of the narrator’s team?",["the Falcons","the Tigers","the Eagles","the Lions"]],["What does the coach say before the game?",["Play as a team and have fun.","You must win.","Run as fast as you can.","Score fast!"]],["What is the situation at half time?",["The team is losing by ten points.","The team is winning.","The score is equal.","The team is winning by five points."]],["How many times does Iván score?",["three times","once","ten times","twice"]],["What is the final score?",["50 to 48","48 to 48","48 to 50","50 to 40"]],["How does the narrator feel at the end?",["extremely happy and excited","disappointed","nervous","bored"]]]},{"t":"Learning to Cook","p":["I am fifteen, and until last month, I couldn’t cook anything. I could only make cereal and instant noodles.","Then my mom started a new job, and she now comes home late on Tuesdays and Thursdays. So I have a new responsibility: I make dinner on those days.","The first Tuesday was a disaster. I tried to make spaghetti, but the water boiled over and the pasta was too soft. My little brother laughed at me.","Now I watch cooking videos and practice. I can make scrambled eggs, quesadillas, rice and a simple chicken soup.","Last Thursday, I made chicken with vegetables. My mom tried it and said, “This is really good!”","I feel proud. Cooking is not easy, but it’s a useful skill, and I enjoy it."],"q":[["What could the narrator make before?",["only cereal and instant noodles","spaghetti","chicken soup","eggs and rice"]],["Why does the narrator cook on Tuesdays and Thursdays?",["His mom comes home late.","He has cooking class.","His dad asked him.","He wants to be a chef."]],["What went wrong the first time?",["The pasta was too soft.","He burned the chicken.","He forgot the salt.","The kitchen was dirty."]],["How does the narrator learn now?",["He watches cooking videos.","He takes classes.","His grandma teaches him.","He reads cookbooks."]],["How does the narrator feel at the end?",["proud","angry","tired","worried"]],["Why did the little brother laugh?",["The first dinner was a disaster.","The narrator told a joke.","The pasta was delicious.","Mom came home early."]]]},{"t":"Shopping for a Birthday Gift","p":["My best friend Ximena’s birthday is next Saturday. I have 500 pesos for a gift, so on Sunday I go to the mall with my cousin.","First, we go to a bookstore. Ximena loves fantasy novels, but she has all the books I see!","Then we go to a clothes store. There is a beautiful jacket, but it costs 900 pesos. It’s too expensive.","We are tired and hungry, so we sit down and eat some pretzels. Then my cousin has an idea. “Ximena loves photos, right? Why don’t you make her a photo album?”","It’s a great idea! We go to a stationery store. I buy a nice album, stickers and colored pens. It costs 320 pesos.","At home, I print our best photos from the last three years. I write a short message under each one. I think it’s the best gift for her."],"q":[["How much money does the narrator have for the gift?",["500 pesos","900 pesos","320 pesos","1,000 pesos"]],["Why doesn’t she buy a book?",["Ximena already has the books.","The books are expensive.","The bookstore is closed.","She doesn’t like reading."]],["Why doesn’t she buy the jacket?",["It’s too expensive.","It’s ugly.","It’s too small.","It’s the wrong color."]],["Whose idea is the photo album?",["her cousin’s","the narrator’s","Ximena’s","the shop assistant’s"]],["What does the narrator write in the album?",["a short message under each photo","a long letter","Ximena’s name only","the date of the party"]],["Why is the photo album a good gift for Ximena?",["She loves photos, and it is personal.","It is very expensive.","She asked for it.","It is a fantasy novel."]]]}],"teens|A2":[{"t":"My First Summer Job","p":["Last summer, I got my first job. I was sixteen, and I wanted to save money for a new laptop. My aunt’s friend owns an ice cream shop in San Pedro, and she needed help on weekends.","The first day was harder than I expected. I had to learn the names of thirty flavors, use the cash register and smile at every customer, even the rude ones. At the end of the day, my arm hurt from scooping ice cream.","Little by little, I got better. I learned to work fast when there was a long line, and I learned that being polite really makes a difference. Some customers came back every week and asked for me by name.","My favorite part was the kids. They always took a long time to choose, and they always chose chocolate in the end.","By the end of August, I had saved enough for my laptop. But I also learned something important: every job deserves respect. Now I always say thank you to people who serve me."],"q":[["Why did the narrator want a job?",["to save money for a laptop","because he was bored","to help his aunt","to buy a new phone"]],["What was difficult on the first day?",["learning flavors and using the cash register","opening the shop","making ice cream","cleaning the tables"]],["Why did his arm hurt?",["from scooping ice cream","from carrying boxes","from playing sports","from writing on the computer"]],["What did the kids always choose?",["chocolate","vanilla","strawberry","mango"]],["What important lesson did the narrator learn?",["Every job deserves respect.","Work is always easy.","Customers are always rude.","Ice cream is the best job."]],["Why did some customers ask for the narrator by name?",["The narrator was polite and friendly with them.","The narrator was the owner’s nephew.","The narrator gave free ice cream.","The narrator was famous."]],["What does “rude” mean in “even the rude ones”?",["not polite","very happy","in a hurry","very young"]]]},{"t":"A Weekend in Mexico City","p":["In March, my class went on a three-day trip to Mexico City. It was my first time in the capital, and I was amazed by how big it is. More than twenty million people live in the metropolitan area!","On the first day, we visited the Zócalo, the huge main square, and the Templo Mayor, an ancient Aztec temple that was discovered in 1978 by electricity workers. It’s incredible that it was hidden under the city for centuries.","On the second day, we went to the National Museum of Anthropology. My favorite object was the Sun Stone, a giant stone calendar. In the afternoon, we took a boat called a trajinera in Xochimilco. There were mariachis on other boats, and we ate elotes on the water.","On the last day, we visited the Frida Kahlo Museum, also called the Blue House. I didn’t know much about her before, but now I’m a fan.","The trip was tiring, but I want to go back with my family soon."],"q":[["How long was the trip?",["three days","one week","one day","two weeks"]],["Who discovered the Templo Mayor?",["electricity workers","archaeologists","students","tourists"]],["What was the narrator’s favorite object in the museum?",["the Sun Stone","a painting by Frida","a mask","a gold necklace"]],["What is a trajinera?",["a boat in Xochimilco","a type of food","a museum","a kind of dance"]],["What is the Frida Kahlo Museum also called?",["the Blue House","the Red House","the Sun House","the Green House"]],["How did the narrator’s opinion of Frida Kahlo change?",["The narrator didn’t know much about her and became a fan.","The narrator didn’t like her paintings.","The narrator already knew everything about her.","The narrator thought the museum was boring."]],["What does “tiring” mean in “The trip was tiring”?",["it made you feel tired","it was very short","it was boring","it was expensive"]]]},{"t":"The Food Truck Idea","p":["In our entrepreneurship class, we had to create a small business and run it for one month. My friends Bruno, Alicia and I decided to sell food at the school’s Friday market.","We didn’t have a real truck, of course. We decorated a table to look like a food truck and called it “The Hungry Owl.” Our product was simple: mini hot dogs with three different sauces.","Before we started, we calculated our costs. Each hot dog cost us about eight pesos to make, so we decided to sell them for fifteen. Alicia designed a menu, Bruno was in charge of cooking and I managed the money.","The first Friday was terrible. We made too many hot dogs and only sold twenty. The second week, we made fewer and offered a combo with a drink. We sold everything in forty minutes!","At the end of the month, we had a profit of 1,350 pesos. We donated half to a local food bank and used the other half for a pizza party."],"q":[["Why did the friends start a business?",["It was a class project.","They needed money.","Their parents asked them.","They wanted to win a prize."]],["What was the name of their business?",["The Hungry Owl","The Food Truck","Friday Dogs","The Happy Dog"]],["What was the narrator’s job?",["managing the money","cooking","designing the menu","selling the hot dogs"]],["What changed in the second week?",["They made fewer hot dogs and offered a combo.","They raised the price.","They sold pizza.","They opened earlier."]],["What did they do with the profit?",["They donated half and had a pizza party.","They bought a real truck.","They gave it to the school.","They saved it all for next year."]],["What mistake did they make on the first Friday?",["They made too many hot dogs.","They sold them too cheap.","They forgot the sauces.","They arrived late."]],["What does “profit” mean in “we had a profit of 1,350 pesos”?",["money you keep after paying the costs","money you borrow","money you spend","the price of one hot dog"]]]},{"t":"The Photography Club","p":["When I joined the photography club at school, I thought it would be easy. I had a good phone camera and I took photos every day. But our teacher, Ms. Treviño, gave us a strange first assignment: “Take fifty photos of your street. No people, no selfies.”","I thought my street was boring. There are just houses, a small store and some trees. But I went out with my phone and started looking carefully.","I noticed things I had never seen before: the shadows of the leaves on a yellow wall, a cat sleeping in a flowerpot, the old painted sign of the store and the way the light changes at six in the evening.","In the next class, Ms. Treviño showed some of our photos on the screen. When she showed my picture of the cat, everyone said, “Wow!”","She explained that good photography is not about expensive cameras. It’s about paying attention.","Now, when I walk anywhere, I look at the world differently."],"q":[["What was the first assignment?",["Take fifty photos of your street.","Take fifty selfies.","Photograph your family.","Photograph your best friends."]],["What did the narrator think about the street at first?",["It was boring.","It was beautiful.","It was dangerous.","It was noisy."]],["Which photo impressed everyone?",["the cat sleeping in a flowerpot","the yellow wall","the store sign","the sunset over the houses"]],["According to Ms. Treviño, what is good photography about?",["paying attention","having an expensive camera","taking many selfies","using many filters"]],["How has the narrator changed?",["She looks at the world differently.","She stopped taking photos.","She bought a new camera.","She takes only selfies now."]],["Why did Ms. Treviño say “No people, no selfies”?",["She wanted students to notice everyday things around them.","People don’t like photos.","Selfies are not allowed at school.","She wanted fewer photos."]],["What does “paying attention” mean in the text?",["looking and listening carefully","spending money","taking a long time","being quiet"]]]},{"t":"Twenty-Four Hours Without My Phone","p":["On Tuesday afternoon, I left my phone on the bus. When I realized it, the bus was already gone. My mom called the bus company, and they said someone could bring it to their office the next day.","That evening, I felt strange. I kept putting my hand in my pocket. I didn’t know what time it was, and I couldn’t check messages. I felt disconnected from everything.","So I did things I usually don’t do. I helped my dad fix a chair. I played chess with my little sister, and she beat me twice. I went to bed early and actually slept well.","The next morning at school, I talked more with my classmates during the break, because I wasn’t looking at a screen. I even talked to a guy from my class I had never spoken to before. He likes the same video games as me!","In the afternoon, I got my phone back. I had forty-three notifications, but honestly, none of them were important.","I’m not saying phones are bad. But that day showed me how much I was missing."],"q":[["Where did the narrator lose the phone?",["on the bus","at school","at the park","in a taxi"]],["How did the narrator feel that evening?",["disconnected","relaxed","excited","angry"]],["What happened when the narrator played chess?",["The little sister won twice.","The narrator won every game.","The dad helped the narrator.","He let her win."]],["What happened at school the next day?",["The narrator talked to a new classmate.","The narrator was late.","The narrator got the phone back.","He went home early."]],["What did the narrator think about the notifications?",["None of them were important.","They were all urgent.","He couldn’t read them.","He answered all of them immediately."]],["What is the writer’s main point at the end?",["Without the phone, the writer noticed many things he was missing.","Phones are very bad and should be banned.","Losing a phone is the worst thing that can happen.","Buses are not safe for phones."]],["What does “disconnected” mean in “I felt disconnected from everything”?",["not in contact with other people or information","very tired","angry","excited"]]]}],"teens|B1":[{"t":"Why Are Teenagers Always Tired?","p":["Many parents complain that their teenage children stay up too late and can’t get out of bed in the morning. They often think it’s a question of discipline. However, science suggests that biology plays a major role.","Our sleep is controlled partly by a hormone called melatonin, which tells the body that it’s time to sleep. In children and adults, the body usually starts producing melatonin around nine or ten at night. During adolescence, this process shifts by up to two hours. As a result, many teenagers genuinely don’t feel sleepy until eleven or even midnight.","The problem is that school still starts early. A teenager who falls asleep at midnight and wakes up at six gets only six hours of sleep, while experts recommend between eight and ten hours for this age group.","Chronic lack of sleep can affect concentration, memory, mood and even the immune system. Some studies have also linked it to a higher risk of accidents.","Because of this research, some schools in other countries have moved their start times later, and many of them reported better attendance and grades.","Teenagers can help themselves, too: getting sunlight in the morning, avoiding bright screens late at night and keeping a regular schedule, even on weekends, can make a real difference."],"q":[["What do many parents think causes teenagers’ sleep problems?",["a lack of discipline","too much homework","bad food","playing video games"]],["What happens to melatonin during adolescence?",["Its production shifts later by up to two hours.","The body stops producing it.","It is produced earlier.","It doubles at night."]],["How many hours of sleep do experts recommend for teenagers?",["between eight and ten","about six","more than twelve","about twelve"]],["What did some schools report after starting later?",["better attendance and grades","more accidents","no change","more homework"]],["Which tip is mentioned in the text?",["getting sunlight in the morning","sleeping late on weekends","drinking coffee at night","drinking energy drinks"]],["What is the writer’s main point about teenagers’ sleep?",["It is mostly a biological issue, not just a lack of discipline.","Teenagers are lazy.","Parents should wake teens up earlier.","Schools should start at six."]],["What does “chronic” mean in “Chronic lack of sleep”?",["lasting a long time","very sudden","healthy","rare"]]]},{"t":"Why Do We Procrastinate?","p":["You have a project due on Friday. It’s Monday, and you have plenty of time. But instead of starting, you watch a few videos, tidy your desk, check your messages… and suddenly it’s Thursday night.","This is procrastination, and almost everyone does it. For a long time, people believed it was simply a problem of laziness or bad time management. Today, many psychologists see it differently. They describe procrastination as a problem of emotion regulation.","In other words, we don’t avoid tasks because we are lazy. We avoid them because they make us feel something unpleasant: boredom, confusion, fear of failure or the pressure to be perfect. Putting the task off gives us immediate relief. The problem is that the relief is temporary, and the stress grows later.","So what helps? One strategy is to make the first step ridiculously small. Instead of “write my essay,” the task becomes “write the title.” Once we start, it’s usually easier to continue.","Another strategy is to be kind to yourself. Research has found that students who forgave themselves for procrastinating on one exam procrastinated less on the next one.","Finally, remove distractions. Putting your phone in another room for twenty-five minutes can be surprisingly effective."],"q":[["How do many psychologists describe procrastination today?",["as a problem of emotion regulation","as simple laziness","as a medical illness","as a problem of intelligence"]],["Why do we avoid tasks, according to the text?",["They make us feel something unpleasant.","We don’t have time.","They are always too easy.","We forget about them."]],["What is the problem with the relief of putting a task off?",["It is temporary, and stress grows later.","It never comes.","It makes us sleepy.","It makes us work faster."]],["What does “make the first step ridiculously small” mean?",["Start with a tiny, easy action.","Do the hardest part first.","Skip the first step.","Ask a friend to do it."]],["What did research find about students who forgave themselves?",["They procrastinated less next time.","They got worse grades.","They stopped studying.","They needed more help from teachers."]],["Why does the writer start with the example of the Friday project?",["to show a situation most readers know","to explain how to write a project","to show that Thursday is the best day","to criticize teachers"]],["What does “relief” mean in “immediate relief”?",["a good feeling when stress goes away","a strong pain","a big problem","a prize"]]]},{"t":"Frida Kahlo: Painting Her Own Reality","p":["Frida Kahlo is one of the most recognized artists in the world, but she never planned to become a painter. As a teenager in Mexico City, she wanted to study medicine.","In 1925, when she was eighteen, the bus she was traveling on collided with a streetcar. Frida suffered terrible injuries to her spine, pelvis and leg. She spent months in bed, wearing a full-body cast. To help her pass the time, her mother had a special easel made so she could paint lying down, and her father lent her his paints. A mirror was placed above her bed.","Looking at her own face every day, Frida began painting self-portraits. Over her life, she created around 150 paintings, and more than a third of them are self-portraits. “I paint myself because I am so often alone,” she once said.","Her work is famous for its bright colors, Mexican traditions and honest representation of pain, identity and the female body. She often wore traditional Tehuana dresses, proudly expressing her Mexican identity.","During her life, Frida was often seen mainly as the wife of the muralist Diego Rivera. Today, her reputation is enormous. Her house in Coyoacán, the Blue House, is one of the most visited museums in Mexico."],"q":[["What did Frida originally want to study?",["medicine","art","history","law"]],["How old was she when she had the accident?",["eighteen","twelve","thirty","twenty-five"]],["Why was a mirror placed above her bed?",["so she could paint herself","to make the room brighter","so she could see the street","to watch her visitors"]],["About what fraction of her paintings are self-portraits?",["more than a third","about a tenth","all of them","about half"]],["How was Frida often seen during her life?",["mainly as Diego Rivera’s wife","as the most famous painter in the world","as a doctor","as a famous doctor"]],["Why did Frida say “I paint myself because I am so often alone”?",["Her injuries kept her alone in bed for long periods.","She didn’t have any friends.","She didn’t like other people.","Diego Rivera told her to."]],["What does “reputation” mean in “her reputation is enormous”?",["what people think and know about someone","the size of a painting","a kind of museum","a group of artists"]]]},{"t":"How Music Changes Your Brain","p":["Have you ever felt a shiver down your spine when listening to a song? About half of people experience these musical “chills.” Scientists have discovered that they are connected to dopamine, a chemical that the brain releases when we experience something pleasurable, like eating delicious food.","Interestingly, studies using brain scans have shown that dopamine is released not only at the most emotional moment of a song, but also in the seconds before it, while we are anticipating it. In other words, part of the pleasure of music comes from prediction.","Music can also affect our bodies. Fast music tends to increase heart rate, while slow music can help people relax. That’s why many athletes use energetic playlists before competitions, and why some hospitals play calm music to reduce patients’ anxiety.","What about studying with music? The answer depends on the person and the task. Instrumental music may help some people concentrate, but songs with lyrics can interfere with reading or writing, because the brain tries to process both sets of words at once.","Finally, learning to play an instrument seems to be good for the brain. It involves memory, coordination, hearing and emotion all at the same time. So if you’ve been thinking about learning the guitar, there’s a scientific reason to start."],"q":[["What are musical “chills” connected to?",["dopamine","melatonin","adrenaline","serotonin"]],["When is dopamine also released?",["while we anticipate an emotional moment","only after the song ends","only during sad songs","only when the song is very fast"]],["Why do hospitals sometimes play calm music?",["to reduce patients’ anxiety","to entertain doctors","to cover noise","to keep patients awake"]],["Why can songs with lyrics interfere with studying?",["The brain processes both sets of words at once.","They are too loud.","They make people sleepy.","They make the brain sleep."]],["What does playing an instrument involve, according to the text?",["memory, coordination, hearing and emotion","only the hands","only memory","only hearing"]],["What does the writer suggest about the pleasure of music?",["Part of it comes from expecting what will happen next.","It only comes from loud songs.","It is the same for everyone.","It disappears after a song ends."]],["What does “anticipating” mean in “while we are anticipating it”?",["expecting something to happen","forgetting something","stopping something","repeating something"]]]},{"t":"The Real Story of Halloween","p":["Every October 31, millions of people put on costumes, carve pumpkins and fill bags with candy. But Halloween didn’t start as a party. Its roots go back more than 2,000 years, to an ancient Celtic festival called Samhain.","The Celts lived in what is now Ireland, Scotland and parts of France. For them, Samhain marked the end of the harvest and the beginning of the dark, cold half of the year. They believed that on that night the boundary between the world of the living and the world of the dead became thin, so they lit huge bonfires and wore animal skins to confuse wandering spirits.","Centuries later, the Church established All Saints’ Day on November 1. The evening before was called All Hallows’ Eve, a name that eventually became “Halloween.”","The famous jack-o’-lantern also has an interesting history. In Ireland, people originally carved faces into turnips and potatoes to scare away evil spirits. When Irish immigrants arrived in the United States in the 1800s, they discovered that pumpkins were bigger and much easier to carve.","Trick-or-treating became popular in the United States in the twentieth century, and from there it spread around the world through movies and television. Today, Halloween is a mix of ancient beliefs, religious traditions and modern pop culture — which may be exactly why so many people enjoy it."],"q":[["What was the name of the ancient Celtic festival?",["Samhain","All Saints’ Day","Hallows","Día de Muertos"]],["What did Samhain mark?",["the end of the harvest and the start of the dark half of the year","the first day of spring","the birthday of a Celtic king","the longest day of the year"]],["Where does the word “Halloween” come from?",["All Hallows’ Eve","a Celtic god","an American movie","a Scottish word for pumpkin"]],["What did people in Ireland originally carve?",["turnips and potatoes","pumpkins","apples","pumpkins and apples"]],["How did trick-or-treating spread around the world?",["through movies and television","through Celtic songs","through school books","through Celtic festivals in America"]],["Why did the Celts wear animal skins on Samhain?",["to confuse the spirits wandering that night","to keep warm at the bonfires","to win a costume contest","to hunt animals"]],["What does “spread” mean in “it spread around the world”?",["became known in many places","became smaller","disappeared","was forgotten"]]]}]};
